; ============================================================================
; compiler.cdz — the Cadenza compiler, authored IN Cadenza.
;
; Written the way it SHOULD be written — clean, idiomatic Cadenza — NOT contorted
; to dodge the current seed's limits. Where the seed cannot compile this, that is
; a GAP in the seed to be fixed (see SEED-GAPS-FOR-SELF-HOSTING.md), which is the
; whole point of the spike: the flagship program tells us what to build.
;
; It lowers a program through a RESOLVED IR ladder so emission is a pure
; serializer — the shape fixed by spec/capabilities/compiler-pipeline.md
; §Representation and informed by the optimizing-compiler research learning:
;
;   Core   — the resolved, analyzed middle IR (direct-style; grows ANF + join
;            points). Names already resolved to codes; every pass is a Core→Core
;            or Core→Lir transformation, NEVER an effect of emitting bytes.
;   Lir    — the TYPED instruction sum (`Instr`): the backend rung the spec
;            mandates. `serialize` pattern-matches it EXHAUSTIVELY to bytes, so a
;            new opcode is a compile error until handled (reject-don't-miscompile).
;   Bytes  — the wasm-component byte stream. `serialize`/`frame` are SERIALIZERS:
;            they resolve no name, decide no type, choose no handler.
;
; The front end (bytes → AST → resolve names to codes) is authored separately and
; needs runtime String support (a seed gap being fixed in parallel); this file is
; the resolved-middle-and-backend half, authored against integer-coded heads —
; which is exactly "resolve names to codes before selecting instructions."
;
; Pipeline: resolve (Node→Core) → fold (Core→Core) → lower (Core→Lir) → serialize
; (Lir→bytes) → frame (bytes→component). Framing is TYPE-DIRECTED: `kind-of` infers
; the result Kind (i64 or Bool) and the frame presents `run` at the matching wasm
; valtype. Operators covered:
;   arithmetic  + - * / %        (i64 → i64)
;   bitwise     & |               (i64 → i64; total — i64.and/i64.or, const-folded and runtime)
;   comparison  < > <= >= = !=    (i64 → Bool)
;   logical     and or not        (Bool → Bool; and/or SHORT-CIRCUIT)
;   control     if/then/else      (surface `NIf` → KIf → structured if/else/end)
;   binding     let               (surface `NLet` → KLet → local.set/get; the function body
;                                  declares its extra i64 locals via count-lets/locals-decl)
;   functions   multi-def modules — params (local.get) + N-ARY calls (surface `NCall`
;               (fn-idx, list Node) → KCall (fn-idx, list Core) → args pushed L-to-R
;               then `call idx`); N-entry type/function/code sections. The def NAMED
;               `main` becomes func 0 (the nullary `run` entry) via a VISITING-ORDER
;               remap (`visit-def`/`find-main`), so a helper-first module `(def (add
;               …)…)(def (main)…)` computes its value (not a trap). A Bool call-argument
;               is widened `i64.extend_i32_u` to the callee's i64 param (params are i64).
;               A function's RESULT KIND is type-directed: `build-ktab` computes a
;               per-function return-kind table as a MONOTONE FIXPOINT (`ktab-iterate`
;               over the FList, `kind-of` of each body under the current table, iterated
;               to convergence), and `kind-of`'s KCall arm reads it — so a Bool-returning
;               helper AND a transitive Bool chain (a helper that only calls a Bool
;               helper) frame as i32, not i64. VERIFIED byte-identical to the seed for
;               `(def (main) (lt 3 5)) (def (lt a b) (< a b))` AND the depth-2 chain
;               `(def (main) (isLt 3 5)) (def (isLt a b) (lt a b)) (def (lt a b) (< a b))`
;               (all funcs → i32). (Was single-pass while seed gap 3k's fixpoint variant
;               OOM'd; 3k now fixed, so the true fixpoint is expressible.)
; NOT YET: shifts `<< >>`. The seed lowers them with a COUNT-RANGE TRAP guard
; (`count >=u 64 → unreachable`) + a left-shift OVERFLOW guard, using SCRATCH LOCALS
; — wasm masks the count mod 64, so an unguarded `i64.shl`/`i64.shr_s` would
; MISCOMPILE (a shift-by-64 would silently become shift-by-0). This compiler's Lir
; is a pure Core→Code fold with no scratch-local allocation, so faithful shift
; lowering is an architectural step (a local-allocating lower pass), not a one-line
; binop. Until then `<< >>` read to an unknown head → KError → `unreachable`
; (a VALID trapping component): decline, don't miscompile. `& |` ARE total single
; opcodes (no guard), so they landed fully. `^` (xor) is not a seed operator.
;
; FRONT END CLOSED: a whole multi-def surface module (a `DList` of `Def`s — head
; NAMES + operand forms, what the reader produces) resolves end-to-end to component
; bytes via `compile-module`: resolve-module (DList→FList, name→code) → per-function
; fold → lower → serialize → frame.
;
; THE READER (input bytes → DList) is the last piece before self-hosting; its
; foundation is built: the CBOR reader primitives decode + NAVIGATE the canonical
; AST from runtime input bytes —
;   head decode:  `cbor-major` / `cbor-info` / `cbor-arg` / `be-bytes` / `cbor-head-len`
;   navigation:   `cbor-skip` / `skip-elems`  (advance past an item / k items)
; all verified on `(quote 42)` = `83 01 80 18 2A` (skip(0)=5 walks the whole array).
; INTEGRATION-VERIFIED (2026-07-07): every surface form composes in one realistic
; program. A `classify x = (if (and (> x 0) (< x 10)) (let y = (* x x) in (- y 1)) 0)`
; called by `main` resolves+folds+lowers+frames to a valid component whose `classify`
; emits a SHORT-CIRCUIT `and` (nested `if (result i32)`), the outer `if (result i64)`,
; the `let` (declared `(local i64)` + local.set/get), and the arithmetic — all from
; surface NAMES. So the pipeline composes correctly across the full surface language.
;
; Name resolution too: `prelude-entry` / `name-eq` locate the Nth prelude symbol
; and byte-compare it to a known operator name (`b"+"`, …) — the read-time
; "resolve names to codes" step, on raw bytes (no runtime String needed).
;   ⚠ `name-eq` is written in its natural `(if (= a b) (recurse) false)` shape but
;   currently DECLINES when reached ("if condition is not Bool" — Tier 2d: a
;   recursive Bool-returning fn with the self-call in an inner `if`'s THEN branch
;   and `false` in ELSE is mis-inferred as non-Bool). It's dead code until the reader
;   calls it, so the compiler still builds; it comes alive when Tier 2d is fixed.
; 🎯 CONNECTED END-TO-END: `bytes → component`. `read-node : Bytes → Node` decodes
; the canonical CBOR AST (head decode + navigation + prelude byte-match), `resolve`
; turns it into Core, and the pipeline compiles it. Verified: the CBOR of `(+ 1 2)`
; → valid 92-byte component (`i64.const 1; i64.const 2; i64.add`). The compiler reads
; a program's own canonical AST and compiles it. (What had blocked this — a "cannot
; box" decline — was NOT a seed gap but my own `PUnknown` placeholder that built an
; out-of-range `Bytes.of` as a trap; replaced by an honest `KError`→`unreachable`
; node, which fixed it. Writing it honestly instead of hacking a trap was the fix.)
;
; 🎯 MODULE bytes → component (the full self-hosting shape) — DONE and wired as `main`.
; `read-module : Bytes → DList` decodes a whole `(module <name> (def …) …)` from its
; canonical CBOR (root `[module-idx, name, def…]`, each def `[def-idx, sig, body]`),
; and `compile-bytes` runs read-module → resolve-module → fold → lower → serialize →
; frame. Verified: the CBOR of `(module m (def (main) 42))` → valid 89-byte component
; (`i64.const 42`). `cbor-skip` handles CBOR tags (major 6 — the `39` bare-name marker
; a module's name uses). The reader decodes every AST-node shape the current surface
; uses: int (uint/negint), bool, unary/binary/ternary apps (not/ops/if), `let` bindings,
; and PARAMETER REFERENCES — a body name-ref (tag `d8 27 <idx>`) resolves to `NLocal
; <slot>` via the def's signature-derived param environment (verified: `(def (dbl x)
; (* x 2))` reads `x` → `local.get 0`).
;   ✅ LET (2026-07-07): `read-let` decodes surface `(let ((x v)…) body)` — bindings
;   `[apply-idx, [name-idx, value]…]` — into nested `NLet` nodes. Each binding takes the
;   next free local slot (env length, past params + enclosing lets); its value reads
;   under the env AS IT STANDS (`let*` — a later binding sees an earlier one); the env
;   then extends with the bound name so the body/rest resolves it. `ienv-pos` returns the
;   LAST matching slot, giving lexical shadowing. Verified from CBOR: single binding →
;   `(local i64); const; local.set 0; local.get 0; local.get 0; add`; multi-binding
;   `(let ((x 5)(y (+ x 1))) (+ x y))` → y reads x's slot (11); nested shadowing → inner
;   wins; all valid components. (`resolve`/`fold`/`lower`/`count-lets`/`kind-of` already
;   handled `NLet`→`KLet`; only the READER couldn't decode the surface — now it does.)
;   ✅ N-ARY CALLS (2026-07-07): `read-call` decodes a call to a user-defined function
;   of ANY arity. `read-module` builds the module's FUNCTION ENV (`mod-fenv` — every
;   def's fn-name prelude index, in def order) ONCE and threads it through every body;
;   `read-app` looks up an application head in `fenv` (after `let`/`if`/`not`) and, if
;   found, reads `(f arg…)` as `NCall (fn-index, list Node)` via `read-call-args` (a
;   recursive push-loop over the operands — the shape seed Gap A had to fix). The
;   fn-index is the head's position in `fenv`, the same positional index
;   `compile-program` frames. `resolve-args`/`fold-args`/`lower-args`/`count-lets-args`
;   map/fold over the arg list; `lower-args` pushes each arg's code LEFT-TO-RIGHT then
;   the KCall arm emits `call` (wasm calling convention). Verified from CBOR, EXECUTED
;   via wasmtime: `(dbl 21)`→42; 2-arg `(add 20 22)` w/ `(add a b)=(- a b)`→−2 (main =
;   `i64.const 20; i64.const 22; call 1`); 3-arg→0; NULLARY `(k)`→99; NESTED-call args
;   `(add (dbl 5) (dbl 6))`→−2; recursion `(dec n)=(if (< n 1) 7 (dec (- n 1)))`→7
;   (fenv resolves forward refs + self-reference).
;   ✅ FIXED (compiler-side): a module whose func 0 (the `run` entry) takes parameters
;   is now caught by `entry-guard` — func 0 becomes a nullary `KError` (→ `unreachable`),
;   a VALID nullary trapping `run`, not an invalid component. Decline-don't-miscompile
;   at the module boundary (verified: a param'd-entry module → `00 0B` run, valid).
; What's left for TRUE self-hosting: grow the accepted surface (recursion, match on
; user sums, String/Bytes ops) until the compiler's own source is within the subset.
; The connectives desugar at `resolve` to a conditional (short-circuit): `(and a b)`
; = `(if a b false)`, `(or a b)` = `(if a true b)`, so the right operand is shielded
; exactly as an unselected branch is (core-semantics.md §Boolean Connectives).
; The fold pass is SEMANTICS-PRESERVING: it folds only where observable behavior
; cannot change — `(/ x 0)` / `(% x 0)` (and INT64_MIN / -1) are NEVER folded (the
; trap is preserved for run time, matching the oracle), and folding an `if` with a
; constant condition drops the untaken branch (short-circuit, so its trap/effect
; never occurs). This is the "each Core→Core rewrite preserves source semantics"
; obligation from the optimizing-compiler research, made concrete.
; ============================================================================

(module compiler

; ─── Diagnostics effect ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
; The compiler reports rejections (type errors, malformed forms) by PERFORMING a
; `Diag.emit <code>` at each rejection point in the recursive check, rather than
; folding the body to a trapping `KError`. The handler installed at `compile`
; accumulates the codes into a `list` (state), and `Diag.collect` reads them back
; out (a getter op — the state is observable only through an operation, there is no
; return clause). This is the operator's "diagnostics via effects" direction: the
; recursive-effectful runtime-compound path the seed now emits (ask-45). The codes
; are all CDZ0201 in this compiler's coarse i64/Bool surface (type mismatch / bad
; branch / non-Bool condition / malformed form); float-promotion (CDZ0301) and
; non-exhaustive-match (CDZ0210) are out of scope. NOTE: until the compile RETURN
; channel carries diagnostics (ask-41 artifact record, or ask-42 result<>), the
; collected list is computed but not yet surfaced to the host; the emitted bytes
; still come from the KError-stub lowering, so behavior is byte-unchanged for now.
(effect Diag
  (op emit (-> Int64 Unit))          ; record a diagnostic code at a rejection point
  (op collect (-> Unit (list Int64))))  ; read the accumulated codes (getter)

; ─── Byte-stream primitives ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

(def (u8 b)
  (doc "A one-byte Bytes value; `b` must be in 0..=255.")
  (Bytes.of (list b)))

(def (cat a b)
  (doc "Concatenate two byte streams.")
  (Bytes.concat a b))

(def (empty-bytes)
  (doc "The zero-length byte stream.")
  (Bytes.of (list)))

(def (uleb n)
  (doc "Unsigned LEB128 of a non-negative Int64 — section/vector lengths, indices, u32 operands.")
  (if (< n 128)
      (u8 (Int.to-byte n))
      (cat (u8 (Int.to-byte (| (& n 127) 128))) (uleb (>> n 7)))))

(def (sleb n)
  (doc "Signed LEB128 of an Int64 — the operand of i64.const.")
  (if (sleb-done n)
      (u8 (Int.to-byte (& n 127)))
      (cat (u8 (Int.to-byte (| (& n 127) 128))) (sleb (>> n 7)))))

(def (sleb-done n)
  (doc "A signed value is complete when the shifted remainder is all sign bits and the current
        group's sign bit agrees: (>>7)=0 with bit6 clear, or (>>7)=-1 with bit6 set. Written as
        nested `if` because the seed has no boolean connectives yet — see Tier 3a-bis in
        SEED-GAPS-FOR-SELF-HOSTING.md. Verified byte-correct against known LEB128 answers.")
  (if (= (>> n 7) 0)
      (if (= (& n 64) 0) true false)
      (if (= (>> n 7) -1) (if (= (& n 64) 64) true false) false)))

; ─── value renderer: an Int64 → its decimal ASCII bytes ─────────────────────
; The first piece of runtime-compound VALUE emission (ask-60): a value's `display`
; string is built from rendering its parts. This renders an Int64 to decimal ASCII
; (the leaf every scalar display needs). Digits are emitted most-significant-first by
; recursing on n/10 before appending n%10. Negative → a leading '-' then the digits of
; the magnitude (guarding INT64_MIN, whose magnitude overflows, by rendering its last
; digit separately). ASCII '0' = 48, '-' = 45. Pure compile-time bytes (the compiler
; runs in Cadenza; `/` `%` are exact here). Independently testable via `Bytes.len`.
(def (digit-char d)
  (doc "The ASCII byte for decimal digit d (0..9): '0'(48) + d.")
  (u8 (Int.to-byte (+ 48 d))))

(def (int-to-decimal n)
  (doc "The decimal ASCII bytes of Int64 `n` (e.g. -12 → \"-12\"). 0 → \"0\"; negatives get a leading '-'
        over the magnitude's digits. INT64_MIN is handled by `neg-magnitude-digits` (its positive magnitude
        is unrepresentable, so the low digit is taken from `n` directly before the sign flips).")
  (if (= n 0)
      (digit-char 0)
  (if (< n 0)
      (cat (u8 (Int.to-byte 45)) (neg-magnitude-digits n))
      (nat-digits n))))

(def (nat-digits n)
  (doc "Decimal digits of a POSITIVE Int64 `n`, most-significant first: recurse on n/10, then this digit
        n%10. n>0 guaranteed by callers (0 handled in int-to-decimal).")
  (if (< n 10)
      (digit-char n)
      (cat (nat-digits (/ n 10)) (digit-char (% n 10)))))

(def (neg-magnitude-digits n)
  (doc "Decimal digits of the MAGNITUDE of a NEGATIVE Int64 `n`, most-significant first — computed WITHOUT
        negating `n` (which overflows for INT64_MIN). Works on the negative value directly: n/10 stays
        negative (or 0), and the digit is `-(n%10)` (n%10 is ≤0 for n<0, so its negation is the 0..9 digit).")
  (if (> n -10)
      (digit-char (- 0 n))                              ; single digit: -(n) is 1..9
      (cat (neg-magnitude-digits (/ n 10)) (digit-char (- 0 (% n 10))))))

; ─── CBOR reader primitives (input side) ────────────────────────────────────
; The compiler's INPUT is the canonical binary AST: deterministic CBOR
; `[version, prelude, root]` where an atom is a CBOR scalar and an application is a
; CBOR array `[head-index, ...children]` (ast-encoding.md / binary-sexpr.md). These
; primitives decode a CBOR item's head — its major type (top 3 bits of the initial
; byte) and argument (low 5 bits, or a following 1/2/4/8-byte big-endian varint).
; This is the input dual of the `uleb`/`section` output primitives above; runtime
; `Bytes.at` + Option-match (both now realized) make it expressible. Verified on
; the real bytes of `(quote 42)` = `83 01 80 18 2A`.

(def (byte-at b i)
  (doc "The byte at offset i of a runtime Bytes, or 0 past the end (callers stay in bounds via len).")
  (match (Bytes.at b i) ((Some x) x) (None 0)))

(def (cbor-major b i)
  (doc "The CBOR major type of the item whose head is at offset i: the top 3 bits of the initial
        byte. 0=uint 1=negint 2=bytes 3=text 4=array 5=map 7=simple/float.")
  (>> (byte-at b i) 5))

(def (cbor-info b i)
  (doc "The CBOR additional-info of the head at offset i: the low 5 bits of the initial byte.
        <24 = the argument itself; 24/25/26/27 = a 1/2/4/8-byte big-endian argument follows.")
  (& (byte-at b i) 31))

(def (cbor-arg b i)
  (doc "The argument value of the CBOR head at offset i: the info directly when <24, else the
        following big-endian bytes (1/2/4/8 for info 24/25/26/27) assembled into an integer.")
  (cbor-arg-info b i (cbor-info b i)))

(def (cbor-arg-info b i info)
  (doc "The CBOR argument for a head at offset i with additional-info `info`, split out so `info`
        is named once. info<24 → info; 24 → 1 byte; 25 → 2 bytes; 26 → 4 bytes; 27 → 8 bytes,
        each big-endian starting at offset i+1.")
  (if (< info 24) info
  (if (= info 24) (byte-at b (+ i 1))
  (if (= info 25) (be-bytes b (+ i 1) 2)
  (if (= info 26) (be-bytes b (+ i 1) 4)
                  (be-bytes b (+ i 1) 8))))))

(def (be-bytes b i n)
  (doc "Assemble `n` big-endian bytes starting at offset i into an integer: byte[i] is most
        significant. `(be-bytes b i 2)` = byte[i]*256 + byte[i+1].")
  (if (< n 1) 0 (+ (* (byte-at b i) (pow256 (- n 1))) (be-bytes b (+ i 1) (- n 1)))))

(def (uint-in-i64-range b i)
  (doc "Is the CBOR uint/negint ARGUMENT at head-offset i representable as a NON-NEGATIVE magnitude below
        2^63? — the range gate for an Int64 literal, checked STRUCTURALLY so it never itself overflows.
        Only an 8-byte argument (info 27) can reach 2^63; a 1/2/4-byte argument (info ≤26) is at most
        2^32-1, always in range. For info 27 the magnitude is < 2^63 iff the MOST-SIGNIFICANT argument byte
        (at offset i+1) is < 0x80 — Int64.max is `7f ff…ff` (top byte 0x7f) and Int64.min encodes as negint
        arg `7f ff…ff` (top byte 0x7f), so `>= 0x80` is the first out-of-range magnitude (2^63) for BOTH
        majors. (The value itself is NOT computed here: `cbor-arg` on a 2^63+ argument would overflow the
        i64 accumulator; this test reads one byte instead.)")
  (if (< (cbor-info b i) 27)
      true
      (< (byte-at b (+ i 1)) 128)))

(def (pow256 k)
  (doc "256^k — the place value of a big-endian byte k positions from the least-significant end.")
  (if (< k 1) 1 (* 256 (pow256 (- k 1)))))

(def (cbor-head-len b i)
  (doc "How many bytes the CBOR head at offset i occupies (initial byte + any following argument
        bytes): 1 for info<24, else 1 + 1/2/4/8 for info 24/25/26/27. The item's payload (array
        elements, text bytes) follows immediately after.")
  (cbor-head-len-info (cbor-info b i)))

(def (cbor-head-len-info info)
  (doc "Head length from additional-info alone.")
  (if (< info 24) 1
  (if (= info 24) 2
  (if (= info 25) 3
  (if (= info 26) 5
                  9)))))

(def (cbor-skip b i)
  (doc "The offset just past the CBOR item whose head is at offset i — the navigation primitive the
        reader uses to reach the root (past version+prelude) and to walk array elements. An array
        (major 4) is its head then each of its `arg` elements skipped in turn; a text/byte string
        (major 3/2) is its head then `arg` payload bytes; a TAG (major 6, e.g. the `39` bare-name
        marker `d8 27 <idx>`) is its head then the ONE tagged item that follows (skipped
        recursively); any scalar (major 0/1/7) is just its head. Verified on `(quote 42)` =
        `83 01 80 18 2A` (skip(0)=5) and on the tag `d8 27 01` (skip=3, head 2 + tagged uint 1).")
  (if (= (cbor-major b i) 4)
      (skip-elems b (+ i (cbor-head-len b i)) (cbor-arg b i))
  (if (or (= (cbor-major b i) 3) (= (cbor-major b i) 2))
      (+ (+ i (cbor-head-len b i)) (cbor-arg b i))
  (if (= (cbor-major b i) 6)
      (cbor-skip b (+ i (cbor-head-len b i)))
      (+ i (cbor-head-len b i))))))

(def (skip-elems b i k)
  (doc "Skip `k` consecutive CBOR items starting at offset i, returning the offset past the last.
        The array-element walk `cbor-skip` uses.")
  (if (< k 1) i (skip-elems b (cbor-skip b i) (- k 1))))

; ─── Prelude & name resolution (the read-time `resolve` seam) ────────────────
; The canonical AST file is `[version, prelude, root]`. The prelude (CBOR array at
; offset 2) is the sorted list of the distinct symbol NAMES the tree references; an
; application node names its head by a prelude INDEX, not the symbol inline. To turn
; a head-index into an operator identity, the reader locates the Nth prelude entry
; (a CBOR text string) and byte-compares its payload against known operator names —
; runtime `Bytes`/byte equality, no `String` conversion needed. This is the
; "resolve names to codes" step at read time, done on raw bytes.

(def (payload-off b i)
  (doc "The offset of a CBOR item's payload (past its head): array elements / text bytes begin here.")
  (+ i (cbor-head-len b i)))

(def (prelude0 b)
  (doc "The offset of the FIRST prelude entry: the prelude array's head is at offset 2 (right after
        the file-array head at 0 and the 1-byte version at 1), so entry 0 begins past that head.")
  (payload-off b 2))

(def (prelude-entry b k)
  (doc "The offset of prelude entry `k` — skip `k` entries from the first. Head-index `k` in an
        application names prelude entry `k`.")
  (skip-elems b (prelude0 b) k))

(def (entry-len b e)
  (doc "The byte-length of the text symbol at prelude-entry offset e (its CBOR text `arg`).")
  (cbor-arg b e))

(def (entry-byte b e j)
  (doc "Byte j of the text symbol at prelude-entry offset e (0-based into its payload).")
  (byte-at b (+ (payload-off b e) j)))

(def (byte-is-digit c)
  (doc "Is byte `c` an ASCII digit '0'..'9' (48..57)?")
  (and (>= c 48) (<= c 57)))

(def (name-is-numeric b e)
  (doc "Does the prelude symbol at offset e LOOK like a numeric literal — a digit-led token (optionally
        after a single leading '+'/'-' sign)? Mirrors the seed's `looks_like_numeric_literal`: such a
        token reached the compiler as a NAME only because the AST encoder could not parse it as an i64,
        i.e. it is OUT OF the Int64 range → a malformed literal (CDZ0201), not an unbound name. A
        digit-led first char also covers the `0x`/`0b` radix prefixes (both begin with '0'). A genuine
        identifier never starts with a digit, so this cannot misfire on a real name. (Only the leading
        char is inspected — enough to discriminate; native emits the same code for any digit-led token.)")
  (if (< (entry-len b e) 1)
      false
      (if (or (= (entry-byte b e 0) 43) (= (entry-byte b e 0) 45))   ; a leading '+'/'-' sign
          (and (>= (entry-len b e) 2) (byte-is-digit (entry-byte b e 1)))
          (byte-is-digit (entry-byte b e 0)))))

(def (name-eq b e lit j n)
  (doc "Does the prelude symbol at offset e equal the literal name bytes `lit` (length n)? Compares
        length then each byte. `lit` is a known operator name as a Bytes literal (e.g. b\"+\"); j
        counts up from 0. Byte-oriented name match — no runtime String needed.")
  (if (= (entry-len b e) n)
      (name-eq-go b e lit j n)
      false))

(def (name-eq-go b e lit j n)
  (doc "Byte-by-byte compare of prelude entry e against literal `lit` from position j (lengths known
        equal = n). All n bytes matching ⇒ true.")
  (if (< j n)
      (if (= (entry-byte b e j) (byte-at lit j))
          (name-eq-go b e lit (+ j 1) n)
          false)
      true))

(def (name-has-prefix b e lit n)
  (doc "Does the prelude symbol at offset e START WITH the `n`-byte literal `lit`? (Length ≥ n and the
        first n bytes match — a PREFIX test, unlike `name-eq`'s exact-length match.) Used to recognize a
        `tuple.N` accessor head (prefix `tuple.`) whose index digits vary.")
  (if (< (entry-len b e) n)
      false
      (name-eq-go b e lit 0 n)))

; ─── wasm section / vector builders ─────────────────────────────────────────
; A section is <id> <uleb len(contents)> <contents>; a vector is <uleb count>
; <items>. These are the natural spellings — `contents` is named once and used
; for both its length and its bytes.

(def (section id contents)
  (doc "A wasm section: a one-byte id, the ULEB byte-length of the contents, then the contents.")
  (cat (u8 id) (cat (uleb (Bytes.len contents)) contents)))

(def (wvec count items)
  (doc "A wasm vector: a ULEB element count, then the concatenated element bytes.")
  (cat (uleb count) items))

(def (sized payload)
  (doc "Prefix a byte stream with its own ULEB length (the wasm 'sized' shape — code entries,
        the embedded core module).")
  (cat (uleb (Bytes.len payload)) payload))

; ─── Lir: the typed backend instruction sum ─────────────────────────────────
; A wasm instruction as a TYPED value, so `emit-instr` is EXHAUSTIVE. The
; i64.const operand rides in the IConst payload.

; A result Kind — the wasm valtype a value/block presents. A SUM (not an int tag)
; so every consumer matches it exhaustively. Declared here because `IIf` carries a
; block-result Kind; `kind-of` (the Core→Kind inference) and the valtype maps live
; near the framing that reads them.
(type Kind (Ki64 | KBool))

; The CONSERVATIVE three-valued kind the type-CHECK pass uses (distinct from the
; two-valued codegen `Kind`). `kind-of` DEFAULTS a parameter / call / KError to
; Ki64 so the framing can pick a valtype; the check must NOT read that default as
; a fact, or it false-rejects a Bool parameter used as an `if` condition (which
; native infers and COMPILES). `CKUnk` is "kind not provable here" — the check
; emits ONLY on a PROVABLE mismatch (both sides concrete + different), so an
; unknown never fires a false rejection. (ask-53 conservative-lattice fix.)
(type CKind (CKi64 | CKBool | CKFloat | CKCompound | CKUnk))

; `IIf` carries the block's result Kind (its wasm blocktype); a structured
; if/else/end brackets its then/else code in the flat stream. This is how control
; flow lives in a flat instruction list: `lower` emits the bracketing instrs
; around the already-lowered branch code, and `serialize` stays a flat fold.
(type Instr
  (IConst Int64        ; i64.const n
   | IBool Int64        ; i32.const n  (0/1 — a Bool leaf)
   | IAdd              ; i64.add
   | ISub              ; i64.sub
   | IMul              ; i64.mul
   | IDiv              ; i64.div_s  (traps on divide-by-zero and INT64_MIN/-1)
   | IMod              ; i64.rem_s  (traps on divide-by-zero)
   | IAnd              ; i64.and    (bitwise AND — total, no trap)
   | IOr               ; i64.or     (bitwise OR — total, no trap)
   | IShl              ; i64.shl    (left shift — count guarded before, overflow guarded after)
   | IShrS             ; i64.shr_s  (arithmetic right shift — count guarded before)
   | IGeU              ; i64.ge_u   (unsigned ≥ — the shift-count out-of-range test)
   | ILt               ; i64.lt_s   (two i64 → i32 Bool)
   | IGt               ; i64.gt_s
   | ILe               ; i64.le_s
   | IGe               ; i64.ge_s
   | IEq               ; i64.eq     (two i64 → i32 Bool)
   | INe               ; i64.ne
   | INot              ; i32.eqz    (Bool → Bool: logical negation)
   | IExtendU          ; i64.extend_i32_u  (widen a Bool i32 0/1 → i64 0/1 — for a Bool call arg to an i64 param)
   | IXor              ; i64.xor    (bitwise XOR — a real backend op; the overflow-check helpers use it)
   | IEqz64            ; i64.eqz    (i64 → i32 is-zero — used by a checked_mul-style helper)
   | ILocal Int64      ; local.get n (read parameter/local n)
   | ISet Int64        ; local.set n (store top of stack into local n)
   | ICall Int64       ; call n     (call function index n)
   | IIf Kind          ; if <blocktype = result Kind>
   | IIfVoid           ; if <blocktype = void 0x40> (a guard block producing no value)
   | IElse             ; else
   | IEnd              ; end
   | IReturn           ; return (early return)
   | IDrop             ; drop (pop and discard one stack value — a `do` block's non-final result)
   | IUnreachable      ; unreachable (a defined trap — the honest way to signal a rejected program)
   ))

; A cons-list of Instr — the code stream a body lowers to. A user cons-list (not
; the built-in `list`) so `serialize`/`lower` fold it with an ordinary `match`.
; Code = a built-in `list<Instr>` — the code stream a body lowers to (ask-62 step 3: the custom `Code`
; cons-sum-type retired for the built-in list). `serialize`/`lower` walk it by index; `code-cat`/`one`/
; `seq` build it with List.push. No custom type needed — the built-in list is the one sequence type.

; ─── serialize: Lir → bytes (the pure serializer) ───────────────────────────
; The exhaustive Instr→bytes map, then a fold over the code stream. This is the
; shape compiler-pipeline.md wants: a recursive function that pattern-matches the
; instruction sum, so an unhandled variant is a compile error, not a fall-through.

(def (emit-instr i)
  (doc "Serialize one Instr to its wasm opcode bytes — the exhaustive backend map.")
  (match i
    ((Instr.IConst n) (cat (u8 0x42) (sleb n)))   ; i64.const n
    ((Instr.IBool n)  (cat (u8 0x41) (sleb n)))   ; i32.const n (0/1)
    ((Instr.IAdd _)   (u8 0x7C))                  ; i64.add
    ((Instr.ISub _)   (u8 0x7D))                  ; i64.sub
    ((Instr.IMul _)   (u8 0x7E))                  ; i64.mul
    ((Instr.IDiv _)   (u8 0x7F))                  ; i64.div_s
    ((Instr.IMod _)   (u8 0x81))                  ; i64.rem_s
    ((Instr.IAnd _)   (u8 0x83))                  ; i64.and
    ((Instr.IOr _)    (u8 0x84))                  ; i64.or
    ((Instr.IShl _)   (u8 0x86))                  ; i64.shl
    ((Instr.IShrS _)  (u8 0x87))                  ; i64.shr_s
    ((Instr.IGeU _)   (u8 0x5A))                  ; i64.ge_u
    ((Instr.ILt _)    (u8 0x53))                  ; i64.lt_s
    ((Instr.IGt _)    (u8 0x55))                  ; i64.gt_s
    ((Instr.ILe _)    (u8 0x57))                  ; i64.le_s
    ((Instr.IGe _)    (u8 0x59))                  ; i64.ge_s
    ((Instr.IEq _)    (u8 0x51))                  ; i64.eq
    ((Instr.INe _)    (u8 0x52))                  ; i64.ne
    ((Instr.INot _)   (u8 0x45))                  ; i32.eqz (logical not)
    ((Instr.IExtendU _) (u8 0xAD))                ; i64.extend_i32_u
    ((Instr.IXor _)   (u8 0x85))                  ; i64.xor
    ((Instr.IEqz64 _) (u8 0x50))                  ; i64.eqz
    ((Instr.ILocal n) (cat (u8 0x20) (uleb n)))   ; local.get n
    ((Instr.ISet n)   (cat (u8 0x21) (uleb n)))   ; local.set n
    ((Instr.ICall n)  (cat (u8 0x10) (uleb n)))   ; call n
    ((Instr.IIf k)    (cat (u8 0x04) (u8 (core-valtype k))))  ; if <blocktype>
    ((Instr.IIfVoid _) (cat (u8 0x04) (u8 0x40))) ; if <void blocktype> (a guard producing no value)
    ((Instr.IElse _)  (u8 0x05))                  ; else
    ((Instr.IEnd _)   (u8 0x0B))                  ; end
    ((Instr.IReturn _) (u8 0x0F))                 ; return
    ((Instr.IDrop _)   (u8 0x1A))                 ; drop
    ((Instr.IUnreachable _) (u8 0x00))))          ; unreachable

(def (serialize code)
  (doc "Fold a Code (a built-in `list<Instr>`) to its concatenated opcode bytes — pure serialization.
        Index-walk over the list, concatenating each instruction's opcode bytes in order.")
  (serialize-go code 0 (List.len code)))
(def (serialize-go code i n)
  (if (< i n)
      (cat (match (List.at code i) ((None _) (empty-bytes)) ((Some x) (emit-instr x)))
           (serialize-go code (+ i 1) n))
      (empty-bytes)))

; ─── Node: the surface AST the front end produces ───────────────────────────
; The compiler's OWN untyped surface tree — deliberately NOT the built-in `Ast`
; type, which is a dead end for self-hosting: `quote` will not flow through a
; function call (declines `unbound name: quote`) and `Ast.*` constructors are not
; usable in user code (`unknown sum variant: Ast.List`). A user-declared sum has
; neither problem — it recurses through functions like any other value — so the
; front end decodes input into THIS, exactly as the CBOR reader will.
;
; A form's head is a STRING — the operator name the reader produces (`"+"`, `"and"`,
; …). This is now possible because runtime strings work (Tier 0, fixed). The node
; is a FLAT `(NPrim (Tuple String Node Node))` — a head applied to (up to) two
; operand forms, exactly a reader's output; the flat 3-tuple also avoids the
; nested-tuple-binder gap (Tier 2b) that the earlier `(Tuple Prim (Tuple …))` shape
; hit. `resolve` maps the head string to a `Prim` sum variant (`head-prim`), then an
; EXHAUSTIVE Prim→Core match selects the constructor — so a head is looked up ONCE,
; at the resolve boundary (the "resolve names to codes" step), and no string
; survives into Core. An unknown head resolves to `PUnknown`, which `resolve`
; declines on (a real front-end diagnostic point).

; The head primitives, including the short-circuiting connectives PAnd/POr and a
; PUnknown catch-all for an unrecognized head string.
(type Prim
  (PAdd | PSub | PMul | PDiv | PMod
   | PBitAnd | PBitOr | PXor             ; bitwise & | ^ (total i64 ops — distinct from the Bool PAnd/POr)
   | PShl | PShr                         ; << >> — left/arithmetic-right shift (count-guarded; << overflow-guarded)
   | PLt | PGt | PLe | PGe | PEq | PNe
   | PAnd | POr | PNot
   | PUnknown             ; head `"?"` — an UNSUPPORTED construct → DECLINE (KError 0, silent, native compiles it)
   | PReject))            ; head `"!"` — a MALFORMED/type-error form → REJECT (KError 1, → CDZ0201 diagnostic)

(type Node
  (NInt Int64
   | NFloat Int64            ; a float surface literal (payload = placeholder; resolves to Core.KFloat, check-only)
   | NCompound Int64         ; a COMPOUND literal (tuple/record/list/map) — resolves to Core.KCompound, check-only
   | NBool Int64             ; a Bool surface literal (0/1)
   | NLocal Int64            ; a parameter/let reference by index (the reader resolves a name → index)
   | NPrim (Tuple String Node Node)  ; (head-name, lhs, rhs) — rhs unused for unary `not`
   | NIf (Tuple Node Node Node)  ; (cond, then, else) — the surface conditional form
   | NLet (Tuple Int64 Node Node)  ; (local-index, bound-value, body) — the reader assigns the index
   | NCall (Tuple Int64 (list Node))  ; (fn-index, arg-list) — a call of an N-ary function (reader resolves name→index)
   | NDo (list Node)))          ; (do e1 … eN) — a sequencing block: evaluate each in order, yield the last

(def (head-prim s)
  (doc "Map a surface head NAME (a String) to its Prim variant — the name→code resolution step.
        An unrecognized head is PUnknown, which `resolve` declines on. This is where the reader's
        symbol strings become the compiler's typed operator codes; no string flows past here.")
  (if (= s "+")  (Prim.PAdd ())
  (if (= s "-")  (Prim.PSub ())
  (if (= s "*")  (Prim.PMul ())
  (if (= s "/")  (Prim.PDiv ())
  (if (= s "%")  (Prim.PMod ())
  (if (= s "&")  (Prim.PBitAnd ())
  (if (= s "|")  (Prim.PBitOr ())
  (if (= s "^")  (Prim.PXor ())
  (if (= s "<<") (Prim.PShl ())
  (if (= s ">>") (Prim.PShr ())
  (if (= s "<")  (Prim.PLt ())
  (if (= s ">")  (Prim.PGt ())
  (if (= s "<=") (Prim.PLe ())
  (if (= s ">=") (Prim.PGe ())
  (if (= s "=")  (Prim.PEq ())
  (if (= s "!=") (Prim.PNe ())
  (if (= s "and") (Prim.PAnd ())
  (if (= s "or")  (Prim.POr ())
  (if (= s "not") (Prim.PNot ())
  (if (= s "!")   (Prim.PReject ())          ; the reject sentinel (malformed / type error → CDZ0201)
                  (Prim.PUnknown ()))))))))))))))))))))))

; ─── resolve: Node → Core (name resolution → the typed middle IR) ─────────────
; The front rung: turn the surface tree into the typed, RESOLVED Core sum where
; each primitive is a distinct constructor. The head STRING is resolved to a Prim
; (head-prim), then an exhaustive Prim→Core match selects the constructor — the
; resolve-before-select seam: everything downstream reads a resolved binding (a
; Core constructor), never re-inspects a head string.

(def (resolve node)
  (doc "Resolve a surface Node to typed Core: NInt→KConst, NBool→KBoolC; NPrim's head string maps
        (head-prim) to a Prim, then an exhaustive Prim→Core match selects the constructor. The
        short-circuit connectives desugar HERE to a conditional (core-semantics.md §Boolean
        Connectives Short-Circuit): `(and a b)` = `(if a b false)`, `(or a b)` = `(if a true b)`,
        `(not a)` = KNot. A recursive walk over a user sum, so it flows through calls (unlike the
        built-in Ast/quote). An unknown head (PUnknown) DECLINES — a real front-end rejection point.")
  (match node
    ((Node.NInt n)             (Core.KConst n))
    ((Node.NFloat n)           (Core.KFloat n))
    ((Node.NCompound n)        (Core.KCompound n))
    ((Node.NBool n)            (Core.KBoolC n))
    ((Node.NLocal n)           (Core.KLocal n))
    ((Node.NIf (tuple c t f))  (Core.KIf (tuple (resolve c) (resolve t) (resolve f))))
    ((Node.NLet (tuple ix v body)) (Core.KLet (tuple ix (resolve v) (resolve body))))
    ((Node.NCall (tuple fi args)) (Core.KCall (tuple fi (resolve-args args 0 (list)))))
    ((Node.NDo forms) (Core.KDo (resolve-args forms 0 (list))))
    ((Node.NPrim (tuple h a b))
      (match (head-prim h)
        ((Prim.PAdd _) (Core.KAdd (tuple (resolve a) (resolve b))))
        ((Prim.PSub _) (Core.KSub (tuple (resolve a) (resolve b))))
        ((Prim.PMul _) (Core.KMul (tuple (resolve a) (resolve b))))
        ((Prim.PDiv _) (Core.KDiv (tuple (resolve a) (resolve b))))
        ((Prim.PMod _) (Core.KMod (tuple (resolve a) (resolve b))))
        ((Prim.PBitAnd _) (Core.KBitAnd (tuple (resolve a) (resolve b))))
        ((Prim.PBitOr _)  (Core.KBitOr (tuple (resolve a) (resolve b))))
        ((Prim.PXor _)    (Core.KXor (tuple (resolve a) (resolve b))))
        ((Prim.PShl _) (Core.KShl (tuple (resolve a) (resolve b))))
        ((Prim.PShr _) (Core.KShr (tuple (resolve a) (resolve b))))
        ((Prim.PLt _)  (Core.KLt (tuple (resolve a) (resolve b))))
        ((Prim.PGt _)  (Core.KGt (tuple (resolve a) (resolve b))))
        ((Prim.PLe _)  (Core.KLe (tuple (resolve a) (resolve b))))
        ((Prim.PGe _)  (Core.KGe (tuple (resolve a) (resolve b))))
        ((Prim.PEq _)  (Core.KEq (tuple (resolve a) (resolve b))))
        ((Prim.PNe _)  (Core.KNe (tuple (resolve a) (resolve b))))
        ; short-circuit: (and a b) = (if a b false); (or a b) = (if a true b)
        ((Prim.PAnd _) (Core.KIf (tuple (resolve a) (resolve b) (Core.KBoolC 0))))
        ((Prim.POr _)  (Core.KIf (tuple (resolve a) (Core.KBoolC 1) (resolve b))))
        ((Prim.PNot _) (Core.KNot (resolve a)))
        ; The two failure sentinels resolve to `KError`, whose Int64 payload is now the CDZ diagnostic CODE
        ; (0 = a silent DECLINE; else = the code to emit). Both lower to `unreachable`.
        ; PUnknown (head `"?"`) = an UNSUPPORTED construct → `(KError 0)` DECLINE (native compiles it; we
        ; honestly decline — NO diagnostic). PReject (head `"!"`) = a REJECTED form carrying its code in the
        ; first operand (an NInt, built by `rejected`) → `(KError code)` (native rejects with that code; the
        ; diagnostics check performs `(Diag.emit code)`). No Bytes hack — an honest coded marker.
        ((Prim.PUnknown _) (Core.KError 0))
        ((Prim.PReject _)  (Core.KError (node-int a)))))))

(def (node-int n)
  (doc "The Int64 an NInt surface node carries — used to read a reject sentinel's CDZ code back out of its
        first operand (built by `rejected`). Any non-NInt node defaults to 201 (the generic malformed/type
        code) so a malformed sentinel can never silently become a decline.")
  (match n ((Node.NInt v) v)
           ((Node.NFloat v) 201) ((Node.NCompound v) 201) ((Node.NBool v) 201) ((Node.NLocal v) 201)
           ((Node.NPrim t) 201) ((Node.NIf t) 201) ((Node.NLet t) 201)
           ((Node.NCall t) 201) ((Node.NDo t) 201)))

(def (node-provably-scalar n)
  (doc "Is surface Node `n` a PROVABLE SCALAR (int/bool/float — NOT a compound) — used to reject `(. <scalar>
        field)` / `(tuple.N <scalar>)` as access on a non-record/non-tuple (CDZ0201), which native rejects
        regardless of record support. Resolves `n` to Core and asks `ck-of` (EMPTY ktab — a literal/arithmetic
        receiver has a ktab-independent kind; a NAME/CALL receiver is CKUnk → NOT provably scalar → decline).
        ⚠ Must EXCLUDE CKCompound: a COMPOUND-valued receiver (e.g. `(. (if c r1 r2) f)` where the branches are
        records) is NOT a scalar — a member access on it is VALID (native compiles it), so it must NOT reject.")
  (match (ck-of (resolve n) (list))
    ((CKind.CKi64 _) true) ((CKind.CKBool _) true) ((CKind.CKFloat _) true)
    ((CKind.CKCompound _) false) ((CKind.CKUnk _) false)))

(def (resolve-args args i out)
  (doc "Resolve a call's argument list (a `list Node`) to a `list Core`, in order: append `resolve`
        of each arg to `out`. `i` walks the input; `out` accumulates (a recursive push-loop over the
        arg list — the shape seed Gap A had to fix). Preserves argument order (arg 0 first), matching
        the left-to-right push order `lower-args` emits and wasm's calling convention.")
  (match (List.at args i)
    ((Some a) (resolve-args args (+ i 1) (List.push out (resolve a))))
    (None out)))

; ─── read: canonical AST bytes → surface Node (the reader, now connected) ────
; The recursive reader that decodes the canonical binary AST (deterministic CBOR
; `[version, prelude, root]`) into a surface `Node`. A CBOR array (major 4) is an
; application `[head-index, ...children]` → `NPrim` whose head string is the prelude
; symbol matched by `name-eq`; any scalar is a bare integer atom → `NInt`. Every
; primitive under it (head decode, navigation, prelude location, name match) is
; verified on real bytes; VERIFIED end-to-end: reading the CBOR of `(+ 1 2)` and
; running `resolve → fold → lower → serialize → frame` yields a valid component
; computing 1+2 (`i64.const 1; i64.const 2; i64.add`). A runtime-read program is not
; const-folded (its values arrive at run time), which is correct — folding is a
; compile-time optimization, and a runtime-derived tree is emitted as it stands.

(def (read-root-off b)
  (doc "The offset of the root node: skip the file-array's version (offset 1) and prelude (offset 2)
        to reach the third element.")
  (cbor-skip b 2))

(def (read-elem0-off b i)
  (doc "The offset of an array item's first element (its head-index): past the array head at i.")
  (+ i (cbor-head-len b i)))

(def (read-head-index b i)
  (doc "The head-index (prelude index) of an application array at offset i — its first element.")
  (cbor-arg b (read-elem0-off b i)))

(def (read-child-off b i k)
  (doc "The offset of application child k (0 = head-index, 1 = first operand, …): skip k items from
        the first element.")
  (skip-elems b (read-elem0-off b i) k))

(def (read-op-name b hidx)
  (doc "The operator NAME string for head-index hidx: match the prelude symbol's bytes against the
        known BINARY operator names. An unrecognized head yields \"?\" (which head-prim maps to
        PUnknown → KError). Extend as the operator set grows. `if`/`not` are handled by arity in
        `read-node`, not here (they are not binary NPrim heads).")
  (if (name-eq b (prelude-entry b hidx) b"+" 0 1) "+"
  (if (name-eq b (prelude-entry b hidx) b"-" 0 1) "-"
  (if (name-eq b (prelude-entry b hidx) b"*" 0 1) "*"
  (if (name-eq b (prelude-entry b hidx) b"/" 0 1) "/"
  (if (name-eq b (prelude-entry b hidx) b"%" 0 1) "%"
  (if (name-eq b (prelude-entry b hidx) b"&" 0 1) "&"
  (if (name-eq b (prelude-entry b hidx) b"|" 0 1) "|"
  (if (name-eq b (prelude-entry b hidx) b"^" 0 1) "^"
  (if (name-eq b (prelude-entry b hidx) b"<<" 0 2) "<<"
  (if (name-eq b (prelude-entry b hidx) b">>" 0 2) ">>"
  (if (name-eq b (prelude-entry b hidx) b"<" 0 1) "<"
  (if (name-eq b (prelude-entry b hidx) b">" 0 1) ">"
  (if (name-eq b (prelude-entry b hidx) b"=" 0 1) "="
  (if (name-eq b (prelude-entry b hidx) b"<=" 0 2) "<="
  (if (name-eq b (prelude-entry b hidx) b">=" 0 2) ">="
  (if (name-eq b (prelude-entry b hidx) b"!=" 0 2) "!="
  (if (name-eq b (prelude-entry b hidx) b"and" 0 3) "and"
  (if (name-eq b (prelude-entry b hidx) b"or" 0 2) "or"
                                                  "?")))))))))))))))))))

(def (head-is b hidx name n)
  (doc "Does application head-index hidx name the operator `name` (a Bytes literal of length n)?
        Used by read-node to recognize the arity-special heads `if` (3 operands) and `not` (1).")
  (name-eq b (prelude-entry b hidx) name 0 n))

; ─── Name references (tagged) → parameter locals ─────────────────────────────
; A bare name in a body — e.g. the `x` in `(* x 2)` — encodes as a CBOR TAG
; (major 6, tag 39 = `d8 27 <idx>`) wrapping the name's prelude index. To resolve it
; the reader carries the function's PARAMETER environment: the prelude indices of the
; parameter names, in order. A name-ref whose prelude index is the env's k-th entry is
; local k (`NLocal k`). The env is a plain integer cons-list of prelude indices — read
; from the def's signature `(fn-name param…)`.

(def (name-ref-index b i)
  (doc "The prelude index a tagged name-ref at offset i wraps: past the tag head is a uint whose
        value is the index. `d8 27 03` → 3.")
  (cbor-arg b (+ i (cbor-head-len b i))))

; The param/function environment is a built-in `list<Int64>` of prelude indices (ask-62: the custom
; `IList` cons-sum-type retired for the built-in list, unblocked once the runtime RC use-after-free
; on a multiply-consumed list value was fixed — ask-63). A name's LOCAL SLOT is its POSITION in this
; list; `ienv-snoc` (= `List.push`) appends at the end, so a fresh `let` binding takes the next free
; slot (the old length). `ienv-pos` returns the HIGHEST matching position, preserving lexical shadowing.

(def (ienv-pos xs target k)
  (doc "The 0-based position of prelude-index `target` in the param/local-index list `xs` (searching
        from position k), or -1 if absent. Maps a name-ref's prelude index to its local slot. Returns
        the LAST (highest) matching position, so a `let` that shadows an earlier param or `let` of the
        same name resolves to the innermost binding (its higher slot), matching lexical shadowing.
        Index-scan over the built-in list from k, tracking the highest match seen.")
  (ienv-pos-go xs target k (List.len xs) (- 0 1)))

(def (ienv-pos-go xs target i n best)
  (doc "Scan positions [i,n) of `xs`, returning the HIGHEST index whose element equals `target`, or
        `best` if none in range. Low→high with overwrite-on-match yields the last (highest) match.")
  (if (< i n)
      (ienv-pos-go xs target (+ i 1) n
        (match (List.at xs i) ((None _) best) ((Some h) (if (= h target) i best))))
      best))

(def (ienv-len xs)
  (doc "The number of entries in a param/local env — the count of bound names in scope, hence the next
        free local slot for a fresh `let` binding.")
  (List.len xs))

(def (ienv-snoc xs v)
  (doc "Append prelude-index `v` at the END of env `xs`, so its position — and thus its assigned local
        slot — is the env's old length: a `let` binding sits just past the existing params and lets.")
  (List.push xs v))

; ─── lce: the literal-compound binding environment (constant propagation) ────
; A `let` binding whose value is a LITERAL compound (`(let ((p (record …))) …)`,
; `(let ((t (tuple …))) …)`) has no runtime representation in this compiler (the
; compound declines), but a PROJECTION of that binding folds to a scalar exactly
; as the direct-literal projection does — native const-folds `(let ((p (record (x
; 1)(y 2)))) (. p y))` to `2` (a scalar, no heap). `lce` records, per compound
; binding, its local SLOT → the byte-OFFSET of its literal-compound value node, so
; the `.`/`tuple.` branches can fold a `NLocal` receiver. It is a sparse assoc
; `list` of `(tuple slot offset)` (only compound bindings appear); a non-compound
; binding adds nothing. Threaded alongside `env`; consulted only at projections.
(def (lce-at xs slot)
  (doc "The literal-compound value-node OFFSET bound to local `slot` in `lce` (an assoc list of
        `(tuple slot offset)`), or -1 if `slot` is not bound to a literal compound. Scans; the LAST
        matching entry wins (a shadowing re-bind pushed later sits earlier — scan returns first found,
        and `read-let` prepends, so the innermost binding is found first).")
  (lce-at-go xs 0 slot))
(def (lce-at-go xs i slot)
  (match (List.at xs i)
    ((None _) (- 0 1))
    ((Some (tuple s off)) (if (= s slot) off (lce-at-go xs (+ i 1) slot)))))

(def (read-node b i env fenv lce)
  (doc "Decode the node at offset i to a surface Node, under parameter environment `env` (a list<Int64> of
        the parameter names' prelude indices) and function environment `fenv` (a list<Int64> of the module's
        function-name prelude indices, in def order — a call head resolves to its function index by
        position). By CBOR major type:
          4 (array)     → an application, dispatched by head + ARITY (read-app);
          6 (tag)       → a bare NAME reference (`d8 27 <idx>`): if its prelude index is a parameter
                          (found in env) → NLocal <its position>, else NInt 0 (an unbound name — a
                          placeholder pending real front-end name resolution);
          0 (uint)      → a non-negative integer literal → NInt (cbor-arg);
          1 (neg int)   → NInt (-1 - arg, CBOR's negint convention);
          7 (simple)    → ONLY the two boolean encodings (info 20 = false `0xF4`, 21 = true `0xF5`)
                          → NBool; any other major-7 (a float `0xFB`, null, …) is UNSUPPORTED → an
                          unknown-marker NPrim `\"?\"` (→ PUnknown → KError → `unreachable`): DECLINE,
                          don't miscompile. (A float literal read as a bool would collapse distinct
                          floats to one value and make `(= f1 f2)` spuriously true — a real miscompile
                          the corpus catches; floats are simply not a supported reader node yet.)
        Any OTHER major (2 bytes, 3 text/string, 5 map) is likewise unsupported → the same marker,
        rather than the old fallthrough that misread it as an integer.
        `bytes → Node`; feed to `resolve` for the typed IR.")
  (if (= (cbor-major b i) 4)
      (read-app b i env fenv lce)
  (if (= (cbor-major b i) 6)
      (if (< (ienv-pos env (name-ref-index b i) 0) 0)
          ; a name-ref NOT in the parameter/let env. TWO sub-cases:
          ;  - a NUMERIC-looking symbol (digit-led, optional sign, or a 0x/0b radix prefix) reached us
          ;    as a NAME only because the AST encoder could not parse it as an i64 — i.e. it is OUT OF
          ;    the Int64 range: a malformed literal, native rejects CDZ0201. Emit `(rejected 201)` — not
          ;    a silent decline (mirrors the seed's `looks_like_numeric_literal` reclassification).
          ;  - otherwise a genuine unbound name (`unit`, a nullary constructor, a free variable): NOT a
          ;    local (emitting `local.get -1` would be INVALID); DECLINE to the unknown-marker (→ KError
          ;    0 → trap, no diagnostic): decline, don't miscompile.
          (if (name-is-numeric b (prelude-entry b (name-ref-index b i)))
              (rejected 201)
              (Node.NPrim (tuple "?" (Node.NInt 0) (Node.NInt 0))))
          ; A bound name. If its slot is a LITERAL-COMPOUND binding (in `lce`), a BARE reference (this path,
          ; a non-projection use) has no runtime rep — DECLINE (a projection would have folded via
          ; `compound-receiver-off` before reaching here; only a bare/non-projection use lands here). Else it
          ; is an ordinary local → its slot.
          (if (>= (lce-at lce (ienv-pos env (name-ref-index b i) 0)) 0)
              (declined)
              (Node.NLocal (ienv-pos env (name-ref-index b i) 0))))
  (if (= (cbor-major b i) 0)
      (if (uint-in-i64-range b i)
          (Node.NInt (cbor-arg b i))           ; uint literal, in range
          (rejected 201))                      ; ≥ 2^63 > Int64.max — out of range → CDZ0201 (not a wrapped bit pattern)
  (if (= (cbor-major b i) 1)
      (if (uint-in-i64-range b i)
          (Node.NInt (- (- 0 1) (cbor-arg b i)))   ; negint: value = -1 - arg, in range
          (rejected 201))                      ; negint arg ≥ 2^63 ⇒ value < Int64.min — out of range → CDZ0201
  (if (= (cbor-major b i) 7)
      (if (= (cbor-info b i) 20)
          (Node.NBool 0)                       ; 0xF4 = false
      (if (= (cbor-info b i) 21)
          (Node.NBool 1)                       ; 0xF5 = true
      (if (>= (cbor-info b i) 25)
          (Node.NFloat 0)                      ; 0xF9/0xFA/0xFB = float16/32/64 → a FLOAT literal (check-only,
                                               ; codegen declines it; distinct from `?` so a mismatch is provable)
          (Node.NPrim (tuple "?" (Node.NInt 0) (Node.NInt 0))))))  ; other major-7 (null/undefined) — decline
      (Node.NPrim (tuple "?" (Node.NInt 0) (Node.NInt 0)))))))))  ; other majors (bytes/text/map) unsupported → KError

(def (app-arity b i)
  (doc "The operand count of an application array at offset i: its CBOR array length minus 1 (the head
        element). `(dbl 21)` = `[dbl-idx, 21]` → arity 1; `(+ a b)` → arity 2.")
  (- (cbor-arg b i) 1))

(def (rejected code)
  (doc "A surface node for a REJECTED form carrying its CDZ diagnostic CODE — the `\"!\"` REJECT head
        resolves to PReject → `(KError code)` → `unreachable` (a defined trap) AND a `(Diag.emit code)`
        diagnostic. The code rides in the sentinel's first operand (an NInt); `resolve`'s PReject arm reads
        it back (reject-code). This is how the reader carries a SPECIFIC code (CDZ0201 malformed, CDZ0210
        non-exhaustive) to the diagnostics channel — coded diagnostics, not a monochrome trap.")
  (Node.NPrim (tuple "!" (Node.NInt code) (Node.NInt 0))))

(def (malformed)
  (doc "A surface node for a MALFORMED application (an arity mismatch on a fixed-arity form) → CDZ0201 —
        native rejects `(+ 1)` / `(if c t)` with `malformed … form: arity mismatch`, code CDZ0201.
        Decline-don't-miscompile: a wrong-operand-count form is rejected, not silently truncated.
        (Distinct from `\"?\"` = an UNSUPPORTED construct, which DECLINES silently — native compiles it.)")
  (rejected 201))

(def (declined)
  (doc "A surface node for an UNSUPPORTED construct (a `\"?\"` head → PUnknown → `(KError 0)` DECLINE →
        `unreachable`, NO diagnostic). Distinct from `malformed` (`\"!\"` REJECT → CDZ0201): a decline is a
        construct native COMPILES that this compiler does not handle yet (a user-sum `match`, a float, a
        string) — reject-don't-miscompile without falsely claiming it is a type error.")
  (Node.NPrim (tuple "?" (Node.NInt 0) (Node.NInt 0))))

(def (record-has-dup b i)
  (doc "Does a `record`/`map` application at offset i repeat a field name / key? Each entry `(name val)` is
        child 1..arity — an array whose HEAD-INDEX is the name's prelude index. Duplicate iff two entries
        share a head-index. Scans every pair (arity is tiny). A non-array entry has no name → contributes
        no index (skipped), so a MALFORMED-shaped record declines rather than false-flagging. Distinct
        prelude indices ⇒ distinct names (interning), so exact-index compare is native's field identity.")
  (dup-scan-outer b i 1 (+ (app-arity b i) 1)))

(def (entry-name-index b i k)
  (doc "The field-name prelude index of `record`/`map` entry k (an array child `(name val)` at child k):
        its head-index. -1 for a non-array entry (no name — cannot participate in a duplicate).")
  (let ((off (read-child-off b i k)))
    (if (= (cbor-major b off) 4) (read-head-index b off) (- 0 1))))

(def (dup-scan-outer b i k end)
  (doc "For each entry k in [k,end), test whether its name-index recurs LATER in the list; recurse k+1.
        O(n^2) over the (tiny) entry count — a repeated field name anywhere ⇒ true.")
  (if (< k end)
      (let ((idx (entry-name-index b i k)))
        (if (and (>= idx 0) (dup-scan-inner b i (+ k 1) end idx))
            true
            (dup-scan-outer b i (+ k 1) end)))
      false))

(def (dup-scan-inner b i k end target)
  (doc "Does any entry in [k,end) have name-index `target`? (The inner half of the pairwise duplicate scan.)")
  (if (< k end)
      (if (= (entry-name-index b i k) target)
          true
          (dup-scan-inner b i (+ k 1) end target))
      false))

; ─── constant projection folding: (tuple.N <tuple-lit>) / (. <record-lit> f) ──
; A projection of a LITERAL compound to one of its elements is a compile-time
; constant the seed FOLDS to a scalar (`(tuple.1 (tuple 42 true))` → `true`,
; `(. (record (x 1) (y 2)) y)` → `2`). Since the compound is written inline, the
; reader can select the element directly — no heap, the result rides the scalar
; envelope. A NON-literal receiver (a name / call / scalar) is NOT folded here (a
; scalar receiver is the CDZ0201 reject above; a name is a decline). Nested
; projections compose because the selected element is itself read via `read-node`.
(def (tuple-accessor-index b hidx)
  (doc "The index N of a `tuple.N` accessor head (prelude entry hidx): parse the decimal digits after the
        `tuple.` prefix (offset 6). Multi-digit supported. Assumes `name-has-prefix …b\"tuple.\" 6` already held.")
  (tuple-accessor-index-go b (prelude-entry b hidx) 6 (entry-len b (prelude-entry b hidx)) 0))
(def (tuple-accessor-index-go b e j n acc)
  (if (< j n)
      (tuple-accessor-index-go b e (+ j 1) n (+ (* acc 10) (- (entry-byte b e j) 48)))
      acc))

(def (node-head-is b roff name nlen)
  (doc "Is the node at offset roff an APPLICATION (CBOR array) whose head names `name` (nlen bytes)? Used to
        detect a literal `(tuple …)` / `(record …)` receiver. A non-array node (a scalar/tag) → false.")
  (if (= (cbor-major b roff) 4)
      (name-eq b (prelude-entry b (read-head-index b roff)) name 0 nlen)
      false))

; ─── constructor-value recognition (ask-60 heap types, const sum-ctor tier) ──
; A CONSTRUCTOR value `(Some 42)`/`(None unit)`/`(Ok (Some 3))` renders structurally like a tuple —
; `(Head elem…)` — and native's rule is CAPITALIZATION: a capitalized head is a nominal constructor
; (rendered structurally), lowercase is an operator/call (folded/called). A plain application ARRAY
; head is always a bare, dot-free name (a dotted `A.B x` becomes the `apply`/`.` shape, head "apply"),
; so the first-byte-uppercase test is the whole discriminator. We only EMIT where native does (a strict
; subset — decline-don't-miscompile): unit-payload for ANY capitalized head, else a verified WHITELIST.
(def (head-is-ctor? b i)
  (doc "Is the node at i an application whose head is a CONSTRUCTOR — a capitalized (A-Z first byte) bare
        name? `tuple`/`list`/`record` and all operators are lowercase, so they never match.")
  (if (= (cbor-major b i) 4)
      (let ((e (prelude-entry b (read-head-index b i))))
        (if (< (entry-len b e) 1)
            false
            (let ((c (entry-byte b e 0))) (and (>= c 65) (<= c 90)))))
      false))

(def (prelude-first-upper? b k)
  (doc "Does prelude entry k's symbol start with an uppercase letter (A-Z)? (Empty → false.)")
  (let ((e (prelude-entry b k)))
    (if (< (entry-len b e) 1)
        false
        (let ((c (entry-byte b e 0))) (and (>= c 65) (<= c 90))))))

(def (dotted-ctor-unit? b i)
  (doc "Is the node at i a DOTTED-CONSTRUCTOR value with a UNIT payload — `(apply (. Recv Variant) unit)`
        where BOTH `Recv` and `Variant` are capitalized (a nominal dotted ctor like `Sign.Zero`/`Ordering.Less`,
        NOT a method call like `Bytes.of` where the member is lowercase)? Unit-payload only: native ALWAYS
        renders `(Recv.Variant unit)`, whereas a non-unit dotted payload is native-type-inference-dependent
        (some accept, some decline — like the sum-eq boundary), so we under-decline those. AST: head `apply`,
        child 1 = `(. Recv Variant)` (a `.`-form arity 2), child 2 = the payload.")
  (if (and (and (= (cbor-major b i) 4) (node-head-is b i b"apply" 5)) (= (app-arity b i) 2))
      (let ((m (read-child-off b i 1)))
        (if (and (and (= (cbor-major b m) 4) (head-is b (read-head-index b m) b"." 1)) (= (app-arity b m) 2))
            (and (and (prelude-first-upper? b (name-ref-index b (read-child-off b m 1)))     ; Recv capitalized
                      (prelude-first-upper? b (name-ref-index b (read-child-off b m 2))))    ; Variant capitalized
                 (node-is-unit? b (read-child-off b i 2)))                                   ; payload = unit
            false))
      false))

(def (render-dotted-ctor-unit b i)
  (doc "Render a dotted-ctor unit value `(apply (. Recv Variant) unit)` → `(Recv.Variant unit)`. The head
        name is `Recv.Variant` (the two prelude symbols joined by `.`); the payload is always `unit`.")
  (let ((m (read-child-off b i 1)))
    (cat (u8 40)                                                                             ; "("
    (cat (entry-bytes b (prelude-entry b (name-ref-index b (read-child-off b m 1))) 0
                      (entry-len b (prelude-entry b (name-ref-index b (read-child-off b m 1)))) (empty-bytes))  ; Recv
    (cat (u8 46)                                                                             ; "."
    (cat (entry-bytes b (prelude-entry b (name-ref-index b (read-child-off b m 2))) 0
                      (entry-len b (prelude-entry b (name-ref-index b (read-child-off b m 2)))) (empty-bytes))  ; Variant
         (Bytes.of (list 32 117 110 105 116 41))))))))                                       ; " unit)"

; ─── let value-passthrough (ask-60): `(let ((v VALUE)) v)` → VALUE ──────────────
; A `let` binding a value and returning EXACTLY the bound variable is equivalent to the value itself:
; `(let ((s (Sign.Pos unit))) s)` = `(Sign.Pos unit)`. Recognize it so the value renders through the let.
; AST: `(let <bindings> <body>)` where bindings = `[apply, [name-idx, VALUE]]` (ONE binding) and body is a
; name-ref to that same `name-idx`. Returns VALUE's offset (else -1). Only the SINGLE-binding, body-is-the-var
; shape — a body that USES the var (`(+ x 1)`) or returns a DIFFERENT thing is not this passthrough.
(def (let-passthru-off b i)
  (doc "If node i is `(let ((v VALUE)) v)` — one binding, body = a bare name-ref to the bound var — the byte
        offset of VALUE; else -1. The let's transparent value-passthrough.")
  (if (and (and (= (cbor-major b i) 4) (head-is b (read-head-index b i) b"let" 3)) (= (app-arity b i) 2))
      (let ((bindings (read-child-off b i 1)) (body (read-child-off b i 2)))
        (if (= (cbor-arg-len-bindings b bindings) 1)                              ; exactly one binding
            (let ((binding (read-child-off b bindings 1)))                        ; the [name-idx, VALUE] entry
              (if (and (= (cbor-major b body) 6)                                  ; body is a name-ref …
                       (= (name-ref-index b body) (read-head-index b binding)))   ; … to the bound var's name
                  (read-child-off b binding 1)                                    ; → VALUE's offset
                  (- 0 1)))
            (- 0 1)))
      (- 0 1)))

(def (node-is-unit? b i)
  (doc "Is the node at i the literal `unit` — a name-ref (CBOR major 6 tag) to the prelude symbol \"unit\"?
        The payload of a nullary constructor value `(None unit)` and a `unit` leaf inside a compound.")
  (if (= (cbor-major b i) 6)
      (name-eq b (prelude-entry b (name-ref-index b i)) b"unit" 0 4)
      false))

(def (node-is-unit-value? b i)
  (doc "Is the node at i the UNIT value in EITHER spelling — the bare name `unit` (a name-ref) OR the empty
        application `()` (a major-4 array headed `apply` with arity 0)? Both denote the sole unit value. Used
        to fold unit-equality (always true — there is exactly one unit value).")
  (if (node-is-unit? b i)
      true
      (and (and (= (cbor-major b i) 4) (node-head-is b i b"apply" 5)) (= (app-arity b i) 0))))

(def (node-is-none? b i)
  (doc "Is the node at i the bare `None` value — a name-ref (CBOR major 6) to the prelude symbol \"None\"?
        (`None` is a nullary constructor spelled bare; it reads as an unbound capitalized name here.) Used to
        fold `(= (String.from-bytes P) None)` — the LHS is `None` iff P is ill-formed UTF-8.")
  (if (= (cbor-major b i) 6)
      (name-eq b (prelude-entry b (name-ref-index b i)) b"None" 0 4)
      false))

(def (from-bytes-producer-off b i)
  (doc "If node i is `(String.from-bytes P)` (a `(. String from-bytes)` application, arity 2) over a foldable
        const byte producer P, return P's offset; else -1. Gates the `(= (String.from-bytes P) None)` fold.")
  (if (and (dotted-method? b i b"String" 6 b"from-bytes" 10) (= (app-arity b i) 2))
      (if (node-is-bytes-producer? b (read-child-off b i 2)) (read-child-off b i 2) (- 0 1))
      (- 0 1)))

(def (head-in-ctor-whitelist? b i)
  (doc "Is the constructor head at i one of the VERIFIED payload-carrying built-in constructors —
        Some/Ok/Err/Left/Right/Just? These render a one-arg payload structurally on native. An unknown
        capitalized head with a non-unit payload is NOT emitted (native may render it, but we under-decline
        rather than risk a known-nullary like None/Zero/Pos/Neg that native DECLINES on a non-unit payload).")
  (let ((e (prelude-entry b (read-head-index b i))))
    (if (name-eq b e b"Some" 0 4) true
    (if (name-eq b e b"Ok" 0 2) true
    (if (name-eq b e b"Err" 0 3) true
    (if (name-eq b e b"Left" 0 4) true
    (if (name-eq b e b"Right" 0 5) true
    (if (name-eq b e b"Just" 0 4) true false))))))))

; ─── string-literal value recognition + render (ask-60 heap types, const string tier) ──
; A CONST string literal as a value `"hello"` is a heap value that displays as `"` + escaped-contents
; + `"`. The AST stores it as a CBOR TEXT node (major 3): its raw UTF-8 bytes follow the head. Native's
; display re-escapes a CLOSED set — `"`→`\"`, `\`→`\\`, newline(10)→`\n`, CR(13)→`\r`, tab(9)→`\t` — and
; a NON-PRINTABLE control byte as `\u{hex}`. We EMIT only strings whose every byte is renderable via the
; closed 5-escape set or is a printable/UTF-8 byte (≥0x20, ≠ the 5 specials); any byte needing `\u{}`
; (a C0 control other than \n\r\t) → DECLINE (we don't reproduce native's `\u{hex}` form — under-decline,
; never mis-render). Multi-byte UTF-8 (bytes ≥0x80) passes through verbatim, matching native.
(def (node-is-string? b i)
  (doc "Is the node at i a STRING literal — a CBOR text item (major 3)?")
  (= (cbor-major b i) 3))

(def (node-is-float-lit? b i)
  (doc "Is the node at i a FLOAT literal — CBOR major 7 with additional-info ≥ 25 (float16/32/64: 0xF9/FA/FB)?
        (`nan`/`inf` are NAME-REFS, not float literals, so they are NOT this — they decline, which is correct.)")
  (and (= (cbor-major b i) 7) (>= (cbor-info b i) 25)))

(def (float-lit-eq? b i j)
  (doc "Are two FLOAT literals equal? The reader normalizes a float to its SHORTEST-EXACT CBOR encoding, so
        two float literals are equal IN VALUE iff their CBOR item bytes are IDENTICAL — verified: `1.0`/`1.00`/
        `1e0` all encode `f9 3c 00`; `100.0`/`1e2` both `f9 56 40`. This is native's CANONICAL-BYTE-FORM
        equality (NOT IEEE `f64.eq`): `-0.0` (`f9 80 00`) ≠ `0.0` (`f9 00 00`) — they differ in the sign bit,
        and native agrees they are unequal. Compare head-length then each byte over the item's span.")
  (if (= (cbor-head-len b i) (cbor-head-len b j))
      (float-lit-eq-go b i j 0 (cbor-head-len b i))
      false))

(def (float-lit-eq-go b i j k n)
  (doc "Compare the CBOR item bytes [k,n) of the two float literals at i and j; all equal ⇒ true.")
  (if (< k n)
      (if (= (byte-at b (+ i k)) (byte-at b (+ j k)))
          (float-lit-eq-go b i j (+ k 1) n)
          false)
      true))

; ─── const compound equality (ask-60): structural `=` of two literal compounds ──
; `(= (tuple 1 2) (tuple 1 2))`, `(= (Some 5) (None unit))`, `(= (list 1 2) (list 1 2 3))`, records, nested —
; folds to a const Bool by a 3-VALUED recursive comparator returning -1 (DECLINE — not comparable / type
; mismatch native rejects), 0 (false), or 1 (true). Native's rules, mirrored exactly:
;   • two SCALARS (int/bool/float/string) → comparable iff SAME KIND; equal by value; a kind mismatch DECLINES.
;   • two TUPLES → same arity AND each element pair comparable (recurse); a different arity or an element
;     type-mismatch DECLINES (native rejects a heterogeneous-position tuple `=`).
;   • two RECORDS → same field NAMES in the same positions; values compared; a different field set DECLINES.
;   • two LISTS → element type must be consistent; a differing LENGTH is `false` (NOT decline — lists are
;     homogeneous); an element type mismatch DECLINES. Empty lists are equal.
;   • two SUM ctors → the SAME sum TYPE (built-in ctor families) → compare variant tag then payload; a
;     DIFFERENT sum type (Some vs Ok) DECLINES. Same tag → compare payloads; different tag → false.
;   • unit = unit → true. Anything else (a name, a call, a non-literal) → DECLINE.
; This is a compile-time structural walk over two AST trees — no heap.
(def (val-kind b i)
  (doc "A small code for the VALUE KIND of literal node i, for compound `=` type-compatibility: 1=int, 2=bool,
        3=float, 4=string, 5=unit, 6=tuple, 7=list, 8=record, 9=ctor(sum), 0=unknown/non-literal. Two operands
        are `=`-comparable only if their kinds match (and, for ctor, the same sum family — checked separately).")
  (if (node-is-const-int? b i) 1
  (if (= (cbor-major b i) 7) (if (< (cbor-info b i) 22) 2 (if (>= (cbor-info b i) 25) 3 0))  ; info 20/21=bool, ≥25=float
  (if (node-is-string? b i) 4
  (if (node-is-unit-value? b i) 5
  (if (and (= (cbor-major b i) 4) (node-head-is b i b"tuple" 5)) 6
  (if (and (= (cbor-major b i) 4) (node-head-is b i b"list" 4)) 7
  (if (and (= (cbor-major b i) 4) (node-head-is b i b"record" 6)) 8
  (if (head-is-ctor? b i) 9 0)))))))))

(def (bool-lit-val b i)
  (doc "The 0/1 value of a bool literal (major-7 info 20=false/21=true) at i.")
  (if (= (cbor-info b i) 21) 1 0))

(def (compound-eq b i j)
  (doc "3-valued structural equality of two LITERAL nodes: -1 decline (not comparable / type mismatch native
        rejects), 0 false, 1 true. Dispatches on `val-kind`; a kind mismatch → -1 (EXCEPT: NOTHING is comparable
        across kinds — even int vs bool → -1, matching native). Recurses for compound kinds.")
  (let ((ki (val-kind b i)) (kj (val-kind b j)))
    (if (or (= ki 0) (or (= kj 0) (not (= ki kj))))
        (- 0 1)                                                   ; unknown kind or kind mismatch → decline
    (if (= ki 1) (if (= (cbor-int-value b i) (cbor-int-value b j)) 1 0)          ; int
    (if (= ki 2) (if (= (bool-lit-val b i) (bool-lit-val b j)) 1 0)              ; bool
    (if (= ki 3) (if (float-lit-eq? b i j) 1 0)                                  ; float (canonical byte form)
    (if (= ki 4) (if (str-bytes-eq? b i j) 1 0)                                  ; string
    (if (= ki 5) 1                                                              ; unit = unit → true
    (if (= ki 6) (compound-eq-tuple b i j)                                       ; tuple
    (if (= ki 7) (compound-eq-list b i j)                                        ; list
    (if (= ki 8) (compound-eq-record b i j)                                      ; record
                 (- 0 1))))))))))))                                              ; ctor(sum): DECLINE (same-sum-type
                                                                                 ; identity is a type check we don't
                                                                                 ; model safely — Some/None same,
                                                                                 ; Some/Ok different; under-decline)

(def (compound-eq-tuple b i j)
  (doc "Tuple `=`: same arity AND every element pair comparable-and-equal → 1; different arity → -1 (type
        error native rejects); an element pair not comparable → -1; any element unequal → 0.")
  (if (= (app-arity b i) (app-arity b j))
      (compound-eq-elems b i j 1 (+ (app-arity b i) 1))
      (- 0 1)))

(def (compound-eq-elems b i j k end)
  (doc "Compare tuple/ctor-payload element pairs [k,end) via compound-eq: any -1 → -1 (decline); any 0 → 0
        (false, but ONLY after confirming the pair was comparable); all 1 → 1.")
  (if (< k end)
      (let ((e (compound-eq b (read-child-off b i k) (read-child-off b j k))))
        (if (< e 0) (- 0 1)                                       ; a pair not comparable → whole thing declines
        (if (= e 0) 0                                             ; a comparable pair unequal → false
            (compound-eq-elems b i j (+ k 1) end))))              ; equal so far → continue
      1))

(def (compound-eq-list b i j)
  (doc "List `=`: differing LENGTH → 0 (false; lists are homogeneous, so different lengths are still
        comparable). Same length → element-wise via compound-eq; an element pair not comparable (mixed element
        type) → -1. Empty lists → 1.")
  (if (= (app-arity b i) (app-arity b j))
      (compound-eq-elems b i j 1 (+ (app-arity b i) 1))
      ; different length: still must be element-TYPE compatible to be a valid `=` (native declines a
      ; heterogeneous-element list). Probe the first element pair for comparability; if either is empty, ok.
      (if (list-elem-types-ok b i j) 0 (- 0 1))))

(def (list-elem-types-ok b i j)
  (doc "For two DIFFERENT-length lists, are their element types compatible (so `=` is `false`, not a type
        error)? Conservative: if BOTH non-empty, the first elements must be the same val-kind; if either is
        empty, ok (an empty list is compatible with any list of that element type — native says false).")
  (if (or (< (app-arity b i) 1) (< (app-arity b j) 1))
      true
      (= (val-kind b (read-child-off b i 1)) (val-kind b (read-child-off b j 1)))))

(def (compound-eq-record b i j)
  (doc "Record `=`: same field COUNT and same field NAMES in the same positions → compare values; a different
        field set → -1 (type error). Each field entry is `(fieldname value)` — head = name, child 1 = value.")
  (if (= (app-arity b i) (app-arity b j))
      (compound-eq-fields b i j 1 (+ (app-arity b i) 1))
      (- 0 1)))

(def (compound-eq-fields b i j k end)
  (doc "Compare record field entries [k,end): field NAMES must match (else -1); values compared via
        compound-eq; any -1 → -1, any 0 → 0, all 1 → 1.")
  (if (< k end)
      (let ((ei (read-child-off b i k)) (ej (read-child-off b j k)))
        (if (prelude-syms-eq? b (read-head-index b ei) (read-head-index b ej))  ; same field name?
            (let ((e (compound-eq b (read-child-off b ei 1) (read-child-off b ej 1))))
              (if (< e 0) (- 0 1) (if (= e 0) 0 (compound-eq-fields b i j (+ k 1) end))))
            (- 0 1)))                                             ; field names differ → decline
      1))

(def (prelude-syms-eq? b ka kb)
  (doc "Do prelude entries ka and kb hold the SAME symbol bytes? Compare length then each byte — a
        prelude-vs-prelude name match (name-eq compares against a Bytes LITERAL, so it can't be used here).")
  (let ((ea (prelude-entry b ka)) (eb (prelude-entry b kb)))
    (if (= (entry-len b ea) (entry-len b eb))
        (prelude-syms-eq-go b ea eb 0 (entry-len b ea))
        false)))

(def (prelude-syms-eq-go b ea eb j n)
  (doc "Byte-compare prelude symbols ea, eb over [j,n) (lengths known equal).")
  (if (< j n)
      (if (= (entry-byte b ea j) (entry-byte b eb j))
          (prelude-syms-eq-go b ea eb (+ j 1) n)
          false)
      true))

(def (str-bytes-eq? b i j)
  (doc "Are the two STRING literals at offsets i and j byte-for-byte equal? Compares length then each
        payload byte. The reader stores strings in NORMALIZED (NFC) form, so a byte comparison of the
        stored text is exactly the language's string equality (native folds `(= \"café\" \"café\")` → true
        on the identical stored bytes; `(= \"\\t\" \" \")` → false). Used to const-fold `(= strlit strlit)`.")
  (if (= (entry-len b i) (entry-len b j))
      (str-bytes-eq-go b i j 0 (entry-len b i))
      false))

(def (str-bytes-eq-go b i j k n)
  (doc "Compare payload bytes [k,n) of the two text literals at i and j; all equal ⇒ true.")
  (if (< k n)
      (if (= (byte-at b (+ (payload-off b i) k)) (byte-at b (+ (payload-off b j) k)))
          (str-bytes-eq-go b i j (+ k 1) n)
          false)
      true))

(def (str-byte-len b i)
  (doc "The BYTE length (UTF-8 byte count) of the string literal at offset i — the stored text's payload
        length. Native `String.byte-len` on a literal folds to this (`\"café\"` → 5, `\"😀\"` → 4).")
  (entry-len b i))

(def (str-scalar-len b i)
  (doc "The SCALAR length (Unicode scalar count) of the string literal at i: count UTF-8 LEAD bytes — a byte
        is a continuation iff `(b & 0xC0) == 0x80`, so a lead byte is `(b & 0xC0) != 0x80`. Native
        `String.scalar-len` folds to this (`\"café\"` → 4, `\"😀\"` → 1, `\"\"` → 0). The stored text is valid
        UTF-8 (the reader validated it), so counting lead bytes is exactly the scalar count.")
  (str-scalar-len-go b i 0 (entry-len b i) 0))

(def (str-scalar-len-go b i k n acc)
  (doc "Count UTF-8 lead bytes in payload [k,n) of the text at i.")
  (if (< k n)
      (str-scalar-len-go b i (+ k 1) n
        (if (= (& (byte-at b (+ (payload-off b i) k)) 0xC0) 0x80) acc (+ acc 1)))
      acc))

; ─── string-PRODUCER model (ask-60): a string literal OR `(String.concat a b)` over producers ──
; A String value is UTF-8 bytes. To fold `String.byte-len`/`scalar-len`/`=` over a `String.concat` of
; literals, model a string PRODUCER recursively (mirroring the byte-producer model): `str-prod-len` = total
; UTF-8 byte count; `str-prod-nth` = the k-th UTF-8 byte. A producer is a string LITERAL (major 3) or
; `(String.concat p q)` over two producers. A non-producer (a name/call/other) → not foldable (-1 len).
(def (node-is-str-producer? b i)
  (doc "Is node i a foldable string producer — a string literal, or `(String.concat p q)` over producers?")
  (if (node-is-string? b i)
      true
      (if (and (dotted-method? b i b"String" 6 b"concat" 6) (= (app-arity b i) 3))
          (and (node-is-str-producer? b (read-child-off b i 2)) (node-is-str-producer? b (read-child-off b i 3)))
          false)))

(def (str-prod-len b i)
  (doc "The UTF-8 BYTE length of a string producer: a literal → its payload length; `String.concat p q` →
        len(p)+len(q). Callers gate on `node-is-str-producer?`.")
  (if (node-is-string? b i)
      (entry-len b i)
      (+ (str-prod-len b (read-child-off b i 2)) (str-prod-len b (read-child-off b i 3)))))  ; concat

(def (str-prod-nth b i k)
  (doc "The k-th UTF-8 byte of a string producer: a literal → payload byte k; `String.concat p q` → p's byte
        k if k < len(p), else q's (k-len(p))-th. Callers guarantee 0 ≤ k < len.")
  (if (node-is-string? b i)
      (byte-at b (+ (payload-off b i) k))
      (let ((lp (str-prod-len b (read-child-off b i 2))))
        (if (< k lp)
            (str-prod-nth b (read-child-off b i 2) k)
            (str-prod-nth b (read-child-off b i 3) (- k lp))))))

(def (str-prod-scalar-len b i)
  (doc "The Unicode SCALAR count of a string producer: count UTF-8 lead bytes (`(byte & 0xC0) != 0x80`) over
        its whole byte sequence. The producer's bytes are valid UTF-8 (concat of validated literals), so
        lead-byte count = scalar count.")
  (str-prod-scalar-len-go b i 0 (str-prod-len b i) 0))

(def (str-prod-scalar-len-go b i k n acc)
  (doc "Count lead bytes in producer i over [k,n) via str-prod-nth.")
  (if (< k n)
      (str-prod-scalar-len-go b i (+ k 1) n
        (if (= (& (str-prod-nth b i k) 0xC0) 0x80) acc (+ acc 1)))
      acc))

(def (str-prod-eq? b i j)
  (doc "Are two string producers equal by byte sequence? Same length AND each byte equal (via str-prod-nth).")
  (if (= (str-prod-len b i) (str-prod-len b j))
      (str-prod-eq-go b i j 0 (str-prod-len b i))
      false))

(def (str-prod-eq-go b i j k n)
  (doc "Compare bytes [k,n) of two string producers via str-prod-nth.")
  (if (< k n)
      (if (= (str-prod-nth b i k) (str-prod-nth b j k))
          (str-prod-eq-go b i j (+ k 1) n)
          false)
      true))

; ─── const String.scalar-len / String.byte-len fold (ask-60) ──────────────────
; `(String.scalar-len "…")` / `(String.byte-len "…")` on a STRING LITERAL folds to a const Int at compile
; time (no heap, no runtime String). The dotted method encodes as `(apply (. String <method>) <arg>)` — a
; major-4 arity-2 application headed by `apply` whose child 1 is `(. String <method>)` (a `.`-form arity-2
; with receiver `String`) and child 2 is the argument. We fold ONLY when the arg is a string literal and
; the method is `scalar-len`/`byte-len`; any other method or a runtime arg falls through → decline.
(def (str-method-of b i)
  (doc "If node i is `(apply (. String <m>) <arg>)` where <arg> is a string PRODUCER (a literal or a
        `String.concat` of producers), the method NAME as a small code: 1 = scalar-len, 2 = byte-len, 0 =
        neither/not this shape. The gate for the scalar-len/byte-len fold — now over producers, so
        `(String.byte-len (String.concat \"ab\" \"cde\"))` → 5 folds too.")
  (if (and (and (= (cbor-major b i) 4) (node-head-is b i b"apply" 5)) (= (app-arity b i) 2))
      (let ((m (read-child-off b i 1)))
        (if (and (and (and (= (cbor-major b m) 4) (head-is b (read-head-index b m) b"." 1)) (= (app-arity b m) 2))
                 (name-eq b (prelude-entry b (name-ref-index b (read-child-off b m 1))) b"String" 0 6))
            (if (node-is-str-producer? b (read-child-off b i 2))
                (let ((mn (read-child-off b m 2)))
                  (if (name-eq b (prelude-entry b (name-ref-index b mn)) b"scalar-len" 0 10) 1
                  (if (name-eq b (prelude-entry b (name-ref-index b mn)) b"byte-len" 0 8) 2 0)))
                0)
            0))
      0))

; ─── const Bytes.len fold (ask-60) ────────────────────────────────────────────
; `(Bytes.len (Bytes.of (list <const-bytes>)))` and `(Bytes.len (String.to-bytes "…"))` fold to a const Int
; — the byte count, computed from the literal at compile time (no heap). `(Bytes.of (list …))` requires every
; element a const int in 0..255 (native rejects an out-of-range byte, so a bad element → NOT foldable → decline).
(def (dotted-method? b i recv rlen meth mlen)
  (doc "Is node i the dotted method `(apply (. <recv> <meth>) …)` — a major-4 arity≥1 `apply` whose child 1 is
        a `.`-form (arity 2) with receiver name `recv` (rlen bytes) and member `meth` (mlen bytes)?")
  (if (and (and (= (cbor-major b i) 4) (node-head-is b i b"apply" 5)) (>= (app-arity b i) 1))
      (let ((m (read-child-off b i 1)))
        (if (and (and (= (cbor-major b m) 4) (head-is b (read-head-index b m) b"." 1)) (= (app-arity b m) 2))
            (and (name-eq b (prelude-entry b (name-ref-index b (read-child-off b m 1))) recv 0 rlen)
                 (name-eq b (prelude-entry b (name-ref-index b (read-child-off b m 2))) meth 0 mlen))
            false))
      false))

(def (const-bytes-len b i)
  (doc "The const BYTE-LENGTH of node i if it is a foldable byte producer, else -1. Forms:
        `(Bytes.of (list <b0 …>))` — every element a const int in 0..255 → element count (out-of-range/
        non-const → -1, matching native's reject/decline); `(String.to-bytes \"…\")` → the string's UTF-8
        byte length; `(Bytes.concat a b)` → len(a)+len(b) if BOTH fold (else -1); `(Bytes.compact x)` →
        len(x). Recurses through concat/compact. Anything else → -1 (not foldable here).")
  (if (dotted-method? b i b"Bytes" 5 b"of" 2)
      (let ((lst (read-child-off b i 2)))                                    ; the (list …) argument
        (if (and (= (cbor-major b lst) 4) (node-head-is b lst b"list" 4))
            (bytes-of-count b lst 1 (+ (app-arity b lst) 1) 0)
            (- 0 1)))
  (if (dotted-method? b i b"String" 6 b"to-bytes" 8)
      (if (node-is-string? b (read-child-off b i 2))
          (str-byte-len b (read-child-off b i 2))
          (- 0 1))
  (if (and (dotted-method? b i b"Bytes" 5 b"concat" 6) (= (app-arity b i) 3))
      (let ((la (const-bytes-len b (read-child-off b i 2)))
            (lb (const-bytes-len b (read-child-off b i 3))))
        (if (and (>= la 0) (>= lb 0)) (+ la lb) (- 0 1)))                    ; both operands must fold
  (if (and (dotted-method? b i b"Bytes" 5 b"compact" 7) (= (app-arity b i) 2))
      (const-bytes-len b (read-child-off b i 2))                             ; compact preserves the value/length
  (if (slice-expect? b i)
      ; (Option.expect (Bytes.slice X start len) msg): the slice's len IF in bounds (0 ≤ start, 0 ≤ len,
      ; start+len ≤ len(X)); an out-of-bounds slice is None → Option.expect TRAPS, so NOT foldable → -1
      ; (mine declines → traps, matching native's trap).
      (let ((sl (read-child-off b i 2)))                                     ; the (Bytes.slice …) node
        (let ((xl (const-bytes-len b (read-child-off b sl 2)))
              (start (cbor-int-value b (read-child-off b sl 3)))
              (len (cbor-int-value b (read-child-off b sl 4))))
          (if (and (>= xl 0) (and (>= start 0) (and (>= len 0) (<= (+ start len) xl))))
              len
              (- 0 1))))
      (- 0 1)))))))

(def (slice-expect? b i)
  (doc "Is node i `(Option.expect (Bytes.slice X <const> <const>) <msg>)` — an Option.expect (arity 3) whose
        child 2 is a `(Bytes.slice X start len)` (arity 4) with CONST-int start & len? The foldable slice shape.")
  (if (and (dotted-method? b i b"Option" 6 b"expect" 6) (= (app-arity b i) 3))
      (let ((sl (read-child-off b i 2)))
        (if (and (dotted-method? b sl b"Bytes" 5 b"slice" 5) (= (app-arity b sl) 4))
            (and (node-is-const-int? b (read-child-off b sl 3)) (node-is-const-int? b (read-child-off b sl 4)))
            false))
      false))

(def (bytes-nth b i k)
  (doc "The k-th byte (0-based) of the foldable byte producer at node i. Recurses: `Bytes.of` → element k's
        const value; `String.to-bytes` → the literal's k-th payload byte; `Bytes.concat a b` → a's k-th byte
        if k < len(a), else b's (k-len(a))-th; `Bytes.compact x` → x's k-th; `(Option.expect (Bytes.slice X
        start len) …)` → X's (start+k)-th byte. Callers guarantee 0 ≤ k < len (via const-bytes-len).")
  (if (dotted-method? b i b"Bytes" 5 b"of" 2)
      (cbor-int-value b (read-child-off b (read-child-off b i 2) (+ k 1)))   ; element k of the (list …)
  (if (dotted-method? b i b"String" 6 b"to-bytes" 8)
      (byte-at b (+ (payload-off b (read-child-off b i 2)) k))               ; k-th UTF-8 byte of the string
  (if (dotted-method? b i b"Bytes" 5 b"concat" 6)
      (let ((la (const-bytes-len b (read-child-off b i 2))))
        (if (< k la)
            (bytes-nth b (read-child-off b i 2) k)
            (bytes-nth b (read-child-off b i 3) (- k la))))
  (if (slice-expect? b i)
      (let ((sl (read-child-off b i 2)))                                     ; slice: X's byte at start+k
        (bytes-nth b (read-child-off b sl 2) (+ (cbor-int-value b (read-child-off b sl 3)) k)))
      (bytes-nth b (read-child-off b i 2) k))))))                           ; Bytes.compact — pass through

(def (bytes-of-count b lst k end acc)
  (doc "Count the elements [k,end) of a `(Bytes.of (list …))` list, requiring each be a const int in 0..255.
        Returns the count, or -1 if any element is non-const or out of the byte range (native rejects those).")
  (if (< k end)
      (let ((eoff (read-child-off b lst k)))
        (if (and (node-is-const-int? b eoff)
                 (and (>= (cbor-int-value b eoff) 0) (<= (cbor-int-value b eoff) 255)))
            (bytes-of-count b lst (+ k 1) end (+ acc 1))
            (- 0 1)))                                                        ; out-of-range/non-const byte → not foldable
      acc))

(def (node-is-bytes-producer? b i)
  (doc "Is node i a FOLDABLE const byte producer — `(Bytes.of (list <0..255 const>))`, `(String.to-bytes
        \"…\")`, `(Bytes.concat a b)` over foldable operands, or `(Bytes.compact x)` over one? Detected by
        `const-bytes-len` ≥ 0 (it returns -1 for any non-foldable / out-of-range form). Gates the const
        Bytes-EQUALITY fold, which compares byte SEQUENCES via `bytes-nth`.")
  (>= (const-bytes-len b i) 0))

(def (const-bytes-eq? b i j)
  (doc "Are two foldable byte producers equal by byte SEQUENCE? Same length (via const-bytes-len) AND each
        byte equal (via bytes-nth, which recurses through concat/compact). Both must be
        `node-is-bytes-producer?` (caller checks).")
  (if (= (const-bytes-len b i) (const-bytes-len b j))
      (const-bytes-eq-go b i j 0 (const-bytes-len b i))
      false))

(def (const-bytes-eq-go b i j k n)
  (doc "Compare bytes [k,n) of the two byte producers via bytes-nth; all equal ⇒ true.")
  (if (< k n)
      (if (= (bytes-nth b i k) (bytes-nth b j k))
          (const-bytes-eq-go b i j (+ k 1) n)
          false)
      true))

; ─── const UTF-8 VALIDITY over a byte producer (ask-60): `(String.from-bytes P)` well-formedness ──
; `String.from-bytes` returns `(Some s)` iff P is WELL-FORMED UTF-8, else `None`. Native's validity is
; STRICT UTF-8 (RFC-3629 / WHATWG): rejects surrogates (ED A0..BF), overlongs (C0/C1, E0 80..9F, F0 80..8F),
; and > U+10FFFF (F4 90.., F5..FF). Verified byte-identical to Python strict decode over a 33-case battery.
; `const-utf8-valid?` walks a const byte producer via `bytes-nth`, implementing the standard length+range DFA.
; A continuation byte is 0x80..0xBF. Guards are AND-chains (short-circuit → no out-of-range `bytes-nth`).
(def (utf8-cont? c)
  (doc "Is byte c a UTF-8 continuation byte (0x80..0xBF)?")
  (and (>= c 0x80) (<= c 0xBF)))

(def (const-utf8-valid? b i)
  (doc "Is the const byte producer at i WELL-FORMED strict UTF-8? Walks by index from 0 to len.")
  (const-utf8-valid-go b i 0 (const-bytes-len b i)))

(def (const-utf8-valid-go b i k n)
  (doc "Validate producer i's bytes [k,n) as strict UTF-8. At each step read the lead byte c and consume a
        1–4 byte sequence per the RFC-3629 range table, then recurse past it. Any ill-formed prefix ⇒ false.
        Every continuation-byte access is guarded by a length check (`(< (+ k J) n)`) ANDed BEFORE the
        `bytes-nth`, so short-circuit prevents an out-of-range read on a truncated sequence.")
  (if (>= k n)
      true                                                        ; consumed all bytes → valid
      (let ((c (bytes-nth b i k)))
        (if (<= c 0x7F)
            (const-utf8-valid-go b i (+ k 1) n)                   ; ASCII 00..7F → 1 byte
        (if (and (>= c 0xC2) (<= c 0xDF))                         ; 2-byte C2..DF + 1 cont
            (if (and (< (+ k 1) n) (utf8-cont? (bytes-nth b i (+ k 1))))
                (const-utf8-valid-go b i (+ k 2) n) false)
        (if (= c 0xE0)                                            ; E0 + A0..BF + cont (no overlong)
            (if (and (and (< (+ k 2) n) (and (>= (bytes-nth b i (+ k 1)) 0xA0) (<= (bytes-nth b i (+ k 1)) 0xBF)))
                     (utf8-cont? (bytes-nth b i (+ k 2))))
                (const-utf8-valid-go b i (+ k 3) n) false)
        (if (and (>= c 0xE1) (<= c 0xEC))                         ; E1..EC + cont + cont
            (if (and (and (< (+ k 2) n) (utf8-cont? (bytes-nth b i (+ k 1)))) (utf8-cont? (bytes-nth b i (+ k 2))))
                (const-utf8-valid-go b i (+ k 3) n) false)
        (if (= c 0xED)                                            ; ED + 80..9F + cont (no surrogate)
            (if (and (and (< (+ k 2) n) (and (>= (bytes-nth b i (+ k 1)) 0x80) (<= (bytes-nth b i (+ k 1)) 0x9F)))
                     (utf8-cont? (bytes-nth b i (+ k 2))))
                (const-utf8-valid-go b i (+ k 3) n) false)
        (if (and (>= c 0xEE) (<= c 0xEF))                         ; EE..EF + cont + cont
            (if (and (and (< (+ k 2) n) (utf8-cont? (bytes-nth b i (+ k 1)))) (utf8-cont? (bytes-nth b i (+ k 2))))
                (const-utf8-valid-go b i (+ k 3) n) false)
        (if (= c 0xF0)                                            ; F0 + 90..BF + cont + cont (no overlong)
            (if (and (and (and (< (+ k 3) n) (and (>= (bytes-nth b i (+ k 1)) 0x90) (<= (bytes-nth b i (+ k 1)) 0xBF)))
                          (utf8-cont? (bytes-nth b i (+ k 2)))) (utf8-cont? (bytes-nth b i (+ k 3))))
                (const-utf8-valid-go b i (+ k 4) n) false)
        (if (and (>= c 0xF1) (<= c 0xF3))                         ; F1..F3 + cont + cont + cont
            (if (and (and (and (< (+ k 3) n) (utf8-cont? (bytes-nth b i (+ k 1))))
                          (utf8-cont? (bytes-nth b i (+ k 2)))) (utf8-cont? (bytes-nth b i (+ k 3))))
                (const-utf8-valid-go b i (+ k 4) n) false)
        (if (= c 0xF4)                                            ; F4 + 80..8F + cont + cont (≤ U+10FFFF)
            (if (and (and (and (< (+ k 3) n) (and (>= (bytes-nth b i (+ k 1)) 0x80) (<= (bytes-nth b i (+ k 1)) 0x8F)))
                          (utf8-cont? (bytes-nth b i (+ k 2)))) (utf8-cont? (bytes-nth b i (+ k 3))))
                (const-utf8-valid-go b i (+ k 4) n) false)
            false))))))))))))                                     ; 80..C1, F5..FF → ill-formed lead

; ─── byte-string VALUE rendering (ask-60): a const Bytes producer → its `b"…"` display ──
; A const Bytes VALUE result (`(Bytes.of (list 1 2 3))`, `(Bytes.concat …)`, a slice) renders as `b"` +
; escaped-bytes + `"`, matching native's byte-string display. Escaping (verified): printable ASCII 0x20–0x7E
; verbatim EXCEPT `"`→`\"` and `\`→`\\`; newline(10)→`\n`, tab(9)→`\t`, CR(13)→`\r`, 0→`\0`; every OTHER byte
; (non-printable, 0x7F, 0x80–0xFF) → `\xNN` (lowercase 2-digit hex). Walks the byte producer via bytes-nth.
(def (hex-digit-lc n)
  (doc "The lowercase-hex ASCII byte for nibble n (0..15): 0-9 → '0'-'9', 10-15 → 'a'-'f'.")
  (if (< n 10) (+ 48 n) (+ 87 n)))                                       ; 87+10='a'(97)

(def (render-byte b c)
  (doc "The display bytes for ONE byte value c in a byte-string: the escaped/verbatim/`\\xNN` form.")
  (if (= c 92)  (Bytes.of (list 92 92))                                  ; \  → \\
  (if (= c 34)  (Bytes.of (list 92 34))                                  ; "  → \"
  (if (= c 10)  (Bytes.of (list 92 110))                                 ; LF → \n
  (if (= c 9)   (Bytes.of (list 92 116))                                 ; TAB→ \t
  (if (= c 13)  (Bytes.of (list 92 114))                                 ; CR → \r
  (if (= c 0)   (Bytes.of (list 92 48))                                  ; 0  → \0
  (if (and (>= c 32) (<= c 126)) (u8 c)                                  ; printable ASCII verbatim
      (Bytes.of (list 92 120 (hex-digit-lc (>> c 4)) (hex-digit-lc (& c 15)))))))))))) ; \xNN

(def (render-bytes-go b i k n out)
  (doc "Append the display bytes of byte-producer i's bytes [k,n) to `out`.")
  (if (< k n)
      (render-bytes-go b i (+ k 1) n (cat out (render-byte b (bytes-nth b i k))))
      out))

(def (render-byte-string b i)
  (doc "Render a const Bytes producer at i to its `b\"…\"` display bytes. Byte-identical to native.")
  (cat (Bytes.of (list 98 34))                                          ; `b"`
       (cat (render-bytes-go b i 0 (const-bytes-len b i) (empty-bytes)) (u8 34))))  ; …escaped… + `"`

; ─── const Bytes.at VALUE output (ask-60): `(Bytes.at <producer> <k>)` → (Some N) | (None unit) ──
; `(Bytes.at b k)` reads byte k of a Bytes, returning `Option<Int>`: in-range (0 ≤ k < len) → `(Some
; <byte>)`, else `(None unit)`. On a const byte producer + const index this folds to a const Option VALUE,
; displayed as `(Some N)` / `(None unit)` — reusing the ctor-value display form. AST: `(apply (. Bytes at)
; <producer> <k>)` (arity 3). No heap needed at compile time — the byte value is read via bytes-nth.
(def (bytes-at? b i)
  (doc "Is node i a foldable `(Bytes.at <producer> <const-int-k>)` — a `(. Bytes at)` dotted method (arity 3)
        whose byte arg is a foldable producer and whose index is a const int?")
  (if (and (dotted-method? b i b"Bytes" 5 b"at" 2) (= (app-arity b i) 3))
      (and (node-is-bytes-producer? b (read-child-off b i 2))
           (node-is-const-int? b (read-child-off b i 3)))
      false))

(def (render-bytes-at b i)
  (doc "Render `(Bytes.at producer k)` to its Option display: in-range → `(Some <byte>)`, out-of-range →
        `(None unit)`. `render-bytes-at` is only called when `bytes-at?` holds (producer + const index).")
  (let ((k (cbor-int-value b (read-child-off b i 3)))
        (n (const-bytes-len b (read-child-off b i 2))))
    (if (and (>= k 0) (< k n))
        (cat (Bytes.of (list 40 83 111 109 101 32))                     ; "(Some "
             (cat (int-to-decimal (bytes-nth b (read-child-off b i 2) k)) (u8 41)))  ; <byte> + ")"
        (Bytes.of (list 40 78 111 110 101 32 117 110 105 116 41)))))    ; "(None unit)"

; ─── const Bytes.slice VALUE output (ask-60): `(Bytes.slice <producer> <s> <n>)` → (Some b"…") | (None unit) ──
; A BARE `(Bytes.slice b start len)` returns `Option<Bytes>`: in-bounds (0 ≤ start, 0 ≤ len, start+len ≤
; len(b)) → `(Some b"…")` over the sub-range, else `(None unit)`. (Distinct from `(Option.expect (Bytes.slice
; …))` which UNWRAPS to the Bytes and traps on None — handled by `slice-expect?`.) AST: `(apply (. Bytes slice)
; <producer> <start> <len>)` (arity 4). Renders via the sub-range byte walk.
(def (bytes-slice? b i)
  (doc "Is node i a foldable BARE `(Bytes.slice <producer> <const start> <const len>)` — `(. Bytes slice)`
        dotted method (arity 4) over a foldable byte producer with const int start & len?")
  (if (and (dotted-method? b i b"Bytes" 5 b"slice" 5) (= (app-arity b i) 4))
      (and (node-is-bytes-producer? b (read-child-off b i 2))
           (and (node-is-const-int? b (read-child-off b i 3)) (node-is-const-int? b (read-child-off b i 4))))
      false))

(def (render-bytes-range b p start len out)
  (doc "Render bytes [start, start+len) of producer p as escaped byte-string content into `out` (via
        render-byte + bytes-nth). Used for a slice's `b\"…\"` sub-range display.")
  (if (< len 1)
      out
      (render-bytes-range b p (+ start 1) (- len 1) (cat out (render-byte b (bytes-nth b p start))))))

(def (render-bytes-slice b i)
  (doc "Render a bare `(Bytes.slice producer start len)` to its Option display: in-bounds → `(Some b\"…\")`
        over the sub-range, out-of-bounds → `(None unit)`. Only called when `bytes-slice?` holds.")
  (let ((p (read-child-off b i 2))
        (start (cbor-int-value b (read-child-off b i 3)))
        (len (cbor-int-value b (read-child-off b i 4))))
    (if (and (>= start 0) (and (>= len 0) (<= (+ start len) (const-bytes-len b p))))
        (cat (Bytes.of (list 40 83 111 109 101 32 98 34))               ; `(Some b"`
             (cat (render-bytes-range b p start len (empty-bytes)) (Bytes.of (list 34 41))))  ; …escaped… + `")`
        (Bytes.of (list 40 78 111 110 101 32 117 110 105 116 41)))))    ; "(None unit)"

; ─── const String.at / String.slice VALUE output (ask-60): Option<String> by SCALAR index ──
; `(String.at s k)` → the k-th Unicode SCALAR as a 1-char String: `Option<Char>` displayed as `(Some "…")`
; (in-range 0 ≤ k < scalar-count) else `(None unit)`. `(String.slice s START END)` → the substring of scalars
; [START, END) as `Option<String>`: `(Some "…")` iff 0 ≤ START ≤ END ≤ scalar-count, else `(None unit)`.
; ⚠ String.slice's args are (start, END) HALF-OPEN — NOT (start, len) like Bytes.slice. Both index by SCALAR,
; so map scalar index → byte offset by walking UTF-8 lead bytes. LITERALS only (corpus cases are all literals;
; a String.concat arg would need scalar walking over the producer — deferred). AST: `(apply (. String at) s k)`
; / `(apply (. String slice) s start end)`.
(def (str-scalar-offset b e k)
  (doc "The BYTE offset (into text-literal e's payload) where Unicode scalar k STARTS — i.e. the position of
        the (k+1)-th UTF-8 LEAD byte (`(byte & 0xC0) != 0x80`). `k`=0 → 0; `k`=scalar-count → the payload
        byte length (one past the end). Assumes 0 ≤ k ≤ scalar-count (caller bounds). Counts lead bytes; when
        `leads` reaches `k` at a lead byte, that byte position is scalar k's start.")
  (if (< k 1)
      0
      (str-scalar-offset-go b e 0 0 k (entry-len b e))))

(def (str-scalar-offset-go b e bytepos leads k n)
  (doc "Walk bytes [bytepos,n) of text e counting LEAD bytes; return the position of the k-th lead byte (the
        start of scalar k). k lead bytes already fully passed ⇒ this position. Runs to n for k=scalar-count.")
  (if (< bytepos n)
      (if (= (& (byte-at b (+ (payload-off b e) bytepos)) 0xC0) 0x80)
          (str-scalar-offset-go b e (+ bytepos 1) leads k n)           ; continuation byte — skip, don't count
          (if (= leads k)
              bytepos                                                  ; the k-th lead byte — scalar k starts here
              (str-scalar-offset-go b e (+ bytepos 1) (+ leads 1) k n)))
      bytepos))                                                        ; end reached (k = scalar-count)

(def (render-str-lit-range b e bstart bend out)
  (doc "Render text-literal e's payload bytes [bstart,bend) as escaped STRING content into `out`, via
        str-escape-byte. (Byte range = a scalar-aligned substring; the bytes are valid UTF-8.)")
  (if (< bstart bend)
      (render-str-lit-range b e (+ bstart 1) bend (cat out (str-escape-byte (byte-at b (+ (payload-off b e) bstart)))))
      out))

(def (string-at? b i)
  (doc "Is node i a foldable `(String.at <string-literal> <const k>)` — `(. String at)` dotted method
        (arity 3) over a string LITERAL with a const int index?")
  (if (and (dotted-method? b i b"String" 6 b"at" 2) (= (app-arity b i) 3))
      (and (node-is-string? b (read-child-off b i 2)) (node-is-const-int? b (read-child-off b i 3)))
      false))

(def (render-string-at b i)
  (doc "Render `(String.at s k)` → `(Some \"<scalar k>\")` if 0 ≤ k < scalar-count, else `(None unit)`. The
        scalar is the bytes [offset(k), offset(k+1)); rendered as a quoted string (native shows a Char as
        `(Some \"…\")`).")
  (let ((s (read-child-off b i 2))
        (k (cbor-int-value b (read-child-off b i 3))))
    (if (and (>= k 0) (< k (str-scalar-len b s)))
        (cat (Bytes.of (list 40 83 111 109 101 32 34))                  ; `(Some "`
             (cat (render-str-lit-range b s (str-scalar-offset b s k) (str-scalar-offset b s (+ k 1)) (empty-bytes))
                  (Bytes.of (list 34 41))))                             ; …escaped… + `")`
        (Bytes.of (list 40 78 111 110 101 32 117 110 105 116 41)))))    ; "(None unit)"

(def (string-slice? b i)
  (doc "Is node i a foldable `(String.slice <string-literal> <const start> <const end>)` — `(. String slice)`
        dotted method (arity 4) over a string LITERAL with const int start & end?")
  (if (and (dotted-method? b i b"String" 6 b"slice" 5) (= (app-arity b i) 4))
      (and (node-is-string? b (read-child-off b i 2))
           (and (node-is-const-int? b (read-child-off b i 3)) (node-is-const-int? b (read-child-off b i 4))))
      false))

(def (render-string-slice b i)
  (doc "Render `(String.slice s start end)` → `(Some \"…\")` for scalars [start,end) if 0 ≤ start ≤ end ≤
        scalar-count, else `(None unit)`. ⚠ args are (start, END), half-open — NOT (start, len).")
  (let ((s (read-child-off b i 2))
        (start (cbor-int-value b (read-child-off b i 3)))
        (end (cbor-int-value b (read-child-off b i 4))))
    (if (and (>= start 0) (and (<= start end) (<= end (str-scalar-len b s))))
        (cat (Bytes.of (list 40 83 111 109 101 32 34))                  ; `(Some "`
             (cat (render-str-lit-range b s (str-scalar-offset b s start) (str-scalar-offset b s end) (empty-bytes))
                  (Bytes.of (list 34 41))))                             ; …escaped… + `")`
        (Bytes.of (list 40 78 111 110 101 32 117 110 105 116 41)))))    ; "(None unit)"

(def (str-byte-renderable? c)
  (doc "Is byte c safe to render via the closed escape set / verbatim? The 5 escapables (\\ \" \\n \\r \\t)
        are fine; a printable/UTF-8 byte (≥0x20) is fine; a C0 control other than \\n\\r\\t (needs `\\u{}`)
        is NOT — decline those.")
  (if (= c 9) true                                    ; tab
  (if (= c 10) true                                   ; newline
  (if (= c 13) true                                   ; CR
  (>= c 32)))))                                        ; printable/UTF-8; C0 controls <32 (≠9/10/13) → false

(def (str-all-renderable? b e j n)
  (doc "Are all bytes [j,n) of the text at offset e renderable (no `\\u{}`-only control byte)?")
  (if (< j n)
      (if (str-byte-renderable? (byte-at b (+ (payload-off b e) j)))
          (str-all-renderable? b e (+ j 1) n)
          false)
      true))

(def (render-string-bytes b e j n out)
  (doc "Append the escaped display bytes of text at offset e [j,n) to `out`: `\\`→`\\\\`, `\"`→`\\\"`,
        newline→`\\n`, CR→`\\r`, tab→`\\t`, else the byte verbatim (printable/UTF-8). Callers ensure every
        byte is renderable (str-all-renderable?).")
  (if (< j n)
      (let ((c (byte-at b (+ (payload-off b e) j))))
        (render-string-bytes b e (+ j 1) n
          (if (= c 92)  (cat out (Bytes.of (list 92 92)))                ; \  → \\
          (if (= c 34)  (cat out (Bytes.of (list 92 34)))                ; "  → \"
          (if (= c 10)  (cat out (Bytes.of (list 92 110)))               ; LF → \n
          (if (= c 13)  (cat out (Bytes.of (list 92 114)))               ; CR → \r
          (if (= c 9)   (cat out (Bytes.of (list 92 116)))               ; TAB→ \t
          (cat out (u8 c)))))))))                                        ; verbatim
      out))

(def (render-string b i)
  (doc "Render a CONST string literal at i to its display bytes: `\"` + escaped-contents + `\"`. Matches
        native's string display exactly for the closed escape set + printable/UTF-8 bytes.")
  (cat (u8 34) (cat (render-string-bytes b i 0 (entry-len b i) (empty-bytes)) (u8 34))))

(def (str-escape-byte c)
  (doc "The display bytes for ONE string byte c: the closed escape set (`\\`→`\\\\`, `\"`→`\\\"`, LF→`\\n`,
        CR→`\\r`, TAB→`\\t`) else the byte verbatim (printable/UTF-8). Shared by the literal and producer
        string renderers.")
  (if (= c 92)  (Bytes.of (list 92 92))
  (if (= c 34)  (Bytes.of (list 92 34))
  (if (= c 10)  (Bytes.of (list 92 110))
  (if (= c 13)  (Bytes.of (list 92 114))
  (if (= c 9)   (Bytes.of (list 92 116))
      (u8 c)))))))

(def (str-prod-all-renderable? b i k n)
  (doc "Are all bytes [k,n) of string producer i renderable via the closed escape set (no `\\u{}`-only C0
        control byte, i.e. every byte is 9/10/13 or ≥32)? Uses str-prod-nth so it works over concat.")
  (if (< k n)
      (if (str-byte-renderable? (str-prod-nth b i k))
          (str-prod-all-renderable? b i (+ k 1) n)
          false)
      true))

(def (render-str-prod b i)
  (doc "Render a string PRODUCER (literal or `String.concat` chain) to its display bytes `\"…escaped…\"`,
        walking via str-prod-nth. Callers ensure all bytes renderable (str-prod-all-renderable?).")
  (cat (u8 34) (cat (render-str-prod-go b i 0 (str-prod-len b i) (empty-bytes)) (u8 34))))

(def (render-str-prod-go b i k n out)
  (doc "Append escaped display bytes of producer i's bytes [k,n) to `out` via str-escape-byte.")
  (if (< k n)
      (render-str-prod-go b i (+ k 1) n (cat out (str-escape-byte (str-prod-nth b i k))))
      out))

; ─── render-ast: a CONSTANT value's AST → its display string (ask-60 heap types) ─
; The `display` of a runtime-compound VALUE is built by rendering its parts. This
; renders a CONSTANT value's AST bytes to the exact string native's renderer produces
; — the compile-time half (the const-compound tier). Format (verified against native):
;   int/negint → decimal ASCII (int-to-decimal);  bool → "true"/"false";
;   (tuple e…)/(list e…) → "(" head " " <elems space-joined> ")"  (so `(list)` → "(list )");
;   (record (f v)…)      → "(record " <"(f " render(v) ")" space-joined> ")".
; A nested compound recurses. A non-const / unsupported node → an empty rendering
; (callers only invoke this on a value they've confirmed const — decline otherwise).
(def (entry-bytes b e j n out)
  (doc "Append prelude symbol `e`'s bytes [j,n) to `out` — copies a head/field NAME into the display.")
  (if (< j n)
      (entry-bytes b e (+ j 1) n (cat out (u8 (entry-byte b e j))))
      out))

(def (head-name-bytes b i)
  (doc "The head NAME bytes of application node at i (e.g. b\"tuple\") — for emitting it in the display.")
  (let ((e (prelude-entry b (read-head-index b i))))
    (entry-bytes b e 0 (entry-len b e) (empty-bytes))))

(def (render-ast b i)
  (doc "Render the CONSTANT value at offset i to its display bytes. A COMPOUND literal (`(tuple/list/record …)`)
        renders structurally (recursing into elements); ANY OTHER node is a scalar EXPRESSION — resolve+fold it
        to a const scalar and render that (so a literal `1`, an arithmetic `(+ 1 1)`→2, a comparison `(< 1 2)`→
        true all render as their constant, matching native's fold-then-display). ONLY call when `render-ok?`
        holds (every scalar sub-node folds to a const); a non-const sub-node renders empty.")
  (if (>= (let-passthru-off b i) 0)
      (render-ast b (let-passthru-off b i))                              ; (let ((v V)) v) → render V
  (if (node-is-unit? b i)
      (Bytes.of (list 117 110 105 116))                                  ; a `unit` leaf → "unit"
  (if (node-is-str-producer? b i)
      (render-str-prod b i)                                              ; a string literal / String.concat → "…escaped…"
  (if (node-is-bytes-producer? b i)
      (render-byte-string b i)                                           ; a const Bytes producer → b"…escaped…"
  (if (bytes-at? b i)
      (render-bytes-at b i)                                              ; (Bytes.at p k) → (Some N) | (None unit)
  (if (bytes-slice? b i)
      (render-bytes-slice b i)                                           ; (Bytes.slice p s n) → (Some b"…") | (None unit)
  (if (string-at? b i)
      (render-string-at b i)                                             ; (String.at s k) → (Some "…") | (None unit)
  (if (string-slice? b i)
      (render-string-slice b i)                                          ; (String.slice s a b) → (Some "…") | (None unit)
  (if (dotted-ctor-unit? b i)
      (render-dotted-ctor-unit b i)                                      ; (Sign.Zero unit) → (Sign.Zero unit)
  (if (body-is-compound-head b i)
      (if (node-head-is b i b"record" 6)
          (render-fields b i (Bytes.of (list 40 114 101 99 111 114 100 32)) 1 (+ (app-arity b i) 1))  ; "(record "
          (render-elems b i (cat (u8 40) (cat (head-name-bytes b i) (u8 32))) 1 (+ (app-arity b i) 1)))  ; "(head " …  (tuple/list/CTOR)
      (render-const-scalar b i))))))))))))                               ; a scalar expression → fold + render its const

(def (render-const-scalar b i)
  (doc "Render a scalar node at i by resolving+folding it to a const: a folded KConst → decimal, a folded KBoolC
        → true/false. `render-ok?` guarantees it folds to a const scalar (else caller declined). A non-const fold
        → empty (defensive).")
  (let ((c (fold (resolve (read-node b i (list) (list) (list))))))
    (if (const? c)
        (if (scrut-is-bool-const c)
            (if (= (const-val c) 0) (Bytes.of (list 102 97 108 115 101)) (Bytes.of (list 116 114 117 101)))  ; false/true
            (int-to-decimal (const-val c)))
        (empty-bytes))))

; Two concrete sequence renderers (no higher-order arg — a function-valued parameter overflows the seed's
; monomorphizer here). `pre` already ends in the opening space; the FIRST element follows it directly, later
; elements get a leading ' '; empty → `pre` + ')' (so `(list)` → "(list )"). `render-elems` = tuple/list values;
; `render-fields` = record `(fieldname value)` entries.
(def (render-elems b i pre k end)
  (doc "Render tuple/list elements k..end of node i, space-joined, prefixed by `pre`, closed by ')'.")
  (if (< k end)
      (render-elems-go b i (cat pre (render-ast b (read-child-off b i k))) (+ k 1) end)
      (cat pre (u8 41))))
(def (render-elems-go b i acc k end)
  (if (< k end)
      (render-elems-go b i (cat acc (cat (u8 32) (render-ast b (read-child-off b i k)))) (+ k 1) end)
      (cat acc (u8 41))))

(def (render-field-entry b eoff)
  (doc "Render one record field entry `(fieldname value)` at eoff → \"(fieldname value)\".")
  (cat (u8 40)
       (cat (head-name-bytes b eoff)
            (cat (u8 32)
                 (cat (render-ast b (read-child-off b eoff 1)) (u8 41))))))
(def (render-fields b i pre k end)
  (doc "Render record field entries k..end of node i, space-joined, prefixed by `pre`, closed by ')'.")
  (if (< k end)
      (render-fields-go b i (cat pre (render-field-entry b (read-child-off b i k))) (+ k 1) end)
      (cat pre (u8 41))))
(def (render-fields-go b i acc k end)
  (if (< k end)
      (render-fields-go b i (cat acc (cat (u8 32) (render-field-entry b (read-child-off b i k)))) (+ k 1) end)
      (cat acc (u8 41))))

; ─── render-ok?: is the value at i FULLY LITERAL (safe for render-ast)? ──────
; `render-ast` reads the RAW AST, so it only renders correctly a value whose leaves
; are LITERAL ints/bools (not `(+ 1 1)` — native folds that to `2`, but render-ast
; would mis-read the application). A fully-literal compound is: an int/bool leaf, or
; a `(tuple/list …)` whose elements are all render-ok, or a `(record (f v)…)` whose
; VALUES are all render-ok. Anything else (arithmetic, a name, a call, a float, an
; unsupported head) → not render-ok → the caller DECLINES (a safe under-decline: a
; foldable-element compound like `(tuple (+ 1 1) 2)` declines rather than mis-renders).
(def (render-ok? b i)
  (doc "Is the value at offset i safe for render-ast? Cases: a `unit` leaf → ok (renders \"unit\"); a CONSTRUCTOR
        value `(Some …)`/`(None unit)` → ok iff EMIT-SAFE (unit payload for any capitalized head, else a
        WHITELIST head with arity 1) AND its payload is render-ok; a `record` → all field-values ok; a `list`
        → all elements ok AND no element is a constructor (native requires a homogeneous element TYPE we don't
        check — under-decline a ctor-in-list); a `tuple` → all elements ok (heterogeneous ctors fine); ANY
        OTHER node → ok iff it FOLDS to a const scalar. Non-ok → caller DECLINES (safe under-decline).")
  (if (>= (let-passthru-off b i) 0)
      (render-ok? b (let-passthru-off b i))                                       ; (let ((v V)) v) ok iff V render-ok
  (if (node-is-unit? b i)
      true
  (if (node-is-str-producer? b i)
      (str-prod-all-renderable? b i 0 (str-prod-len b i))                        ; string/concat ok iff no `\u{}`-only byte
  (if (node-is-bytes-producer? b i)
      true                                                                       ; a const Bytes producer renders as b"…" (every byte escapable)
  (if (bytes-at? b i)
      true                                                                       ; (Bytes.at p k) → (Some N)/(None unit), always renderable
  (if (bytes-slice? b i)
      true                                                                       ; (Bytes.slice p s n) → (Some b"…")/(None unit), always renderable
  (if (string-at? b i)
      (str-all-renderable? b (read-child-off b i 2) 0 (entry-len b (read-child-off b i 2)))   ; ok iff the string has no `\u{}`-only byte
  (if (string-slice? b i)
      (str-all-renderable? b (read-child-off b i 2) 0 (entry-len b (read-child-off b i 2)))
  (if (dotted-ctor-unit? b i)
      true                                                                       ; (Recv.Variant unit) always renderable
  (if (head-is-ctor? b i)
      (if (ctor-emit-safe? b i)
          (render-ok-elems b i 1 (+ (app-arity b i) 1))                          ; payload(s) must be render-ok
          false)
  (if (node-head-is b i b"record" 6)
      (render-ok-fields b i 1 (+ (app-arity b i) 1))
  (if (node-head-is b i b"list" 4)
      (if (list-has-ctor-elem? b i 1 (+ (app-arity b i) 1))
          false                                                                  ; ctor-in-list: type-homogeneity we can't verify → decline
          (render-ok-elems b i 1 (+ (app-arity b i) 1)))
  (if (node-head-is b i b"tuple" 5)
      (render-ok-elems b i 1 (+ (app-arity b i) 1))
      (folds-to-const-scalar b i)))))))))))))))                                  ; scalar expr must fold to a const

(def (ctor-emit-safe? b i)
  (doc "May we EMIT this constructor value (a strict subset of what native renders)? A UNIT payload is safe for
        ANY capitalized head (native always renders `(Head unit)`, including nullary None/Zero/…). A NON-unit
        payload is safe only for a WHITELIST head (Some/Ok/Err/Left/Right/Just) at ARITY 1 — an unknown
        capitalized head with a non-unit payload might be a nullary ctor native DECLINES, so we under-decline.")
  (if (= (app-arity b i) 1)
      (if (node-is-unit? b (read-child-off b i 1))
          true
          (head-in-ctor-whitelist? b i))
      false))                                                                    ; arity ≠ 1 → not a value we emit

(def (list-has-ctor-elem? b i k end)
  (doc "Does any list element k..end have a constructor head? Used to DECLINE a `list` containing ctor values —
        native requires a homogeneous element type across the list, a unification we don't perform.")
  (if (< k end)
      (if (head-is-ctor? b (read-child-off b i k)) true (list-has-ctor-elem? b i (+ k 1) end))
      false))

(def (folds-to-const-scalar b i)
  (doc "Does the scalar node at i resolve+fold to a const scalar (KConst/KBoolC)? — the leaf renderability test.
        A literal folds to itself; `(+ 1 1)` → KConst 2; a name/call/runtime/float does NOT → false.")
  (const? (fold (resolve (read-node b i (list) (list) (list))))))
(def (render-ok-elems b i k end)
  (doc "Are all tuple/list elements k..end render-ok?")
  (if (< k end)
      (if (render-ok? b (read-child-off b i k)) (render-ok-elems b i (+ k 1) end) false)
      true))
(def (render-ok-fields b i k end)
  (doc "Are all record field VALUES (child 1 of each entry) k..end render-ok? Each entry `(fieldname value)`
        encodes as `[name-idx, value]` — head = field name, ONE operand = the value, so `app-arity` = 1. A
        malformed entry (not an array, or not arity 1) → not ok.")
  (if (< k end)
      (let ((eoff (read-child-off b i k)))
        (if (and (= (cbor-major b eoff) 4) (= (app-arity b eoff) 1))
            (if (render-ok? b (read-child-off b eoff 1)) (render-ok-fields b i (+ k 1) end) false)
            false))
      true))

; ─── compound-value component emission (ask-60 heap types, const tier) ──────
; A CONST compound VALUE (e.g. `(tuple 1 2)`) compiles NOT to the scalar `run:()→i64`
; envelope but to a RESOURCE-WITH-DISPLAY component: exports make/display/cabi_realloc/
; memory, imports the heap runtime `intr.new`; `display` writes the RENDERED string
; (render-ast) into memory and returns a [ptr,len] descriptor. Recipe VERIFIED
; byte-identical to native: fixed core-module head + fixed realloc/make bodies +
; GENERATED display body (per-char i32.store8 over the rendered string) + fixed
; component wrap. The three fixed blobs are transcribed from native's emission.
(def (compound-corehead)
  (doc "Fixed core-module head: magic + type/import/func/memory/global/export sections (before the code
        section). Independent of the value — 106 bytes verbatim from native's compound component.")
  (Bytes.of (list 0 97 115 109 1 0 0 0 1 18 3 96 1 127 1 127 96 4 127 127 127 127 1 127 96 0 1 127 2 12 1 4 105 110 116 114 3 110 101 119 0 0 3 4 3 1 2 0 5 3 1 0 1 6 7 1 127 1 65 192 0 11 7 42 4 6 109 101 109 111 114 121 2 0 12 99 97 98 105 95 114 101 97 108 108 111 99 0 1 4 109 97 107 101 0 2 7 100 105 115 112 108 97 121 0 3)))
(def (compound-codeprefix)
  (doc "Fixed code-section prefix: the `cabi_realloc` and `make` func entries (25 bytes), before the
        generated `display` entry. Verbatim from native.")
  (Bytes.of (list 17 1 1 127 35 0 33 4 35 0 32 3 106 36 0 32 4 11 6 0 65 0 16 0 11)))
(def (compound-wrap)
  (doc "Fixed component-level wrap (427 bytes): resource type, canon resource.new, canon lift(memory,realloc),
        the nested sub-component with the resource import/export, and exports. Independent of the value.")
  (Bytes.of (list 7 4 1 63 127 0 8 3 1 2 0 2 19 2 1 1 3 110 101 119 0 0 0 0 1 4 105 110 116 114 18 0 7 7 2 105 0 64 0 0 1 6 10 1 0 0 1 1 4 109 97 107 101 8 6 1 0 0 1 0 2 7 13 2 104 0 64 1 4 115 101 108 102 3 0 115 6 41 3 0 0 1 1 7 100 105 115 112 108 97 121 0 2 1 1 6 109 101 109 111 114 121 0 0 1 1 12 99 97 98 105 95 114 101 97 108 108 111 99 8 10 1 0 0 2 2 3 0 4 3 4 4 195 1 0 97 115 109 13 0 1 0 10 22 1 0 17 105 109 112 111 114 116 45 116 121 112 101 45 118 97 108 117 101 3 1 7 13 2 104 0 64 1 4 115 101 108 102 1 0 115 10 32 1 0 27 105 109 112 111 114 116 45 109 101 116 104 111 100 45 118 97 108 117 101 45 100 105 115 112 108 97 121 1 2 7 7 2 105 0 64 0 0 3 10 21 1 0 16 105 109 112 111 114 116 45 102 117 110 99 45 109 97 107 101 1 4 11 11 1 0 5 118 97 108 117 101 3 0 0 7 13 2 104 5 64 1 4 115 101 108 102 6 0 115 11 29 1 0 21 91 109 101 116 104 111 100 93 118 97 108 117 101 46 100 105 115 112 108 97 121 1 0 1 1 7 7 7 2 105 5 64 0 0 8 11 12 1 0 4 109 97 107 101 1 1 1 1 9 5 73 1 0 0 3 17 105 109 112 111 114 116 45 116 121 112 101 45 118 97 108 117 101 3 0 27 105 109 112 111 114 116 45 109 101 116 104 111 100 45 118 97 108 117 101 45 100 105 115 112 108 97 121 1 1 16 105 109 112 111 114 116 45 102 117 110 99 45 109 97 107 101 1 0 11 21 1 0 15 99 97 100 101 110 122 97 58 114 117 110 47 114 117 110 5 0 0)))

(def (display-char-stores s i n)
  (doc "The `display` per-char body: for each byte of the rendered string `s` (0..n), emit `i32.const (8+i);
        i32.const <char>; i32.store8` = [0x41 sleb(8+i) 0x41 sleb(char) 0x3a 0x00 0x00]. The string is written
        at memory offset 8.")
  (if (< i n)
      (cat (cat (u8 0x41) (cat (sleb (+ 8 i)) (cat (u8 0x41) (cat (sleb (byte-at s i)) (Bytes.of (list 0x3a 0x00 0x00))))))
           (display-char-stores s (+ i 1) n))
      (empty-bytes)))

(def (display-body s)
  (doc "The full `display` func code entry (size-prefixed, 0 locals): the per-char stores over `s`, then store
        the descriptor [ptr=8 @0, len @4], then `i32.const 0; end`. Matches native's display func byte-for-byte.")
  (let ((body (cat (display-char-stores s 0 (Bytes.len s))
                   (cat (Bytes.of (list 0x41 0x00 0x41 0x08 0x36 0x02 0x00))       ; i32.store ptr=8 @ addr 0
                        (cat (cat (Bytes.of (list 0x41 0x04 0x41)) (cat (sleb (Bytes.len s)) (Bytes.of (list 0x36 0x02 0x00))))  ; store len @ addr 4
                             (Bytes.of (list 0x41 0x00 0x0b)))))))                 ; i32.const 0; end
    (sized (cat (u8 0x00) body))))                                                 ; 0 locals, then size-prefixed

(def (compound-component s)
  (doc "Assemble a resource-with-display component whose value renders to string `s`. Recipe (VERIFIED
        byte-identical to native): core-module = corehead ++ code-section(3 funcs: realloc,make,display(s));
        component = magic ++ section1(sized core-module) ++ fixed wrap.")
  (let ((codesec (section 0x0A (cat (u8 0x03) (cat (compound-codeprefix) (display-body s)))))
        (magic (Bytes.of (list 0x00 0x61 0x73 0x6D 0x0D 0x00 0x01 0x00))))
    (let ((core (cat (compound-corehead) codesec)))
      (cat magic (cat (cat (u8 0x01) (sized core)) (compound-wrap))))))

; ─── heap-list emission (ask-60 heap types, TIER-2 stage 1: runtime int-list) ──
; A runtime int-LIST value (`(List.push (list) 5)` — a list built by push, NOT a const
; `(list ...)` literal which folds) compiles to a HEAP-IMPORTING component: it EXPORTS `run`
; returning an opaque u32 heap handle and IMPORTS the value-heap runtime ops (box-int 0,
; arr-* 6-9, sum-new 10, vec-empty 24, vec-push 27, ...). Shape is DIFFERENT from the const
; resource-with-display tier. The envelope is SIX fixed blobs (transcribed byte-identical
; from native) with ONE generated body -- `run`, which builds the list:
;   vec-empty ; per element [i64.const e ; box-int ; vec-push] ; end
; Verified byte-identical to native for [5],[7,8],[1,2,3,4],[100,-3,0].
(def (hl-prefix)   (doc "Fixed component graph + core-module header (1983 B).") (Bytes.of (list 0 97 115 109 13 0 1 0 7 248 7 1 66 82 1 64 1 2 97 48 120 0 121 4 0 7 98 111 120 45 105 110 116 1 0 1 64 1 2 97 48 121 0 120 4 0 7 103 101 116 45 105 110 116 1 1 1 64 1 2 97 48 127 0 121 4 0 8 98 111 120 45 98 111 111 108 1 2 1 64 1 2 97 48 121 0 127 4 0 8 103 101 116 45 98 111 111 108 1 3 1 64 1 2 97 48 117 0 121 4 0 9 98 111 120 45 102 108 111 97 116 1 4 1 64 1 2 97 48 121 0 117 4 0 9 103 101 116 45 102 108 111 97 116 1 5 1 64 1 2 97 48 121 0 121 4 0 9 97 114 114 45 97 108 108 111 99 1 6 1 64 3 2 97 48 121 2 97 49 121 2 97 50 121 0 121 4 0 7 97 114 114 45 115 101 116 1 7 1 64 2 2 97 48 121 2 97 49 121 0 121 4 0 7 97 114 114 45 103 101 116 1 8 1 64 1 2 97 48 121 0 121 4 0 7 97 114 114 45 108 101 110 1 9 1 64 2 2 97 48 121 2 97 49 121 0 121 4 0 7 115 117 109 45 110 101 119 1 10 1 64 1 2 97 48 121 0 121 4 0 8 115 117 109 45 100 105 115 99 1 11 1 64 1 2 97 48 121 0 121 4 0 11 115 117 109 45 112 97 121 108 111 97 100 1 12 1 64 1 2 97 48 121 0 121 4 0 11 98 121 116 101 115 45 97 108 108 111 99 1 13 1 64 3 2 97 48 121 2 97 49 121 2 97 50 121 0 121 4 0 9 98 121 116 101 115 45 115 101 116 1 14 1 64 2 2 97 48 121 2 97 49 121 0 121 4 0 9 98 121 116 101 115 45 103 101 116 1 15 1 64 1 2 97 48 121 0 121 4 0 9 98 121 116 101 115 45 108 101 110 1 16 1 64 1 2 97 48 121 1 0 4 0 3 100 117 112 1 17 1 64 1 2 97 48 121 1 0 4 0 4 100 114 111 112 1 18 1 64 1 2 97 48 121 0 121 4 0 9 109 97 112 45 97 108 108 111 99 1 19 1 64 4 2 97 48 121 2 97 49 121 2 97 50 121 2 97 51 121 0 121 4 0 7 109 97 112 45 115 101 116 1 20 1 64 2 2 97 48 121 2 97 49 121 0 121 4 0 7 109 97 112 45 107 101 121 1 21 1 64 2 2 97 48 121 2 97 49 121 0 121 4 0 7 109 97 112 45 118 97 108 1 22 1 64 1 2 97 48 121 0 121 4 0 7 109 97 112 45 108 101 110 1 23 1 64 0 0 121 4 0 9 118 101 99 45 101 109 112 116 121 1 24 1 64 1 2 97 48 121 0 121 4 0 7 118 101 99 45 108 101 110 1 25 1 64 2 2 97 48 121 2 97 49 121 0 121 4 0 7 118 101 99 45 103 101 116 1 26 1 64 2 2 97 48 121 2 97 49 121 0 121 4 0 8 118 101 99 45 112 117 115 104 1 27 1 64 3 2 97 48 121 2 97 49 121 2 97 50 121 0 121 4 0 10 118 101 99 45 117 112 100 97 116 101 1 28 1 64 2 2 97 48 121 2 97 49 121 0 121 4 0 12 98 121 116 101 115 45 99 111 110 99 97 116 1 29 1 64 3 2 97 48 121 2 97 49 121 2 97 50 121 0 121 4 0 11 98 121 116 101 115 45 115 108 105 99 101 1 30 1 64 1 2 97 48 121 0 121 4 0 13 98 121 116 101 115 45 99 111 109 112 97 99 116 1 31 1 64 0 0 121 4 0 9 109 97 112 45 101 109 112 116 121 1 32 1 64 3 2 97 48 121 2 97 49 121 2 97 50 121 0 121 4 0 10 109 97 112 45 105 110 115 101 114 116 1 33 1 64 2 2 97 48 121 2 97 49 121 0 121 4 0 10 109 97 112 45 108 111 111 107 117 112 1 34 1 64 2 2 97 48 121 2 97 49 121 0 121 4 0 10 109 97 112 45 114 101 109 111 118 101 1 35 1 64 1 2 97 48 121 0 121 4 0 8 109 97 112 45 115 105 122 101 1 36 1 64 1 2 97 48 121 0 121 4 0 8 109 97 112 45 105 116 101 114 1 37 1 64 1 2 97 48 121 0 121 4 0 13 109 97 112 45 105 116 101 114 45 110 101 120 116 1 38 1 64 1 2 97 48 121 0 121 4 0 12 109 97 112 45 105 116 101 114 45 107 101 121 1 39 1 64 1 2 97 48 121 0 121 4 0 12 109 97 112 45 105 116 101 114 45 118 97 108 1 40 10 25 1 0 20 99 97 100 101 110 122 97 58 114 117 110 116 105 109 101 47 104 101 97 112 5 0 6 12 1 1 0 0 7 98 111 120 45 105 110 116 8 5 1 1 0 0 0 6 12 1 1 0 0 7 103 101 116 45 105 110 116 8 5 1 1 0 1 0 6 13 1 1 0 0 8 98 111 120 45 98 111 111 108 8 5 1 1 0 2 0 6 13 1 1 0 0 8 103 101 116 45 98 111 111 108 8 5 1 1 0 3 0 6 14 1 1 0 0 9 98 111 120 45 102 108 111 97 116 8 5 1 1 0 4 0 6 14 1 1 0 0 9 103 101 116 45 102 108 111 97 116 8 5 1 1 0 5 0 6 14 1 1 0 0 9 97 114 114 45 97 108 108 111 99 8 5 1 1 0 6 0 6 12 1 1 0 0 7 97 114 114 45 115 101 116 8 5 1 1 0 7 0 6 12 1 1 0 0 7 97 114 114 45 103 101 116 8 5 1 1 0 8 0 6 12 1 1 0 0 7 97 114 114 45 108 101 110 8 5 1 1 0 9 0 6 12 1 1 0 0 7 115 117 109 45 110 101 119 8 5 1 1 0 10 0 6 13 1 1 0 0 8 115 117 109 45 100 105 115 99 8 5 1 1 0 11 0 6 16 1 1 0 0 11 115 117 109 45 112 97 121 108 111 97 100 8 5 1 1 0 12 0 6 16 1 1 0 0 11 98 121 116 101 115 45 97 108 108 111 99 8 5 1 1 0 13 0 6 14 1 1 0 0 9 98 121 116 101 115 45 115 101 116 8 5 1 1 0 14 0 6 14 1 1 0 0 9 98 121 116 101 115 45 103 101 116 8 5 1 1 0 15 0 6 14 1 1 0 0 9 98 121 116 101 115 45 108 101 110 8 5 1 1 0 16 0 6 8 1 1 0 0 3 100 117 112 8 5 1 1 0 17 0 6 9 1 1 0 0 4 100 114 111 112 8 5 1 1 0 18 0 6 14 1 1 0 0 9 109 97 112 45 97 108 108 111 99 8 5 1 1 0 19 0 6 12 1 1 0 0 7 109 97 112 45 115 101 116 8 5 1 1 0 20 0 6 12 1 1 0 0 7 109 97 112 45 107 101 121 8 5 1 1 0 21 0 6 12 1 1 0 0 7 109 97 112 45 118 97 108 8 5 1 1 0 22 0 6 12 1 1 0 0 7 109 97 112 45 108 101 110 8 5 1 1 0 23 0 6 14 1 1 0 0 9 118 101 99 45 101 109 112 116 121 8 5 1 1 0 24 0 6 12 1 1 0 0 7 118 101 99 45 108 101 110 8 5 1 1 0 25 0 6 12 1 1 0 0 7 118 101 99 45 103 101 116 8 5 1 1 0 26 0 6 13 1 1 0 0 8 118 101 99 45 112 117 115 104 8 5 1 1 0 27 0 6 15 1 1 0 0 10 118 101 99 45 117 112 100 97 116 101 8 5 1 1 0 28 0 6 17 1 1 0 0 12 98 121 116 101 115 45 99 111 110 99 97 116 8 5 1 1 0 29 0 6 16 1 1 0 0 11 98 121 116 101 115 45 115 108 105 99 101 8 5 1 1 0 30 0 6 18 1 1 0 0 13 98 121 116 101 115 45 99 111 109 112 97 99 116 8 5 1 1 0 31 0 6 14 1 1 0 0 9 109 97 112 45 101 109 112 116 121 8 5 1 1 0 32 0 6 15 1 1 0 0 10 109 97 112 45 105 110 115 101 114 116 8 5 1 1 0 33 0 6 15 1 1 0 0 10 109 97 112 45 108 111 111 107 117 112 8 5 1 1 0 34 0 6 15 1 1 0 0 10 109 97 112 45 114 101 109 111 118 101 8 5 1 1 0 35 0 6 13 1 1 0 0 8 109 97 112 45 115 105 122 101 8 5 1 1 0 36 0 6 13 1 1 0 0 8 109 97 112 45 105 116 101 114 8 5 1 1 0 37 0 6 18 1 1 0 0 13 109 97 112 45 105 116 101 114 45 110 101 120 116 8 5 1 1 0 38 0 6 17 1 1 0 0 12 109 97 112 45 105 116 101 114 45 107 101 121 8 5 1 1 0 39 0 6 17 1 1 0 0 12 109 97 112 45 105 116 101 114 45 118 97 108 8 5 1 1 0 40 0)))
(def (hl-corehead) (doc "Fixed core-module magic+type+import (951 B).")         (Bytes.of (list 0 97 115 109 1 0 0 0 1 253 1 46 96 1 126 1 127 96 1 127 1 126 96 1 127 1 127 96 1 127 1 127 96 1 124 1 127 96 1 127 1 124 96 1 127 1 127 96 3 127 127 127 1 127 96 2 127 127 1 127 96 1 127 1 127 96 2 127 127 1 127 96 1 127 1 127 96 1 127 1 127 96 1 127 1 127 96 3 127 127 127 1 127 96 2 127 127 1 127 96 1 127 1 127 96 1 127 0 96 1 127 0 96 1 127 1 127 96 4 127 127 127 127 1 127 96 2 127 127 1 127 96 2 127 127 1 127 96 1 127 1 127 96 0 1 127 96 1 127 1 127 96 2 127 127 1 127 96 2 127 127 1 127 96 3 127 127 127 1 127 96 2 127 127 1 127 96 3 127 127 127 1 127 96 1 127 1 127 96 0 1 127 96 3 127 127 127 1 127 96 2 127 127 1 127 96 2 127 127 1 127 96 1 127 1 127 96 1 127 1 127 96 1 127 1 127 96 1 127 1 127 96 1 127 1 127 96 4 127 127 127 127 1 127 96 2 126 127 1 127 96 0 1 127 96 2 127 127 1 127 96 0 1 127 2 172 5 41 4 104 101 97 112 7 98 111 120 45 105 110 116 0 0 4 104 101 97 112 7 103 101 116 45 105 110 116 0 1 4 104 101 97 112 8 98 111 120 45 98 111 111 108 0 2 4 104 101 97 112 8 103 101 116 45 98 111 111 108 0 3 4 104 101 97 112 9 98 111 120 45 102 108 111 97 116 0 4 4 104 101 97 112 9 103 101 116 45 102 108 111 97 116 0 5 4 104 101 97 112 9 97 114 114 45 97 108 108 111 99 0 6 4 104 101 97 112 7 97 114 114 45 115 101 116 0 7 4 104 101 97 112 7 97 114 114 45 103 101 116 0 8 4 104 101 97 112 7 97 114 114 45 108 101 110 0 9 4 104 101 97 112 7 115 117 109 45 110 101 119 0 10 4 104 101 97 112 8 115 117 109 45 100 105 115 99 0 11 4 104 101 97 112 11 115 117 109 45 112 97 121 108 111 97 100 0 12 4 104 101 97 112 11 98 121 116 101 115 45 97 108 108 111 99 0 13 4 104 101 97 112 9 98 121 116 101 115 45 115 101 116 0 14 4 104 101 97 112 9 98 121 116 101 115 45 103 101 116 0 15 4 104 101 97 112 9 98 121 116 101 115 45 108 101 110 0 16 4 104 101 97 112 3 100 117 112 0 17 4 104 101 97 112 4 100 114 111 112 0 18 4 104 101 97 112 9 109 97 112 45 97 108 108 111 99 0 19 4 104 101 97 112 7 109 97 112 45 115 101 116 0 20 4 104 101 97 112 7 109 97 112 45 107 101 121 0 21 4 104 101 97 112 7 109 97 112 45 118 97 108 0 22 4 104 101 97 112 7 109 97 112 45 108 101 110 0 23 4 104 101 97 112 9 118 101 99 45 101 109 112 116 121 0 24 4 104 101 97 112 7 118 101 99 45 108 101 110 0 25 4 104 101 97 112 7 118 101 99 45 103 101 116 0 26 4 104 101 97 112 8 118 101 99 45 112 117 115 104 0 27 4 104 101 97 112 10 118 101 99 45 117 112 100 97 116 101 0 28 4 104 101 97 112 12 98 121 116 101 115 45 99 111 110 99 97 116 0 29 4 104 101 97 112 11 98 121 116 101 115 45 115 108 105 99 101 0 30 4 104 101 97 112 13 98 121 116 101 115 45 99 111 109 112 97 99 116 0 31 4 104 101 97 112 9 109 97 112 45 101 109 112 116 121 0 32 4 104 101 97 112 10 109 97 112 45 105 110 115 101 114 116 0 33 4 104 101 97 112 10 109 97 112 45 108 111 111 107 117 112 0 34 4 104 101 97 112 10 109 97 112 45 114 101 109 111 118 101 0 35 4 104 101 97 112 8 109 97 112 45 115 105 122 101 0 36 4 104 101 97 112 8 109 97 112 45 105 116 101 114 0 37 4 104 101 97 112 13 109 97 112 45 105 116 101 114 45 110 101 120 116 0 38 4 104 101 97 112 12 109 97 112 45 105 116 101 114 45 107 101 121 0 39 4 104 101 97 112 12 109 97 112 45 105 116 101 114 45 118 97 108 0 40)))
(def (hl-coremid)  (doc "Fixed core func+mem+global+export sections (55 B).")   (Bytes.of (list 3 7 6 41 42 42 43 44 45 5 3 1 0 1 6 6 1 127 1 65 16 11 7 31 3 6 109 101 109 111 114 121 2 0 12 99 97 98 105 95 114 101 97 108 108 111 99 0 41 3 114 117 110 0 46)))
(def (hl-codepre)  (doc "Fixed code head: funccount + funcs 0,1,2 (int renderer).") (Bytes.of (list 6 29 1 1 127 35 0 32 2 106 65 1 107 65 0 32 2 107 113 33 4 32 4 32 3 106 36 0 32 4 11 40 0 32 0 66 9 86 4 64 32 0 66 10 128 32 1 16 42 33 1 11 32 1 65 48 32 0 66 10 130 167 106 58 0 0 32 1 65 1 106 11 44 1 1 126 32 0 66 0 83 4 64 32 1 65 45 58 0 0 32 1 65 1 106 33 1 66 0 32 0 125 33 2 5 32 0 33 2 11 32 2 32 1 16 42 11)))
(def (hl-codesuf)  (doc "Fixed code tail: funcs 4,5 (list-display walk + descriptor).") (Bytes.of (list 128 1 2 1 127 1 127 32 1 33 2 32 2 65 40 58 0 0 32 2 65 236 0 58 0 1 32 2 65 233 0 58 0 2 32 2 65 243 0 58 0 3 32 2 65 244 0 58 0 4 32 2 65 5 106 33 2 65 0 33 3 2 64 3 64 32 3 32 0 16 25 78 13 1 32 2 65 32 58 0 0 32 2 65 1 106 33 2 32 0 32 3 16 26 16 1 32 2 16 43 33 2 32 3 65 1 106 33 3 12 0 11 11 32 2 65 41 58 0 0 32 2 65 1 106 33 2 32 2 11 41 2 1 127 1 127 16 44 33 0 65 16 33 1 32 0 32 1 16 45 33 1 65 0 65 16 54 2 0 65 4 32 1 65 16 107 54 2 0 65 0 11)))
(def (hl-suffix)   (doc "Fixed component instance + wiring + exports (564 B).") (Bytes.of (list 2 235 3 2 1 41 7 98 111 120 45 105 110 116 0 0 7 103 101 116 45 105 110 116 0 1 8 98 111 120 45 98 111 111 108 0 2 8 103 101 116 45 98 111 111 108 0 3 9 98 111 120 45 102 108 111 97 116 0 4 9 103 101 116 45 102 108 111 97 116 0 5 9 97 114 114 45 97 108 108 111 99 0 6 7 97 114 114 45 115 101 116 0 7 7 97 114 114 45 103 101 116 0 8 7 97 114 114 45 108 101 110 0 9 7 115 117 109 45 110 101 119 0 10 8 115 117 109 45 100 105 115 99 0 11 11 115 117 109 45 112 97 121 108 111 97 100 0 12 11 98 121 116 101 115 45 97 108 108 111 99 0 13 9 98 121 116 101 115 45 115 101 116 0 14 9 98 121 116 101 115 45 103 101 116 0 15 9 98 121 116 101 115 45 108 101 110 0 16 3 100 117 112 0 17 4 100 114 111 112 0 18 9 109 97 112 45 97 108 108 111 99 0 19 7 109 97 112 45 115 101 116 0 20 7 109 97 112 45 107 101 121 0 21 7 109 97 112 45 118 97 108 0 22 7 109 97 112 45 108 101 110 0 23 9 118 101 99 45 101 109 112 116 121 0 24 7 118 101 99 45 108 101 110 0 25 7 118 101 99 45 103 101 116 0 26 8 118 101 99 45 112 117 115 104 0 27 10 118 101 99 45 117 112 100 97 116 101 0 28 12 98 121 116 101 115 45 99 111 110 99 97 116 0 29 11 98 121 116 101 115 45 115 108 105 99 101 0 30 13 98 121 116 101 115 45 99 111 109 112 97 99 116 0 31 9 109 97 112 45 101 109 112 116 121 0 32 10 109 97 112 45 105 110 115 101 114 116 0 33 10 109 97 112 45 108 111 111 107 117 112 0 34 10 109 97 112 45 114 101 109 111 118 101 0 35 8 109 97 112 45 115 105 122 101 0 36 8 109 97 112 45 105 116 101 114 0 37 13 109 97 112 45 105 116 101 114 45 110 101 120 116 0 38 12 109 97 112 45 105 116 101 114 45 107 101 121 0 39 12 109 97 112 45 105 116 101 114 45 118 97 108 0 40 0 0 1 4 104 101 97 112 18 0 6 37 3 0 0 1 1 3 114 117 110 0 2 1 1 6 109 101 109 111 114 121 0 0 1 1 12 99 97 98 105 95 114 101 97 108 108 111 99 7 5 1 64 0 0 115 8 11 1 0 0 41 3 0 3 0 4 42 1 11 9 1 0 3 114 117 110 1 41 0)))

(def (hl-elems-code xs i n out)
  (doc "Emit per-element construction for list elements [i,n): each is i64.const e (sleb), call 0 (box-int),
        call 27 (vec-push). Left-to-right, matching native push order.")
  (if (< i n)
      (hl-elems-code xs (+ i 1) n
        (cat out (match (List.at xs i)
                   ((None _) (empty-bytes))
                   ((Some e) (cat (u8 0x42) (cat (sleb e) (Bytes.of (list 0x10 0x00 0x10 0x1B))))))))
      out))

(def (hl-run-body xs)
  (doc "The generated run body for an int-list: 0 local decls, call 24 (vec-empty), then per-element
        const/box/push, then 0x0B end. NOT length-prefixed (caller wraps).")
  (cat (Bytes.of (list 0x00 0x10 0x18))
       (cat (hl-elems-code xs 0 (List.len xs) (empty-bytes))
            (u8 0x0B))))

(def (heap-list-component xs)
  (doc "Assemble the heap-importing component for a runtime int-list `xs`. VERIFIED byte-identical to native:
        prefix ++ (id=1 ++ sized CORE) ++ suffix; CORE = corehead ++ coremid ++ codesec; codesec = section 10
        over (codepre ++ sized(run-body) ++ codesuf).")
  (let ((runbody (hl-run-body xs)))
    (let ((codesec (section 0x0A (cat (hl-codepre) (cat (sized runbody) (hl-codesuf))))))
      (let ((core (cat (hl-corehead) (cat (hl-coremid) codesec))))
        (cat (hl-prefix) (cat (cat (u8 0x01) (sized core)) (hl-suffix)))))))

; ─── unit-value component (ask-60): a nullary `main` returning the UNIT value ──
; `(def (main) unit)` / `(def (main) ())` compiles to an 86-byte component exporting `run : () -> ()` (an
; empty result — NOT a scalar, NOT a heap value). The whole component is a FIXED BLOB (no value to splice),
; transcribed byte-identical from native (`unit` and `()` emit the identical 86 bytes). A self-contained
; whole-program shape (nullary main, one function), so a fixed template is legitimate here.
(def (unit-component)
  (doc "The fixed 86-byte component for a program whose result is the unit value. Byte-identical to native.")
  (Bytes.of (list 0 97 115 109 13 0 1 0 1 33 0 97 115 109 1 0 0 0 1 4 1 96 0 0 3 2 1 0 7 7 1 3 114 117 110 0 0 10 4 1 2 0 11 2 4 1 0 0 0 7 5 1 64 0 1 0 6 9 1 0 0 1 0 3 114 117 110 8 6 1 0 0 0 0 0 11 9 1 0 3 114 117 110 1 0 0)))


(def (compound-receiver-off b roff env lce)
  (doc "The byte-offset of the LITERAL COMPOUND a projection's receiver at `roff` denotes, or -1 if the
        receiver is not a (statically-known) compound. Two sources: (a) the receiver IS a literal `(tuple
        …)`/`(record …)` array → its own offset; (b) the receiver is a bare NAME (tag) bound by a `let` to a
        literal compound — resolve its local slot (`env`) and look it up in `lce` (constant propagation).
        This unifies the direct-literal and let-bound projection cases. -1 for anything else (a param, a
        call, a scalar) → the caller declines or rejects.")
  (if (and (= (cbor-major b roff) 4)
           (or (node-head-is b roff b"tuple" 5) (node-head-is b roff b"record" 6)))
      roff                                          ; a literal (tuple …)/(record …) — its own offset
  (if (= (cbor-major b roff) 6)
      (lce-at lce (ienv-pos env (name-ref-index b roff) 0))  ; a bound name → its lce compound offset (or -1)
  (if (and (and (= (cbor-major b roff) 4) (head-is b (read-head-index b roff) b"." 1)) (= (app-arity b roff) 2))
      (proj-record-field-off b roff env lce)        ; nested `(. <compound> field)` receiver → the projected sub-compound's offset
  (if (and (and (= (cbor-major b roff) 4) (name-has-prefix b (prelude-entry b (read-head-index b roff)) b"tuple." 6))
           (= (app-arity b roff) 1))
      (proj-tuple-elem-off b roff env lce)          ; nested `(tuple.N <compound>)` receiver → the projected sub-compound's offset
      (- 0 1))))))                                  ; a scalar / other → not a compound receiver

(def (proj-record-field-off b roff env lce)
  (doc "For a `(. <receiver> field)` at roff whose receiver folds to a compound (recursively), the byte-offset
        of the projected FIELD's value node (which may itself be a sub-compound), or -1 if the receiver isn't a
        record or the field is absent. Enables NESTED projection: `(. (. (record (o (record (i 7)))) o) i)`.")
  (let ((sub (compound-receiver-off b (read-child-off b roff 1) env lce)))
    (if (and (>= sub 0) (node-head-is b sub b"record" 6))
        (record-field-value-off b sub (read-head-index b (read-child-off b roff 2)) 1 (+ (app-arity b sub) 1))
        (- 0 1))))

(def (proj-tuple-elem-off b roff env lce)
  (doc "For a `(tuple.N <receiver>)` at roff whose receiver folds to a compound, the byte-offset of element N,
        or -1 if the receiver isn't a tuple or N is out of range. Enables nested tuple projection.")
  (let ((sub (compound-receiver-off b (read-child-off b roff 1) env lce)))
    (if (and (and (>= sub 0) (node-head-is b sub b"tuple" 5))
             (< (tuple-accessor-index b (read-head-index b roff)) (app-arity b sub)))
        (read-child-off b sub (+ (tuple-accessor-index b (read-head-index b roff)) 1))
        (- 0 1))))

(def (record-field-value-off b roff field-hidx k end)
  (doc "In a literal `(record (f0 v0) (f1 v1) …)` at offset roff, the VALUE-node offset of the field whose
        name-index = `field-hidx`, scanning entries k..end (each entry `(name value)` is child k, an array
        whose head-index is the field name and whose child 1 is the value). -1 if absent. Returns the FIRST
        match (source order); a duplicate field is a separate reject, not reached for a well-formed record.")
  (if (< k end)
      (let ((eoff (read-child-off b roff k)))
        (if (and (= (cbor-major b eoff) 4) (= (read-head-index b eoff) field-hidx))
            (read-child-off b eoff 1)
            (record-field-value-off b roff field-hidx (+ k 1) end)))
      (- 0 1)))

(def (project-field b recv-off field-hidx env fenv lce)
  (doc "Project field `field-hidx` off the receiver at recv-off, returning the folded Node — or a decline/reject.
        THREE receiver shapes: (a) `(if c A B)` → DISTRIBUTE: `NIf(read c, project A, project B)` (native folds a
        projection through a conditional to a scalar select — `(. (if c A B) f)` emits the SAME bytes as `(if c
        (. A f) (. B f))`; each branch re-projects, composing with nested ifs/records); (b) a literal/let-bound
        `(record …)` (via `compound-receiver-off`) → the field's value node (declines if the field is absent — we
        don't model the field set); (c) a PROVABLE-SCALAR receiver → CDZ0201 (member access on a non-record);
        anything else DECLINES (unknown runtime shape). Mirrors the direct-projection logic, adding if-distribution.")
  (if (and (and (= (cbor-major b recv-off) 4) (head-is b (read-head-index b recv-off) b"if" 2))
           (= (app-arity b recv-off) 3))
      (Node.NIf (tuple (read-node b (read-child-off b recv-off 1) env fenv lce)
                       (project-field b (read-child-off b recv-off 2) field-hidx env fenv lce)
                       (project-field b (read-child-off b recv-off 3) field-hidx env fenv lce)))
      (let ((roff (compound-receiver-off b recv-off env lce)))
        (if (and (>= roff 0) (node-head-is b roff b"record" 6))
            (let ((voff (record-field-value-off b roff field-hidx 1 (+ (app-arity b roff) 1))))
              (if (< voff 0) (declined) (read-node b voff env fenv lce)))
            (if (node-provably-scalar (read-node b recv-off env fenv lce))
                (rejected 201)
                (declined))))))

(def (project-tuple-elem b recv-off n env fenv lce)
  (doc "Project element `n` off the tuple receiver at recv-off, returning the folded Node — or a decline/reject.
        The tuple-side mirror of `project-field`: (a) `(if c A B)` receiver → DISTRIBUTE `NIf(read c, project A,
        project B)` (native folds `(tuple.N (if c A B))` to the same scalar select as `(if c (tuple.N A) (tuple.N
        B))`); (b) a literal/let-bound `(tuple …)` → element n's node (declines if n is past the arity — we don't
        model the arity); (c) a PROVABLE-SCALAR receiver → CDZ0201; else DECLINE (unknown runtime shape).")
  (if (and (and (= (cbor-major b recv-off) 4) (head-is b (read-head-index b recv-off) b"if" 2))
           (= (app-arity b recv-off) 3))
      (Node.NIf (tuple (read-node b (read-child-off b recv-off 1) env fenv lce)
                       (project-tuple-elem b (read-child-off b recv-off 2) n env fenv lce)
                       (project-tuple-elem b (read-child-off b recv-off 3) n env fenv lce)))
      (let ((roff (compound-receiver-off b recv-off env lce)))
        (if (and (>= roff 0) (node-head-is b roff b"tuple" 5))
            (if (< n (app-arity b roff))
                (read-node b (read-child-off b roff (+ n 1)) env fenv lce)
                (declined))
            (if (node-provably-scalar (read-node b recv-off env fenv lce))
                (rejected 201)
                (declined))))))

(def (form-is-value-def b foff)
  (doc "Is the `do`-form at foff a VALUE definition `(def <name> <value>)` — head `def`, arity 2, and element 1
        is a BARE NAME (a major-6 tag), NOT a `(f params)` function-def (a major-4 array)? A value-def is scoped
        for the rest of the block, desugaring to a `let`. A function-def / module / effect decl is NOT this
        (declines — local functions are a separate, harder case).")
  (and (and (= (cbor-major b foff) 4) (head-is b (read-head-index b foff) b"def" 3))
       (and (= (app-arity b foff) 2) (= (cbor-major b (read-child-off b foff 1)) 6))))

(def (read-do b i env fenv lce k end)
  (doc "Read a `do` block's forms k..end. A leading VALUE-DEFINITION form `(def name value)` desugars to
        `(let ((name value)) (do <rest>))` — the value reads under the current env, the name binds at the next
        slot, and the REST of the block reads under the extended env (so later forms/defs see it; sequential
        `def`s nest as `let`s). A non-value-def form starts the ordinary tail: the remaining forms read into an
        NDo. If a value-def is the LAST form, its body is the value itself (a `do` ending in a def yields it).")
  (let ((foff (read-child-off b i k)))
    (if (form-is-value-def b foff)
        (let ((name-hidx (read-head-index b (read-child-off b foff 1)))
              (val-node (read-node b (read-child-off b foff 2) env fenv lce)))
          (Node.NLet (tuple (ienv-len env) val-node
                            (if (< (+ k 1) end)
                                (read-do b i (ienv-snoc env name-hidx) fenv lce (+ k 1) end)
                                (Node.NLocal (ienv-len env))))))   ; def is the last form → yield the bound value
        ; not a value-def: the ordinary tail — read forms k..end into an NDo (a single form → just that form).
        (if (< (+ k 1) end)
            (Node.NDo (read-call-args b i env fenv lce k end (list)))
            (read-node b foff env fenv lce)))))

(def (read-app b i env fenv lce)
  (doc "Decode an application array at offset i by its head name and arity, under param env `env` and
        function env `fenv`. Dispatch order: `let` (head + bindings + body) → nested NLet; `if` (head +
        3 operands) → NIf; `not` (head + 1 operand) → NPrim \"not\"; then, if the head names a MODULE
        FUNCTION (its prelude index is in `fenv`) → a call (read-call, resolving the head to its
        function index); otherwise a binary operator NPrim (head + 2 operands). Children read
        recursively under the same envs. Function lookup precedes operator recognition, but operator
        symbols never appear in `fenv` (only `def` names do), so operators still fall through.
        FIXED-ARITY forms (`if`=3, `not`=1, a binary operator=2) CHECK the application's operand count
        (`app-arity`) and route a mismatch to `malformed` (→ KError) rather than reading past / dropping
        operands — a `(+ 1)` or `(if c t)` is a rejected malformed form, not a truncated one (ask-30).")
  ; const String.scalar-len / String.byte-len on a string LITERAL → fold to a const Int (ask-60). Checked
  ; FIRST because it recognizes the `(apply (. String <m>) strlit)` shape exactly; any other apply/method
  ; falls through (str-method-of → 0) to the ordinary dispatch → decline. No heap, no runtime String.
  (if (> (str-method-of b i) 0)
      (if (= (str-method-of b i) 1)
          (Node.NInt (str-prod-scalar-len b (read-child-off b i 2)))  ; scalar-len → Unicode scalar count (over producer)
          (Node.NInt (str-prod-len b (read-child-off b i 2))))        ; byte-len → UTF-8 byte count (over producer)
  ; const Bytes.len on a `(Bytes.of (list …))` / `(String.to-bytes "…")` literal → fold to a const Int (ask-60).
  ; `const-bytes-len` returns the byte count (≥0) for a foldable literal, or -1 (a non-const/out-of-range byte,
  ; a runtime arg, or another form → fall through → decline).
  (if (and (dotted-method? b i b"Bytes" 5 b"len" 3) (= (app-arity b i) 2))
      (if (>= (const-bytes-len b (read-child-off b i 2)) 0)
          (Node.NInt (const-bytes-len b (read-child-off b i 2)))
          (declined))                                              ; a Bytes.len on a non-foldable arg → decline
  (if (head-is b (read-head-index b i) b"let" 3)
      ; A `let` is `(let <bindings> <body>)` — arity EXACTLY 2. `(let ())` (no body) and `(let ((x 1)))`
      ; (bindings but no body) are arity 1: a malformed form native rejects CDZ0201 "malformed `let` form:
      ; arity mismatch", not a program that yields 0. Guard the arity before reading child 2 (the body),
      ; which would otherwise index past the array end and mis-read a bindings entry as the body.
      (if (= (app-arity b i) 2)
          (read-let b (read-child-off b i 1) (read-child-off b i 2) env 0
                    (cbor-arg-len-bindings b (read-child-off b i 1)) fenv lce)
          (malformed))
  (if (head-is b (read-head-index b i) b"do" 2)
      ; (do e1 … eN): a sequencing block. A `do` with no forms is malformed. A leading VALUE-DEFINITION form
      ; `(def <name> <value>)` (name is a bare tag, NOT a `(f params)` function-def) is scoped for the rest of
      ; the block — it desugars to `(let ((name value)) (do <rest>))` (read-do). A FUNCTION-def `(def (f …) …)`
      ; or a `module`/`effect` declaration in do-position is a separate, harder case (local functions / modules-
      ; as-values) — still declines. Otherwise the forms read as ordinary nodes into an NDo.
      (if (< (app-arity b i) 1)
          (malformed)
          (read-do b i env fenv lce 1 (+ (app-arity b i) 1)))
  (if (head-is b (read-head-index b i) b"match" 5)
      (read-match b i env fenv lce)
  (if (head-is b (read-head-index b i) b":" 1)
      ; Type annotation `(: expr Type)` (arity 2). A consistent annotation is TRANSPARENT — it yields the
      ; value (`(: 42 Int64)` → 42, `(: (< 1 2) Bool)` → true). A CONTRADICTORY one (`(: 42 Bool)`, `(: (< 1
      ; 2) Int64)`) is rejected CDZ0203. Handled only for the scalar types this compiler models (`Int64`,
      ; `Bool`): if the annotation names Int64/Bool, check the expr's provable kind against it — match →
      ; erase (read the inner expr), provable mismatch → `(rejected 203)`, unprovable (a param) → yield the
      ; inner expr (transparent, conservative — native would infer it). A NON-scalar type (UInt8, BigInt,
      ; a `(UInt N)` width, a compound type) → DECLINE (this compiler doesn't model those types).
      (if (= (app-arity b i) 2)
          (read-annotation b i env fenv lce)
          (malformed))
  (if (or (or (head-is b (read-head-index b i) b"record" 6) (head-is b (read-head-index b i) b"map" 3))
          (or (head-is b (read-head-index b i) b"tuple" 5) (head-is b (read-head-index b i) b"list" 4)))
      ; A `record`/`map`/`tuple`/`list` LITERAL — this compiler doesn't build a compound VALUE yet (the M2
      ; heap frontier), so as a bare value it still DECLINES (KCompound → `unreachable` in `lower`). But two
      ; new behaviors: (1) a DUPLICATE record field / map key is a well-formedness error native rejects CDZ0201
      ; (structural — repeated entry head-index); (2) resolving to `NCompound`→`KCompound` (not a generic
      ; KError decline) makes the compound PROVABLY-not-scalar, so a scalar-vs-compound `if`-branch / a compound
      ; arith or `if`-condition operand is a PROVABLE reject (the fold-discards-dead-branch soundness hole).
      (if (and (or (head-is b (read-head-index b i) b"record" 6) (head-is b (read-head-index b i) b"map" 3))
               (record-has-dup b i))
          (rejected 201)
          (Node.NCompound 0))
  (if (head-is b (read-head-index b i) b"if" 2)
      (if (= (app-arity b i) 3)
          (Node.NIf (tuple (read-node b (read-child-off b i 1) env fenv lce)
                           (read-node b (read-child-off b i 2) env fenv lce)
                           (read-node b (read-child-off b i 3) env fenv lce)))
          (malformed))
  (if (head-is b (read-head-index b i) b"not" 3)
      (if (= (app-arity b i) 1)
          (Node.NPrim (tuple "not" (read-node b (read-child-off b i 1) env fenv lce) (Node.NInt 0)))
          (malformed))
  (if (head-is b (read-head-index b i) b"." 1)
      ; Member access `(. receiver name)`. This compiler does not build records, so a member access is
      ; normally a DECLINE. But member access on a PROVABLE NON-RECORD (a scalar receiver — an int/bool/float
      ; literal or an arithmetic result) is a WELL-FORMEDNESS error native rejects CDZ0201 "member access on a
      ; non-record", regardless of record support. If arity is 2 AND the receiver resolves to a provably-scalar
      ; Core kind → `(rejected 201)`; a NAME receiver (unknown shape — could be a record) DECLINES, matching
      ; native (which takes a runtime-shape path there, not a CDZ0201). Reject-don't-miscompile, precisely.
      ; NEW: a LITERAL `(record (f v) …)` receiver → FOLD to the named field's value (a compile-time projection
      ; to a scalar; nested `(. (. …) …)` composes since the folded value is itself read via `read-node`). A
      ; missing field → decline (native rejects CDZ0201, but we don't model the record's field set here — a
      ; safe under-reject). Arity must be 2 (receiver + field name); the field is child 2's head-index.
      (if (= (app-arity b i) 2)
          ; Delegate to `project-field`: it folds a literal/let-bound record receiver to the field's value,
          ; DISTRIBUTES over an `(if c A B)` receiver (native folds projection-through-conditional to a scalar
          ; select), rejects a provable-scalar receiver (CDZ0201), and declines an unknown runtime shape.
          (project-field b (read-child-off b i 1) (read-head-index b (read-child-off b i 2)) env fenv lce)
          (declined))
  (if (name-has-prefix b (prelude-entry b (read-head-index b i)) b"tuple." 6)
      ; Tuple access `(tuple.N receiver)`. Three cases by receiver:
      ;  - a LITERAL `(tuple e0 e1 …)` → FOLD: read element N directly (a compile-time projection to a scalar;
      ;    rides the scalar envelope, no heap). Out-of-range N → decline (native rejects, but we don't model the
      ;    tuple arity here; a safe under-reject).
      ;  - a PROVABLE-SCALAR receiver → `(rejected 201)` "tuple access on a non-tuple" (native rejects).
      ;  - else (a name / runtime tuple) → DECLINE (needs the runtime heap path).
      (if (= (app-arity b i) 1)
          ; Delegate to `project-tuple-elem`: folds a literal/let-bound tuple receiver to element N, DISTRIBUTES
          ; over an `(if c A B)` receiver, rejects a provable-scalar receiver (CDZ0201), declines unknown shapes.
          (project-tuple-elem b (read-child-off b i 1) (tuple-accessor-index b (read-head-index b i)) env fenv lce)
          (declined))
  (if (< (ienv-pos fenv (read-head-index b i) 0) 0)
      ; Not a module function. Either a binary operator (head recognized by `read-op-name`) or an UNKNOWN
      ; head. A recognized binary operator with the wrong arity is MALFORMED (reject). But an UNKNOWN head
      ; (`read-op-name` → `"?"`, e.g. `list`, a user constructor) is an UNSUPPORTED construct — a DECLINE at
      ; ANY arity, NOT a malformed binary op: `(list 1 2 3)` is a list literal native compiles, not a bad `+`.
      (if (= (read-op-name b (read-head-index b i)) "?")
          (declined)                                    ; unknown head → unsupported construct → DECLINE
          (if (= (app-arity b i) 2)
              ; CONST EQUALITY const-fold (ask-60): `(= a b)` where both operands are the SAME literal kind —
              ; two STRING literals (byte-compare the stored NFC text), two byte producers (`Bytes.of`/concat/
              ; slice… — byte-compare via bytes-nth), OR two FLOAT literals (byte-compare the shortest-exact
              ; CBOR encoding = native's canonical-byte-form equality, so `-0.0` ≠ `0.0`). Native realizes ONLY
              ; `=` on these (not `!= < <=`, and a type-mismatched pair DECLINES — mirrored by NOT folding a
              ; mixed pair). Any other operator, or a mixed/non-literal pair, falls through → `?`/KError → decline.
              (if (name-eq b (prelude-entry b (read-head-index b i)) b"=" 0 1)
                  ; `(= (String.from-bytes P) None)` (either operand order) over a const byte producer P → const
                  ; Bool: `None` iff P is NOT well-formed UTF-8. Native realizes ONLY this `= … None` fold on the
                  ; from-bytes result (the `(Some s)` case is a heap string — not foldable here). Reuses the strict
                  ; UTF-8 validator; folds to a Bool (emitted byte-identically).
                  (if (and (>= (from-bytes-producer-off b (read-child-off b i 1)) 0) (node-is-none? b (read-child-off b i 2)))
                      (Node.NBool (bool01 (not (const-utf8-valid? b (from-bytes-producer-off b (read-child-off b i 1))))))
                  (if (and (node-is-none? b (read-child-off b i 1)) (>= (from-bytes-producer-off b (read-child-off b i 2)) 0))
                      (Node.NBool (bool01 (not (const-utf8-valid? b (from-bytes-producer-off b (read-child-off b i 2))))))
                  (if (and (node-is-str-producer? b (read-child-off b i 1)) (node-is-str-producer? b (read-child-off b i 2)))
                      (Node.NBool (bool01 (str-prod-eq? b (read-child-off b i 1) (read-child-off b i 2))))
                  (if (and (node-is-bytes-producer? b (read-child-off b i 1)) (node-is-bytes-producer? b (read-child-off b i 2)))
                      (Node.NBool (bool01 (const-bytes-eq? b (read-child-off b i 1) (read-child-off b i 2))))
                  (if (and (node-is-float-lit? b (read-child-off b i 1)) (node-is-float-lit? b (read-child-off b i 2)))
                      (Node.NBool (bool01 (float-lit-eq? b (read-child-off b i 1) (read-child-off b i 2))))
                  (if (and (node-is-unit-value? b (read-child-off b i 1)) (node-is-unit-value? b (read-child-off b i 2)))
                      (Node.NBool 1)                     ; unit = unit is always true (one unit value; `()`≡`unit`)
                  ; COMPOUND equality (tuple/list/record literals) → 3-valued structural compare. ≥0 folds;
                  ; -1 (not comparable / type mismatch native rejects) falls through → generic NPrim → decline.
                  (if (>= (compound-eq b (read-child-off b i 1) (read-child-off b i 2)) 0)
                      (Node.NBool (compound-eq b (read-child-off b i 1) (read-child-off b i 2)))
                      (Node.NPrim (tuple (read-op-name b (read-head-index b i))
                                         (read-node b (read-child-off b i 1) env fenv lce)
                                         (read-node b (read-child-off b i 2) env fenv lce))))))))))
                  (Node.NPrim (tuple (read-op-name b (read-head-index b i))
                                     (read-node b (read-child-off b i 1) env fenv lce)
                                     (read-node b (read-child-off b i 2) env fenv lce))))
              (malformed)))                             ; recognized operator, wrong arity → REJECT
      (read-call b i env fenv lce (ienv-pos fenv (read-head-index b i) 0)))))))))))))))

; ─── `match` on Bool: (match scrut (true e) (false e)) → (if scrut …) ────────
; A `match` is `[match, scrutinee, arm…]` where each arm is `[pattern, body]`. This
; compiler handles the BOOL case only: exactly two arms whose patterns are the bool
; literals `true`/`false` (in either order) — desugared to the `if` the backend already
; lowers. A match on a USER SUM (constructor-pattern arms) is a separate, larger gap
; (ask-13, no built-in-list/sum pattern surface yet); such a match DECLINES (→ KError)
; rather than mis-reading. The bool patterns are CBOR major-7 simple values (20=false,
; 21=true); `arm-pat-is-bool`/`arm-pat-bool` read them, `arm-body` reads the arm's 2nd
; element. Complementary patterns (one true, one false) are required; a doubled or
; non-bool pattern declines.
(def (arm-pat-off b arm-off)
  (doc "Offset of an arm's PATTERN. An arm encodes as `[apply, pattern, body]` — element 0 is the reserved
        `apply` head (the arm is a computed pair, like a `let` binding), so the PATTERN is element 1.")
  (read-child-off b arm-off 1))
(def (arm-body-off b arm-off)
  (doc "Offset of an arm's BODY — element 2 of the `[apply, pattern, body]` arm array (past apply+pattern).")
  (read-child-off b arm-off 2))
(def (pat-is-bool b poff)
  (doc "Is the pattern at poff a bool literal? (CBOR major 7, info 20=false / 21=true.)")
  (and (= (cbor-major b poff) 7)
       (or (= (cbor-info b poff) 20) (= (cbor-info b poff) 21))))
(def (pat-bool b poff)
  (doc "The bool value of a bool-literal pattern at poff: 1 for true (info 21), 0 for false (info 20).")
  (if (= (cbor-info b poff) 21) 1 0))

; ─── const-scalar literal-pattern match folding ─────────────────────────────
; When a `match` scrutinee is a compile-time CONSTANT scalar (a literal, or a
; folded projection like `(. r n)` off a let-bound record), the match selects one
; arm at compile time — native folds `(match 5 (5 100) (_ 200))` → 100. An arm is
; `(pattern body)`: a LITERAL-pattern arm encodes `[apply, pat, body]` (arm arity 2:
; pattern + body); a WILDCARD arm `(_ body)` encodes `[_, body]` (arm arity 1: the
; `_` is the head, body is child 1). This fold walks the arms selecting the first
; whose literal pattern equals the scrutinee, else the wildcard; NO matching arm and
; NO wildcard → non-exhaustive, DECLINE (native declines/rejects — a safe under-reject).
; Only fires for a CONST scalar scrutinee; a runtime scrutinee declines (needs real
; match codegen). Bool patterns keep their own path (desugar to `if`) — this handles
; the INT-literal case the bool path doesn't.
(def (arm-is-wildcard b arm-off)
  (doc "Is the arm at arm-off a WILDCARD arm `(_ body)`? It encodes as a 2-element array `[_, body]` (arm
        arity 1 — the `_` is the head, not an `apply`), distinct from a literal-pattern arm `[apply, pat,
        body]` (arm arity 2). Detect by arm arity 1 AND head naming `_`.")
  (and (= (app-arity b arm-off) 1)
       (name-eq b (prelude-entry b (read-head-index b arm-off)) b"_" 0 1)))

(def (arm-wildcard-body-off b arm-off)
  (doc "The BODY offset of a wildcard arm `[_, body]` — child 1 (past the `_` head).")
  (read-child-off b arm-off 1))

(def (pat-const-val b poff)
  (doc "The Int64 value a LITERAL scalar pattern at poff denotes (a uint/negint literal). Used to compare an
        int-literal arm pattern against a const scrutinee. Assumes the pattern is a plain integer node.")
  (if (= (cbor-major b poff) 1)
      (- (- 0 1) (cbor-arg b poff))    ; negint
      (cbor-arg b poff)))              ; uint (major 0)

(def (pat-is-int b poff)
  (doc "Is the pattern at poff a plain integer literal (CBOR major 0 uint or 1 negint)? — the pattern kind
        this const-fold matches against an integer scrutinee.")
  (or (= (cbor-major b poff) 0) (= (cbor-major b poff) 1)))

(def (scrut-is-bool-const node)
  (doc "Is a folded Core scrutinee a BOOL constant (`KBoolC`)? A bool-const match keeps the existing bool
        desugar path (2 complementary arms → `if`); only an INT-const scrutinee takes the literal-pattern
        fold. Distinguishes the two const kinds so `(match true (true 1)(false 0))` isn't int-folded.")
  (match node ((Core.KBoolC n) true)
              ((Core.KConst n) false) ((Core.KFloat n) false) ((Core.KCompound n) false)
              ((Core.KAdd p) false) ((Core.KSub p) false) ((Core.KMul p) false)
              ((Core.KDiv p) false) ((Core.KMod p) false) ((Core.KBitAnd p) false)
              ((Core.KBitOr p) false) ((Core.KXor p) false) ((Core.KShl p) false)
              ((Core.KShr p) false) ((Core.KLt p) false) ((Core.KGt p) false)
              ((Core.KLe p) false) ((Core.KGe p) false) ((Core.KEq p) false)
              ((Core.KNe p) false) ((Core.KNot p) false) ((Core.KIf p) false)
              ((Core.KLocal n) false) ((Core.KLet p) false) ((Core.KCall p) false)
              ((Core.KDo forms) false) ((Core.KError k) false)))

(def (fold-const-match b i env fenv lce sv nargs k)
  (doc "Fold a `match` whose scrutinee is the CONST Int64 `sv`: walk arms k..nargs (arm j is match child
        j+1), returning the BODY node of the first arm that matches — a wildcard `(_ body)` matches always;
        a literal-int arm `(v body)` matches iff `v = sv`. No matching arm → `(declined)` (non-exhaustive:
        native declines/rejects, a safe under-reject). Bool-literal arms are NOT handled here (the bool path
        owns those); an arm whose pattern is neither an int literal nor `_` → decline (a construct pattern,
        ask-13). Reads the selected body under the current envs so it folds/resolves in scope.")
  (if (< k nargs)
      (let ((arm-off (read-child-off b i (+ k 1))))
        (if (arm-is-wildcard b arm-off)
            (read-node b (arm-wildcard-body-off b arm-off) env fenv lce)   ; `_` matches → its body
            (if (and (= (app-arity b arm-off) 2) (pat-is-int b (arm-pat-off b arm-off)))
                (if (= (pat-const-val b (arm-pat-off b arm-off)) sv)
                    (read-node b (arm-body-off b arm-off) env fenv lce)     ; literal matches → its body
                    (fold-const-match b i env fenv lce sv nargs (+ k 1)))   ; try next arm
                (declined))))                                              ; non-int, non-`_` pattern → decline (ask-13)
      (declined)))                                                         ; ran out of arms, no match → non-exhaustive decline

; ─── runtime integer-literal match → an if-chain of equality tests ──────────
; `(match <scalar> (l0 b0) (l1 b1) … (catchall bc))` on a RUNTIME Int64 scrutinee
; lowers to `(if (= s l0) b0 (if (= s l1) b1 … bc))`. The catchall (required — an
; integer type has infinitely many values, so literal-only arms are never
; exhaustive → decline, matching native's CDZ0210) is `_` / `else` / a NAME that
; BINDS the scrutinee (usable in its body). To lower cleanly this compiler handles
; the case where the SCRUTINEE IS A BARE LOCAL (a param / let binding): reading a
; local is idempotent, so each arm compares `(= <local> lit)` with no re-eval, and a
; name-binding catchall aliases the pattern name to the SAME local slot (no new
; binding needed). A non-local scrutinee (a complex expr) or a non-int/non-catchall
; arm (a constructor pattern) DECLINES — the runtime-heap / sum-match frontier.
(def (arm-is-catchall b arm-off)
  (doc "Is the arm at arm-off a CATCHALL — a 2-element `[head, body]` (arm arity 1) whose head is `_`, `else`,
        or a binding NAME? Matches any scrutinee. (A literal-pattern arm is a 3-element `[apply, lit, body]`,
        arm arity 2 — NOT a catchall.) Any arm-arity-1 head is a catchall: `_`/`else`/a bound name all match all.")
  (= (app-arity b arm-off) 1))
(def (arm-catchall-binds-name b arm-off)
  (doc "Does this catchall arm BIND a name (a name pattern like `k`, which binds the scrutinee), as opposed to
        `_`/`else` (which discard)? True unless the head is `_` or `else`. The bound name's prelude index is the
        arm's head-index; its body reads with that name aliased to the scrutinee's local slot.")
  (and (not (name-eq b (prelude-entry b (read-head-index b arm-off)) b"_" 0 1))
       (not (name-eq b (prelude-entry b (read-head-index b arm-off)) b"else" 0 4))))

(def (scrut-local-slot b i env)
  (doc "If the match scrutinee (child 1) is a BARE-NAME reference to a param/let LOCAL, its slot; else -1. A
        bare local is the tractable runtime-int-match scrutinee (idempotent to re-read, so no let-bind needed).")
  (if (= (cbor-major b (read-child-off b i 1)) 6)
      (ienv-pos env (name-ref-index b (read-child-off b i 1)) 0)
      (- 0 1)))

(def (arms-all-int-or-catchall b i nargs k)
  (doc "Do all arms k..nargs of the match at i have an INT-literal pattern (3-elem `[apply, int, body]`) or are
        a CATCHALL (2-elem)? — the guard that this is an int-literal match (not a sum/constructor match, ask-13).")
  (if (< k nargs)
      (let ((arm-off (read-child-off b i (+ k 1))))
        (if (arm-is-catchall b arm-off)
            (arms-all-int-or-catchall b i nargs (+ k 1))
            (if (and (= (app-arity b arm-off) 2) (pat-is-int b (arm-pat-off b arm-off)))
                (arms-all-int-or-catchall b i nargs (+ k 1))
                false)))
      true))

(def (match-has-catchall b i nargs k)
  (doc "Does the match at i have a CATCHALL arm among k..nargs? Required for a runtime int-match to be
        exhaustive (an int type is infinite); no catchall → non-exhaustive → the caller declines (native CDZ0210).")
  (if (< k nargs)
      (if (arm-is-catchall b (read-child-off b i (+ k 1)))
          true
          (match-has-catchall b i nargs (+ k 1)))
      false))

(def (build-int-match-chain b i env fenv lce slot nargs k)
  (doc "Build the if-chain for a runtime int-literal match on scrutinee LOCAL `slot`, from arm k..nargs: a
        literal arm `(v body)` → `(if (= (local slot) v) body <rest>)`; a CATCHALL `(head body)` → its body
        (a name-binding catchall reads its body with the name aliased to `slot`, since the scrutinee IS that
        local). The catchall terminates the chain. Assumes `arms-all-int-or-catchall` + a catchall exists.")
  (let ((arm-off (read-child-off b i (+ k 1))))
    (if (arm-is-catchall b arm-off)
        ; catchall body: a name-binding catchall `(k body)` binds `k` = scrutinee; wrap the body in a `let`
        ; that binds the name to the scrutinee LOCAL at a FRESH slot (`ienv-len env`), so the body reads it as
        ; an ordinary local. `_`/`else` discard → the body directly (no binding).
        (if (arm-catchall-binds-name b arm-off)
            (Node.NLet (tuple (ienv-len env)
                              (Node.NLocal slot)
                              (read-node b (arm-wildcard-body-off b arm-off)
                                         (ienv-snoc env (read-head-index b arm-off)) fenv lce)))
            (read-node b (arm-wildcard-body-off b arm-off) env fenv lce))
        ; literal arm: (if (= scrut lit) body <rest of chain>)
        (Node.NIf (tuple (Node.NPrim (tuple "=" (Node.NLocal slot) (Node.NInt (pat-const-val b (arm-pat-off b arm-off)))))
                         (read-node b (arm-body-off b arm-off) env fenv lce)
                         (build-int-match-chain b i env fenv lce slot nargs (+ k 1)))))))

(def (read-annotation b i env fenv lce)
  (doc "Read a type annotation `(: expr Type)`: the inner expr is child 1, the type-name a bare-name tag at
        child 2. For the scalar types this compiler models — `Int64` and `Bool` — check the expr's PROVABLE
        kind (`ck-of` of the folded inner Core) against the annotation: a match ERASES the annotation (yield
        the inner expr — transparent); a provable mismatch is `(rejected 203)` (`(: 42 Bool)`); an unprovable
        kind (a param) yields the inner expr conservatively (native infers it — a safe under-reject of the
        contradiction check, never a false CDZ0203). A non-Int64/Bool type name (UInt8, BigInt, `(UInt N)`,
        a compound) → DECLINE — this compiler does not model those types.")
  (let ((expr-off (read-child-off b i 1))
        (ty-off (read-child-off b i 2)))
    (if (name-annotation-is b ty-off b"Int64" 5)
        (read-annotation-checked b expr-off env fenv lce (CKind.CKi64 ()))
    (if (name-annotation-is b ty-off b"Bool" 4)
        (read-annotation-checked b expr-off env fenv lce (CKind.CKBool ()))
        (declined)))))                              ; a type this compiler doesn't model → decline

(def (name-annotation-is b ty-off name n)
  (doc "Does the annotation type node at ty-off name the scalar type `name` (n bytes)? The type is a bare
        NAME tag (major 6); resolve its prelude entry and compare. A non-tag type (a `(UInt N)` application)
        → false (declines upstream).")
  (if (= (cbor-major b ty-off) 6)
      (name-eq b (prelude-entry b (name-ref-index b ty-off)) name 0 n)
      false))

(def (read-annotation-checked b expr-off env fenv lce want)
  (doc "Given the annotation's wanted scalar kind `want` (CKi64/CKBool), read the inner expr and check its
        PROVABLE kind: a concrete mismatch → `(rejected 203)` (annotation contradicts the value); otherwise
        (match or unprovable) → the inner expr node, erasing the transparent annotation.")
  (let ((expr-node (read-node b expr-off env fenv lce)))
    (let ((k (ck-of (fold (resolve expr-node)) (list))))
      (if (and (ck-concrete k) (not (ck-eq k want)))
          (rejected 203)                            ; provable kind mismatch — CDZ0203
          expr-node))))                             ; consistent or unprovable → transparent (yield the value)

(def (read-match b i env fenv lce)
  (doc "Decode `(match scrut arm…)` — the BOOL case. Two complementary bool-literal arms desugar to
        `(if scrut true-body false-body)`. A SINGLE bool-literal arm (`(match b (true 1))`) is provably
        NON-EXHAUSTIVE — a Bool has exactly two values and the sole arm covers one — so it is REJECTED
        CDZ0210 (native's code), via `(rejected 210)`. A single CONSTRUCTOR-pattern arm DECLINES (ask-13:
        can't prove a user sum's variant count, so no exhaustiveness claim). Any other shape (≠1/2 arms,
        non-bool binary pattern, same-polarity pair) DECLINES — reject-don't-miscompile.")
  ; First: if the scrutinee folds to a CONST Int64 scalar, select the matching arm at compile time
  ; (int-literal patterns + optional `_` wildcard) — `(match 5 (5 100) (_ 200))` → 100, composing with the
  ; projection fold (`(match (. r n) …)` where `r` is a let-bound record). A NON-const scrutinee falls
  ; through to the bool path / decline. `fold-const-match` declines a non-int/non-`_` pattern (ask-13) and a
  ; non-exhaustive const match (no arm matched, no wildcard) — a safe under-reject matching native.
  (let ((scrut-core (fold (resolve (read-node b (read-child-off b i 1) env fenv lce)))))
    (if (and (const? scrut-core) (not (scrut-is-bool-const scrut-core)))
        (fold-const-match b i env fenv lce (const-val scrut-core) (app-arity b i) 1)
        ; RUNTIME int-literal match on a BARE-LOCAL scrutinee → an if-chain of `=` tests. Requires: scrutinee is
        ; a local (slot ≥ 0), every arm is an int-literal or a catchall, AND a catchall exists (exhaustive — an
        ; int type is infinite). Otherwise fall through to the bool path / decline (a sum-match, a complex
        ; scrutinee, or a non-exhaustive int-match all DECLINE — the runtime-heap / ask-13 / CDZ0210 frontier).
        ; (A COMPLEX runtime scrutinee — `(match (% n 2) …)` — needs a scrutinee-once let-bind with a
        ; non-colliding fresh slot; deferred, declines for now — a safe under-reject, native folds/dispatches it.)
        (if (and (and (>= (scrut-local-slot b i env) 0) (arms-all-int-or-catchall b i (app-arity b i) 1))
                 (match-has-catchall b i (app-arity b i) 1))
            (build-int-match-chain b i env fenv lce (scrut-local-slot b i env) (app-arity b i) 1)
        (if (= (app-arity b i) 3)
            (read-match-arms b i env fenv lce (read-child-off b i 2) (read-child-off b i 3))
        (if (= (app-arity b i) 2)
            ; one arm: a lone BOOL-literal arm is a provably non-exhaustive bool match → CDZ0210; a lone
            ; constructor arm is a user-sum match we can't prove exhaustiveness for → DECLINE (ask-13).
            (if (pat-is-bool b (arm-pat-off b (read-child-off b i 2)))
                (rejected 210)
                (declined))
            (declined)))))))                                      ; other arity — a user-sum match (ask-13), DECLINE

(def (read-match-arms b i env fenv lce a1 a2)
  (doc "Given the two arm offsets a1/a2, verify both patterns are bool literals of OPPOSITE polarity,
        then build `(if scrut true-body false-body)` picking each arm's body by its pattern polarity.
        Declines otherwise.")
  (if (and (pat-is-bool b (arm-pat-off b a1)) (pat-is-bool b (arm-pat-off b a2)))
      (if (= (pat-bool b (arm-pat-off b a1)) (pat-bool b (arm-pat-off b a2)))
          (declined)                                              ; two same-polarity arms — DECLINE (not clearly CDZ0201)
          ; arms complementary: the TRUE-arm's body is the `then`, the FALSE-arm's the `else`.
          (Node.NIf (tuple (read-node b (read-child-off b i 1) env fenv lce)
                           (read-node b (arm-body-off b (if (= (pat-bool b (arm-pat-off b a1)) 1) a1 a2)) env fenv lce)
                           (read-node b (arm-body-off b (if (= (pat-bool b (arm-pat-off b a1)) 1) a2 a1)) env fenv lce))))
      (declined)))                                                ; a non-bool (constructor) pattern — ask-13, DECLINE

(def (read-call b i env fenv lce fidx)
  (doc "Decode a call to module function `fidx` (the head resolved through fenv) — of ANY arity. The
        application `[fn-idx, arg…]` reads its operands (children 1..arity) into a `list Node` (in
        order) via `read-call-args`, yielding `NCall (fn-index, arg-list)`. A nullary call `(f)` reads
        an empty arg list. (`resolve` maps `resolve` over the list, `lower` pushes each arg then emits
        `call`, both left-to-right — wasm's calling convention.)")
  (Node.NCall (tuple fidx (read-call-args b i env fenv lce 1 (+ (app-arity b i) 1) (list)))))

(def (read-call-args b i env fenv lce k n out)
  (doc "Read a call's operands — application children k..n (child 0 is the head) at offset i — into a
        `list Node`, in order, appended to `out`. A recursive push-loop over the operands (the shape
        seed Gap A had to fix): each operand reads under the same param/function envs.")
  (if (< k n)
      (read-call-args b i env fenv lce (+ k 1) n
                      (List.push out (read-node b (read-child-off b i k) env fenv lce)))
      out))

; ─── `let`: sequential bindings over local slots ────────────────────────────
; Surface `(let ((x v)…) body)` encodes as `[let-idx, bindings, body]` where
; `bindings = [apply-idx, pair…]` and each `pair = [name-idx, value]` (an
; application headed by the binding name — the reserved `apply` head is the
; bindings-array's own head, since its elements are computed pairs, not a symbol
; call). The reader assigns each binding the next free local slot (the env's
; current length, just past the params and any enclosing lets), reads its value
; under the env AS IT STANDS (so a later binding sees an earlier one — `let*`),
; then extends the env with the binding's prelude index at that slot. Bindings
; become NESTED NLet nodes (innermost = the body), matching KLet's one-binding
; shape; sequential nesting also gives correct lexical shadowing (a re-bound name
; snocs a higher slot, and ienv-pos returns the last match).

(def (cbor-arg-len-bindings b i)
  (doc "The number of `let` bindings in the bindings-array at offset i: its CBOR array length minus 1
        (the array's own `apply` head element).")
  (- (cbor-arg b i) 1))

(def (read-let b bindings body env k n fenv lce)
  (doc "Read the k-th..n-th `let` bindings (bindings-array at `bindings`, body at `body`) into nested
        NLet nodes, under env, function env `fenv`, and literal-compound env `lce`. Binding k is bindings
        element (k+1) = `[name-idx, value]`: its slot is the env's current length; its value reads under
        the current env; the body/rest reads under the env extended with the binding name at that slot.
        CONSTANT PROPAGATION: if the binding's VALUE is a literal `(tuple …)`/`(record …)`, record its
        slot→value-offset in `lce` so a projection of the binding folds (native const-folds these to a
        scalar; the compound itself has no runtime rep here). When k = n, read the body under the full env.")
  (if (< k n)
      (let ((voff (read-child-off b (read-child-off b bindings (+ k 1)) 1))
            (slot (ienv-len env)))
        (if (or (node-head-is b voff b"tuple" 5) (node-head-is b voff b"record" 6))
            ; A LITERAL-COMPOUND binding: the compound has no runtime rep, so bind the slot to a HARMLESS
            ; placeholder (NInt 0 — a dead i64 local; slot numbering stays consistent for later bindings/body)
            ; and record slot→value-offset in `lce`. A PROJECTION of this binding folds via `compound-receiver-off`
            ; (which resolves the receiver tag through `lce` directly, never touching the local); a NON-projection
            ; use (a bare reference) resolves through read-node's NLocal path, which DECLINES a compound slot
            ; (see read-node) — so the placeholder 0 is never observed as a value. This is constant propagation of
            ; a compound-valued let into its projections, matching native's fold.
            (Node.NLet (tuple slot (Node.NInt 0)
                              (read-let b bindings body
                                        (ienv-snoc env (read-head-index b (read-child-off b bindings (+ k 1))))
                                        (+ k 1) n fenv (List.push lce (tuple slot voff)))))
            (Node.NLet (tuple slot
                              (read-node b voff env fenv lce)
                              (read-let b bindings body
                                        (ienv-snoc env (read-head-index b (read-child-off b bindings (+ k 1))))
                                        (+ k 1) n fenv lce)))))
      (read-node b body env fenv lce)))

; ─── The surface module: a list of function definitions ─────────────────────
; A whole program is `(module <name> (def (f params…) body) …)`. The reader
; produces a `Def` per definition and a `DList` (cons-list) of them — exactly what
; the CBOR decoder will yield. A `Def` is a FLAT 3-tuple `(name, param-count, body)`
; — flat so it destructures without hitting the nested-tuple-binder gap (Tier 2b);
; nesting `(name, (np, body))` declines. `resolve-module` walks the DList to the
; typed `FList` the multi-function backend (`compile-program`) consumes, resolving
; each def's body Node → Core. This closes the front end: a multi-def surface tree
; now flows all the way to component bytes.

(type Def (D (Tuple String Int64 Node)))   ; (name, param-count, body-Node)
; The reader's def list is a built-in `list<Def>` (ask-62: the custom `DList` cons-sum-type retired for
; the built-in list). Position = def index (visiting order; position 0 = main → func 0). `read-module`
; builds it, `resolve-module` walks it to a `list<Func>`.

(def (def-nparams d) (match d ((Def.D (tuple nm np body)) np)))
(def (def-body d)    (match d ((Def.D (tuple nm np body)) body)))

; ─── Core: the resolved middle IR ───────────────────────────────────────────
; A constant, or a primitive with two Core operands. The primitive is already
; RESOLVED to a distinct constructor (not a name looked up during emission) —
; this is the resolved-names-before-instruction-selection property, structurally.
; Core→Core rewrites (folding, RC, effect lowering) will live here; today it only
; lowers to Lir.

; Each node also has a KIND (i64 for arithmetic, Bool for comparison) — see
; `kind-of` below, which the framing reads to pick the result valtype bytes.
(type Core
  (KConst Int64                ; an i64 constant
   | KFloat Int64              ; a FLOAT literal — CHECK-ONLY: codegen DECLINES it (this compiler emits no float
                               ; ops, so a bare float is a decline-trap, matching the frontier), but `ck-of` reports
                               ; it as `CKFloat` so an int-vs-float mismatch (branch/arith/compare) is PROVABLE and
                               ; rejected CDZ0201/CDZ0301 like native. The Int64 payload is a placeholder (never
                               ; emitted). Distinct from `(KError 0)` so the float kind cannot poison the decline path.
   | KCompound Int64           ; a COMPOUND LITERAL (`(tuple …)`/`(record …)`/`(list …)`/`(map …)`) — CHECK-ONLY,
                               ; like KFloat: codegen DECLINES it (no value heap yet), but `ck-of` reports `CKCompound`
                               ; so a SCALAR-vs-compound mismatch (a scalar `if`-branch beside a compound one, a
                               ; compound arith/if-cond operand) is a PROVABLE reject — native rejects it, and folding
                               ; a dead compound branch away without this would MISCOMPILE (the soundness hole this
                               ; closes). Two compounds are NOT a provable mismatch (shape-dependent — same shape
                               ; compiles). Only a LITERAL compound head resolves here; a generic decline stays KError.
   | KBoolC Int64              ; a Bool constant (0=false 1=true)
   | KAdd (Tuple Core Core)    ; i64 arithmetic
   | KSub (Tuple Core Core)
   | KMul (Tuple Core Core)
   | KDiv (Tuple Core Core)
   | KMod (Tuple Core Core)
   | KBitAnd (Tuple Core Core) ; i64 bitwise AND (total)
   | KBitOr (Tuple Core Core)  ; i64 bitwise OR (total)
   | KXor (Tuple Core Core)    ; i64 bitwise XOR (total)
   | KShl (Tuple Core Core)    ; i64 << (count-guarded, overflow-guarded — traps like *)
   | KShr (Tuple Core Core)    ; i64 >> arithmetic (count-guarded; never overflows)
   | KLt (Tuple Core Core)     ; i64 comparisons → Bool
   | KGt (Tuple Core Core)
   | KLe (Tuple Core Core)
   | KGe (Tuple Core Core)
   | KEq (Tuple Core Core)
   | KNe (Tuple Core Core)
   | KNot Core                 ; Bool → Bool (logical negation)
   | KIf (Tuple Core Core Core)  ; (cond, then, else) — cond Bool, branches same Kind
   | KLocal Int64              ; a parameter/let reference (local n), i64
   | KLet (Tuple Int64 Core Core)  ; (local-index, bound-value, body) — bind local N to value, eval body
   | KCall (Tuple Int64 (list Core))  ; (fn-index, arg-list) — call an N-ary i64…→i64 function
   | KDo (list Core)           ; (do e1 … eN) — evaluate each in order (non-final results dropped), yield last
   | KError Int64))            ; a failed program — lowers to `unreachable` (a defined trap). The Int64 is the
                               ; failure KIND: 0 = DECLINE (a construct this compiler does not support yet —
                               ; a float/string/list/unbound name; native COMPILES it, we honestly decline), 1
                               ; = REJECT (a genuine type error / malformed form — native REJECTS it CDZ0201).
                               ; Only a REJECT becomes a diagnostic; a DECLINE is silent (the frontier). Both
                               ; still lower to `unreachable`. This split lets the diagnostics `check` pass emit
                               ; CDZ0201 for rejects WITHOUT false-flagging the unsupported-construct declines.

; ─── fold: Core → Core (the first Core→Core rewrite) ─────────────────────────
; Constant folding: a primitive whose operands both reduce to constants collapses
; to a single KConst. This is THE test of the resolved-IR architecture — it is a
; pure Core→Core transformation, inserted BEFORE `lower`, that touches neither
; `lower` nor `serialize` nor framing. Emission stays a serializer of whatever
; Core it is handed; folding is just a smaller Core to serialize.
;
; Bottom-up: fold each operand first, then — if both folded to KConst — evaluate
; the primitive at compile time into one KConst; otherwise rebuild the primitive
; around its folded operands (a partially-constant tree keeps its runtime parts).
; `const?`/`const-val` are the only inspection the fold needs; everything else is
; ordinary constructor/`match` work.

(def (const? node)
  (doc "Does this Core node denote a compile-time constant (a KConst or KBoolC)? A KFloat is NOT a fold
        constant (this compiler never folds float ops) — false, so the folder leaves it for codegen to decline.")
  (match node
    ((Core.KConst n)  true)
    ((Core.KFloat n)  false)
    ((Core.KCompound n)  false)
    ((Core.KBoolC n)  true)
    ((Core.KAdd p)    false)
    ((Core.KSub p)    false)
    ((Core.KMul p)    false)
    ((Core.KDiv p)    false)
    ((Core.KMod p)    false)
    ((Core.KBitAnd p) false)
    ((Core.KBitOr p)  false)
    ((Core.KXor p)    false)
    ((Core.KShl p)    false)
    ((Core.KShr p)    false)
    ((Core.KLt p)     false)
    ((Core.KGt p)     false)
    ((Core.KLe p)     false)
    ((Core.KGe p)     false)
    ((Core.KEq p)     false)
    ((Core.KNe p)     false)
    ((Core.KNot p)    false)
    ((Core.KIf p)     false)
    ((Core.KLocal n)  false)
    ((Core.KLet p)    false)
    ((Core.KCall p)   false)
    ((Core.KDo p)     false)
    ((Core.KError _)  false)))

(def (const-val node)
  (doc "The Int64 a KConst/KBoolC carries (0 for a non-constant — callers guard with const?).")
  (match node
    ((Core.KConst n)  n)
    ((Core.KFloat n)  0)
    ((Core.KCompound n)  0)
    ((Core.KBoolC n)  n)
    ((Core.KAdd p)    0)
    ((Core.KSub p)    0)
    ((Core.KMul p)    0)
    ((Core.KDiv p)    0)
    ((Core.KMod p)    0)
    ((Core.KBitAnd p) 0)
    ((Core.KBitOr p)  0)
    ((Core.KXor p)    0)
    ((Core.KShl p)    0)
    ((Core.KShr p)    0)
    ((Core.KLt p)     0)
    ((Core.KGt p)     0)
    ((Core.KLe p)     0)
    ((Core.KGe p)     0)
    ((Core.KEq p)     0)
    ((Core.KNe p)     0)
    ((Core.KNot p)    0)
    ((Core.KIf p)     0)
    ((Core.KLocal n)  0)
    ((Core.KLet p)    0)
    ((Core.KCall p)   0)
    ((Core.KDo p)     0)
    ((Core.KError _)  0)))

(def (fold-bin build a b op)
  (doc "Fold one binary primitive: recurse into both operands; if both are constant, evaluate
        `op` on their values into a KConst; otherwise rebuild the primitive (`build`) around the
        folded operands so the runtime parts survive.")
  (fold-bin-go build (fold a) (fold b) op))

(def (fold-bin-go build fa fb op)
  (doc "The join step, over already-folded operands `fa`/`fb`. Split out so each operand is named
        once (referenced twice: for const? and for const-val / rebuild).")
  (if (and (const? fa) (const? fb))
      (Core.KConst (op (const-val fa) (const-val fb)))
      (build fa fb)))

(def (mk-add a b) (Core.KAdd (tuple a b)))
(def (mk-sub a b) (Core.KSub (tuple a b)))
(def (mk-mul a b) (Core.KMul (tuple a b)))
(def (mk-div a b) (Core.KDiv (tuple a b)))
(def (mk-mod a b) (Core.KMod (tuple a b)))
(def (mk-bitand a b) (Core.KBitAnd (tuple a b)))
(def (mk-bitor a b)  (Core.KBitOr (tuple a b)))
(def (mk-xor a b)    (Core.KXor (tuple a b)))
(def (mk-shl a b) (Core.KShl (tuple a b)))
(def (mk-shr a b) (Core.KShr (tuple a b)))
(def (mk-lt a b)  (Core.KLt (tuple a b)))
(def (mk-gt a b)  (Core.KGt (tuple a b)))
(def (mk-le a b)  (Core.KLe (tuple a b)))
(def (mk-ge a b)  (Core.KGe (tuple a b)))
(def (mk-eq a b)  (Core.KEq (tuple a b)))
(def (mk-ne a b)  (Core.KNe (tuple a b)))

(def (op-add a b) (+ a b))
(def (op-sub a b) (- a b))
(def (op-mul a b) (* a b))
(def (op-div a b) (/ a b))
(def (op-mod a b) (% a b))
(def (op-bitand a b) (& a b))
(def (op-bitor a b)  (| a b))
(def (op-xor a b)    (^ a b))
(def (op-shr a b) (>> a b))    ; const arithmetic right shift — count already checked in-range by fold-shift

; A comparison folds two i64 constants to a BOOL constant (KBoolC 0/1), not a KConst
; — the result kind is Bool, so the folded leaf must carry that kind for `kind-of`
; and the framing to pick the i32/bool valtypes. `bool01` maps the Cadenza Bool the
; comparison yields to the 0/1 an i32 Bool leaf holds.
(def (bool01 b) (if b 1 0))
(def (fold-cmp build a b keep)
  (doc "Fold a comparison primitive: recurse into operands; if both are constant, evaluate the
        comparison to a KBoolC; else rebuild. `keep` is the comparison on the two constant values.")
  (fold-cmp-go build (fold a) (fold b) keep))
(def (fold-cmp-go build fa fb keep)
  (if (and (const? fa) (const? fb))
      (Core.KBoolC (bool01 (keep (const-val fa) (const-val fb))))
      (build fa fb)))
(def (cmp-lt a b) (< a b))
(def (cmp-gt a b) (> a b))
(def (cmp-le a b) (<= a b))
(def (cmp-ge a b) (>= a b))
(def (cmp-eq a b) (= a b))
(def (cmp-ne a b) (not (= a b)))

; `not` folds a constant Bool by flipping its 0/1; a runtime Bool is preserved.
(def (fold-not a)
  (doc "Fold logical negation: a constant Bool flips (1↔0); otherwise keep the KNot around the
        folded operand.")
  (fold-not-go (fold a)))
(def (fold-not-go fa)
  (if (const? fa)
      (Core.KBoolC (if (= (const-val fa) 0) 1 0))
      (Core.KNot fa)))

; `if` folds when its CONDITION is a constant: pick the taken branch, DROP the
; other. This is sound and short-circuiting — the dropped branch is never emitted,
; so a trap/effect in it does not occur, exactly as run-time short-circuit `if`
; specifies. A runtime condition keeps all three (folded) sub-trees.
(def (fold-if c t f)
  (doc "Fold a conditional: if the condition folds to a constant Bool, become the taken branch
        (folded); else rebuild KIf around the three folded sub-trees.")
  (fold-if-go (fold c) (fold t) (fold f)))
(def (fold-if-go fc ft ff)
  (if (const? fc)
      (if (= (const-val fc) 0) ff ft)
      (Core.KIf (tuple fc ft ff))))

; Division and modulo TRAP at run time on a zero divisor (and div overflows on
; INT64_MIN / -1). Folding such a case would change the program's meaning — a
; runtime trap must not become a compile-time value (nor a compile-time trap the
; source did not ask for). So the fold is GUARDED: it collapses `(/ c d)` /
; `(% c d)` to a constant ONLY when the divisor is a non-zero constant that does
; not trigger the overflow case; otherwise the primitive is preserved and the trap
; happens at run time exactly as written. This is a semantics-preservation
; obligation on the rewrite (spec/learnings optimizing-compiler catalog #6), made
; concrete: the pass may only fold operations that cannot trap.

(def (fold-div build a b op)
  (doc "Fold a division/modulo primitive, preserving a run-time trap: fold to a constant only when
        the divisor is a non-zero, non-overflowing constant; otherwise keep the primitive so the
        trap occurs at run time as the source specified.")
  (fold-div-go build (fold a) (fold b) op))

(def (fold-div-go build fa fb op)
  (doc "Join step for division/modulo folding — both operands already folded.")
  (if (and (and (const? fa) (const? fb)) (foldable-divisor (const-val fa) (const-val fb)))
      (Core.KConst (op (const-val fa) (const-val fb)))
      (build fa fb)))

(def (foldable-divisor num den)
  (doc "May `(/ num den)` / `(% num den)` be folded at compile time? Only when the divisor is
        non-zero AND the pair is not the INT64_MIN / -1 overflow case (both of which TRAP at run
        time, so folding them would change the program's observable behavior).")
  (and (not (= den 0))
       (not (and (= num -9223372036854775808) (= den -1)))))

; ─── Overflow-safe folding for + - * ─────────────────────────────────────────
; `+`/`-`/`*` on two constants must fold ONLY when the result fits Int64 — an
; overflowing `(* Int64.min -1)` / `(+ Int64.max 1)` MUST trap at RUN TIME (numeric
; model), so folding it would (a) change observable behavior and (b) TRAP THE
; COMPILER (the seed's `+`/`*` themselves trap on overflow, so `op-mul` would abort
; the fold, not the program). The overflow PREDICATES below never perform the
; trapping op — they test with a safe rearrangement (compare against MAX-b / MIN-b,
; or divide, which is safe for the guarded operands) — mirroring `foldable-divisor`.
(def (imax) 9223372036854775807)
(def (imin) -9223372036854775808)

(def (add-overflows a b)
  (doc "Does `a + b` overflow Int64? Positive overflow iff b>0 ∧ a>MAX−b; negative iff b<0 ∧ a<MIN−b.
        (MAX−b is safe when b>0; MIN−b is safe when b<0 — neither subtraction overflows.)")
  (if (> b 0) (> a (- (imax) b))
  (if (< b 0) (< a (- (imin) b))
              false)))

(def (sub-overflows a b)
  (doc "Does `a − b` overflow Int64? `a − b` = `a + (−b)`; but −MIN overflows, so handle b=MIN
        specially (a−MIN overflows unless a<0 makes it… a−MIN = a+|MIN| which overflows iff a≥0…
        simpler: a−MIN overflows iff a > −1, i.e. a ≥ 0). Otherwise reduce to add-overflows a (−b).")
  (if (= b (imin))
      (> a -1)                              ; a − MIN overflows exactly when a ≥ 0
      (add-overflows a (- 0 b))))

(def (mul-overflows a b)
  (doc "Does `a * b` overflow Int64? Zero never overflows. The MIN × −1 (and −1 × MIN) case overflows.
        Otherwise a*b overflows iff a > MAX/b or a < MIN/b (sign-aware via truncating division, which
        is safe for a non-zero, non-(MIN,−1) divisor `b`). Never performs the trapping `*`.")
  (if (= a 0) false
  (if (= b 0) false
  (if (and (= a (imin)) (= b -1)) true
  (if (and (= b (imin)) (= a -1)) true
  (if (> b 0)
      (if (> a (/ (imax) b)) true (< a (/ (imin) b)))
      (if (< b (- 0 1))
          (if (< a (/ (imax) b)) true (> a (/ (imin) b)))
          false)))))))                       ; b = -1 (with a ≠ MIN) never overflows

(def (fold-checked build a b op overflows)
  (doc "Fold a `+`/`-`/`*` primitive, preserving a run-time overflow trap: recurse into operands; if
        both are constant AND `overflows` says the result fits, evaluate `op` into a KConst; else keep
        the primitive so the overflow traps at run time exactly as the source specifies.")
  (fold-checked-go build (fold a) (fold b) op overflows))
(def (fold-checked-go build fa fb op overflows)
  (if (and (and (const? fa) (const? fb)) (not (overflows (const-val fa) (const-val fb))))
      (Core.KConst (op (const-val fa) (const-val fb)))
      (build fa fb)))

; ─── Shift folding (count-range + left-shift-overflow guarded, non-trapping) ──
; A shift count outside [0,64) has no defined value (#Overflow Is Defined); a left
; shift is exact ×2^count, so it overflows exactly when that product does. Both are
; tested WITHOUT performing the trapping op (mirroring add/mul-overflows): the count
; by range, the left-shift overflow by comparing `a` against MAX>>count / MIN>>count
; (arithmetic `>>`, safe for 0≤count<64). If either would trap, KEEP the primitive so
; the run-time guard traps exactly as the const path's spec requires (const/runtime agree).
(def (count-out-of-range b)
  (doc "Is shift count `b` outside [0,64)? Both a negative count and a count ≥ 64 are undefined.")
  (or (< b 0) (>= b 64)))

(def (shl-overflows a b)
  (doc "Does `a << b` overflow Int64 (assuming b already in [0,64))? A left shift is ×2^b, which fits
        iff MIN/2^b ≤ a ≤ MAX/2^b; `MAX >> b` / `MIN >> b` (arithmetic, safe for b in range) are those
        bounds. Never performs the trapping `<<`.")
  (or (> a (>> (imax) b)) (< a (>> (imin) b))))

(def (fold-shl a b)
  (doc "Fold `(<< a b)`: fold operands; if both constant, the count is in range, AND the shift does not
        overflow, evaluate to `a << b` via a bounds-checked multiply-by-2^b path (op-shl); else keep the
        primitive so the run-time guard traps (out-of-range count or overflow).")
  (fold-shl-go (fold a) (fold b)))
(def (fold-shl-go fa fb)
  (if (and (and (const? fa) (const? fb))
           (and (not (count-out-of-range (const-val fb)))
                (not (shl-overflows (const-val fa) (const-val fb)))))
      (Core.KConst (op-shl (const-val fa) (const-val fb)))
      (mk-shl fa fb)))
; `a << b` for an in-range, non-overflowing (a,b): the seed's own `<<` (which traps on overflow / bad
; count). fold-shl-go only calls this once it has ruled out BOTH, so it never traps here — and using the
; seed's shift means the fold matches the seed's const path bit-for-bit (const/runtime agree).
(def (op-shl a b) (<< a b))

(def (fold-shr a b)
  (doc "Fold `(>> a b)`: fold operands; if both constant AND the count is in range, evaluate to the
        arithmetic `a >> b`; else keep the primitive so the run-time count guard traps. A right shift
        never overflows — the only guard is the count range.")
  (fold-shr-go (fold a) (fold b)))
(def (fold-shr-go fa fb)
  (if (and (and (const? fa) (const? fb)) (not (count-out-of-range (const-val fb))))
      (Core.KConst (op-shr (const-val fa) (const-val fb)))
      (mk-shr fa fb)))

; ─── let constant-propagation (fold a constant-bound let into its body) ───────
; `(let ((x V)) body)` where V folds to a CONSTANT: substitute V for `x` (`KLocal
; ix`) throughout body AND DROP the slot, so `(let ((x 10)) (+ x 5))` folds to `15`
; (no local round-trip) — matching native's const path. Sound because V is a constant
; (pure — no re-evaluation / effect-ordering concern). ⚠️ Dropping slot `ix` SHIFTS the
; local-index space: every remaining slot ABOVE `ix` (a `KLocal j`/`KLet j` with j > ix)
; must DECREMENT by 1, or `count-lets` (which counts the FEWER remaining lets) would
; declare too few locals and a `local.get j` would be out of bounds — an INVALID
; component (the exact bug an un-reindexed substitution caused). `subst-reindex` does
; both in one walk: `KLocal ix`→val, and any slot > ix shifts down. A NON-constant V
; keeps the `let` (substituting a computed value could duplicate work / reorder effects).
(def (shift-slot n ix)
  (doc "A local index `n` after slot `ix` is removed: unchanged if below `ix`, decremented if above.
        (`n = ix` is never asked — that reference was replaced by the substituted value.)")
  (if (> n ix) (- n 1) n))

(def (subst-reindex node ix val)
  (doc "Substitute `KLocal ix`→`val` (a constant) AND reindex every other local slot down by one if it is
        above `ix` (because slot `ix` is being removed). Recurses all node shapes. Keeps the local-index
        space contiguous so `count-lets` still matches the max slot used.")
  (match node
    ((Core.KConst n)  (Core.KConst n))
    ((Core.KFloat n)  (Core.KFloat n))
    ((Core.KCompound n)  (Core.KCompound n))
    ((Core.KBoolC n)  (Core.KBoolC n))
    ((Core.KLocal n)  (if (= n ix) val (Core.KLocal (shift-slot n ix))))
    ((Core.KAdd (tuple a b)) (Core.KAdd (tuple (subst-reindex a ix val) (subst-reindex b ix val))))
    ((Core.KSub (tuple a b)) (Core.KSub (tuple (subst-reindex a ix val) (subst-reindex b ix val))))
    ((Core.KMul (tuple a b)) (Core.KMul (tuple (subst-reindex a ix val) (subst-reindex b ix val))))
    ((Core.KDiv (tuple a b)) (Core.KDiv (tuple (subst-reindex a ix val) (subst-reindex b ix val))))
    ((Core.KMod (tuple a b)) (Core.KMod (tuple (subst-reindex a ix val) (subst-reindex b ix val))))
    ((Core.KBitAnd (tuple a b)) (Core.KBitAnd (tuple (subst-reindex a ix val) (subst-reindex b ix val))))
    ((Core.KBitOr (tuple a b))  (Core.KBitOr (tuple (subst-reindex a ix val) (subst-reindex b ix val))))
    ((Core.KXor (tuple a b))    (Core.KXor (tuple (subst-reindex a ix val) (subst-reindex b ix val))))
    ((Core.KShl (tuple a b)) (Core.KShl (tuple (subst-reindex a ix val) (subst-reindex b ix val))))
    ((Core.KShr (tuple a b)) (Core.KShr (tuple (subst-reindex a ix val) (subst-reindex b ix val))))
    ((Core.KLt (tuple a b))  (Core.KLt (tuple (subst-reindex a ix val) (subst-reindex b ix val))))
    ((Core.KGt (tuple a b))  (Core.KGt (tuple (subst-reindex a ix val) (subst-reindex b ix val))))
    ((Core.KLe (tuple a b))  (Core.KLe (tuple (subst-reindex a ix val) (subst-reindex b ix val))))
    ((Core.KGe (tuple a b))  (Core.KGe (tuple (subst-reindex a ix val) (subst-reindex b ix val))))
    ((Core.KEq (tuple a b))  (Core.KEq (tuple (subst-reindex a ix val) (subst-reindex b ix val))))
    ((Core.KNe (tuple a b))  (Core.KNe (tuple (subst-reindex a ix val) (subst-reindex b ix val))))
    ((Core.KNot a)           (Core.KNot (subst-reindex a ix val)))
    ((Core.KIf (tuple c t f)) (Core.KIf (tuple (subst-reindex c ix val) (subst-reindex t ix val) (subst-reindex f ix val))))
    ; A nested let's binding slot `j` is always > ix (reader assigns sequentially), so it too shifts down.
    ((Core.KLet (tuple j v body)) (Core.KLet (tuple (shift-slot j ix) (subst-reindex v ix val) (subst-reindex body ix val))))
    ((Core.KCall (tuple fi args)) (Core.KCall (tuple fi (subst-reindex-args args 0 ix val (list)))))
    ((Core.KDo forms) (Core.KDo (subst-reindex-args forms 0 ix val (list))))
    ((Core.KError k)  (Core.KError k))))

(def (subst-reindex-args args i ix val out)
  (doc "Apply `subst-reindex` across an arg/form list (a `list Core`), preserving order.")
  (match (List.at args i)
    ((Some a) (subst-reindex-args args (+ i 1) ix val (List.push out (subst-reindex a ix val))))
    (None out)))

(def (fold-let ix fv body)
  (doc "Fold a `let`, given its ALREADY-FOLDED value `fv`. If `fv` is a CONSTANT, substitute it for the
        binding throughout the body, REINDEX the remaining slots down (slot `ix` is removed), and fold the
        result — the binding disappears (constant propagation, matching native: `(let ((x 10)) (+ x 5))` →
        15). Otherwise keep the `let`, folding its body.")
  (if (const? fv)
      (fold (subst-reindex body ix fv))
      (Core.KLet (tuple ix fv (fold body)))))

(def (fold node)
  (doc "Constant-fold a Core tree bottom-up: a non-trapping primitive over two constants becomes
        one KConst; a division/modulo folds only when it cannot trap (guarded); a constant is
        already reduced. The canonical Core→Core rewrite — same type in and out, composable with
        any other Core→Core pass, invisible to emission, and semantics-preserving (a run-time trap
        stays a run-time trap).")
  (match node
    ((Core.KConst n)         (Core.KConst n))
    ((Core.KFloat n)         (Core.KFloat n))
    ((Core.KCompound n)      (Core.KCompound n))
    ((Core.KBoolC n)         (Core.KBoolC n))
    ((Core.KAdd (tuple a b)) (fold-checked mk-add a b op-add add-overflows))
    ((Core.KSub (tuple a b)) (fold-checked mk-sub a b op-sub sub-overflows))
    ((Core.KMul (tuple a b)) (fold-checked mk-mul a b op-mul mul-overflows))
    ((Core.KDiv (tuple a b)) (fold-div mk-div a b op-div))
    ((Core.KMod (tuple a b)) (fold-div mk-mod a b op-mod))
    ((Core.KBitAnd (tuple a b)) (fold-bin mk-bitand a b op-bitand))
    ((Core.KBitOr (tuple a b))  (fold-bin mk-bitor a b op-bitor))
    ((Core.KXor (tuple a b))    (fold-bin mk-xor a b op-xor))
    ((Core.KShl (tuple a b)) (fold-shl a b))
    ((Core.KShr (tuple a b)) (fold-shr a b))
    ((Core.KLt (tuple a b))  (fold-cmp mk-lt a b cmp-lt))
    ((Core.KGt (tuple a b))  (fold-cmp mk-gt a b cmp-gt))
    ((Core.KLe (tuple a b))  (fold-cmp mk-le a b cmp-le))
    ((Core.KGe (tuple a b))  (fold-cmp mk-ge a b cmp-ge))
    ((Core.KEq (tuple a b))  (fold-cmp mk-eq a b cmp-eq))
    ((Core.KNe (tuple a b))  (fold-cmp mk-ne a b cmp-ne))
    ((Core.KNot a)           (fold-not a))
    ((Core.KIf (tuple c t f)) (fold-if c t f))
    ; A parameter reference is irreducible. A call folds its argument but stays a call —
    ; the callee's body is not inlined (that would reintroduce the exponential blowup and,
    ; for a recursive callee, not terminate); the call is a real runtime `call`.
    ((Core.KLocal n)         (Core.KLocal n))
    ((Core.KLet (tuple ix v body)) (fold-let ix (fold v) body))
    ((Core.KCall (tuple fi args)) (Core.KCall (tuple fi (fold-args args 0 (list)))))
    ; Fold each form of a `do`, but KEEP them all — a non-final form must still be EVALUATED at run time
    ; (it may trap, e.g. `(do (+ MAX 1) 3)`), so it is not dropped at fold time; `lower` drops its RESULT.
    ((Core.KDo forms) (Core.KDo (fold-args forms 0 (list))))
    ((Core.KError k)         (Core.KError k))))

(def (fold-args args i out)
  (doc "Fold each argument of a call's arg list (a `list Core`), preserving order: append `fold` of
        each to `out`. A partially-constant argument keeps its runtime parts; the call itself never
        folds (no inlining).")
  (match (List.at args i)
    ((Some a) (fold-args args (+ i 1) (List.push out (fold a))))
    (None out)))

; ─── lower: Core → Lir (a Core→Lir transformation) ──────────────────────────
; Post-order: lower each operand, then append the primitive's instruction. Stack
; discipline matches wasm — operands pushed, then the binary op consumes them.

(def (code-cat xs ys)
  (doc "Append two Code lists (xs ++ ys). Code is a built-in `list<Instr>` (ask-62 step 3: the custom
        `Code` cons-sum-type retired for the built-in list); append ys onto xs element-by-element with
        List.push (the surface has no List.concat).")
  (code-cat-go xs ys 0 (List.len ys)))
(def (code-cat-go xs ys i n)
  (if (< i n)
      (code-cat-go (match (List.at ys i) ((None _) xs) ((Some e) (List.push xs e))) ys (+ i 1) n)
      xs))

(def (one i)
  (doc "The singleton Code holding one instruction — a one-element built-in list.")
  (List.push (list) i))

(def (cs2 a b) (code-cat a b))
(def (cs3 a b c) (code-cat a (code-cat b c)))
(def (seq is)
  (doc "A Code from a built-in `list` of Instrs, in order — for the fixed instruction sequences of the
        overflow-check helpers. Code IS a `list<Instr>` now, so this is the identity.")
  is)

; ─── Overflow-checked + - * (inline guards over 3 scratch locals) ────────────
; wasm `i64.add/sub/mul` WRAP on overflow; the numeric model requires `+ - *` to
; TRAP (numeric-model §Overflow Is Defined; `/ %` already trap via the opcode). So
; each runtime `+ - *` lowers to: compute the wrapping result into a scratch local,
; test for signed overflow, `unreachable` if overflowed, else leave the result. This
; matches the native seed's `checked_add/sub/mul` helper bodies exactly, but emitted
; INLINE (this compiler has no cross-function helper mechanism yet). `sb` is the base
; index of 3 scratch i64 locals reserved past the params + let-locals (a=sb, b=sb+1,
; r=sb+2); a nested checked op fully drains its scratch before an enclosing op uses
; it, so 3 slots shared across the whole body suffice. The a/b operands arrive on the
; stack (already lowered) and are stashed to sb/sb+1 first.
(def (checked-add sb)
  (doc "Overflow-checked i64.add over operands already on the stack. r=a+b; overflow iff (a^r)&(b^r)<0.")
  (seq (list (Instr.ISet (+ sb 1)) (Instr.ISet sb)                 ; b→sb+1, a→sb (pop reverse)
             (Instr.ILocal sb) (Instr.ILocal (+ sb 1)) (Instr.IAdd ()) (Instr.ISet (+ sb 2))  ; r=a+b
             (Instr.ILocal sb) (Instr.ILocal (+ sb 2)) (Instr.IXor ())        ; a^r
             (Instr.ILocal (+ sb 1)) (Instr.ILocal (+ sb 2)) (Instr.IXor ())  ; b^r
             (Instr.IAnd ()) (Instr.IConst 0) (Instr.ILt ())        ; (a^r)&(b^r) < 0 ?
             (Instr.IIfVoid ()) (Instr.IUnreachable ()) (Instr.IEnd ())        ; if so → trap
             (Instr.ILocal (+ sb 2)))))                             ; else result r
(def (checked-sub sb)
  (doc "Overflow-checked i64.sub. r=a-b; overflow iff (a^b)&(a^r)<0.")
  (seq (list (Instr.ISet (+ sb 1)) (Instr.ISet sb)
             (Instr.ILocal sb) (Instr.ILocal (+ sb 1)) (Instr.ISub ()) (Instr.ISet (+ sb 2))  ; r=a-b
             (Instr.ILocal sb) (Instr.ILocal (+ sb 1)) (Instr.IXor ())        ; a^b
             (Instr.ILocal sb) (Instr.ILocal (+ sb 2)) (Instr.IXor ())        ; a^r
             (Instr.IAnd ()) (Instr.IConst 0) (Instr.ILt ())
             (Instr.IIfVoid ()) (Instr.IUnreachable ()) (Instr.IEnd ())
             (Instr.ILocal (+ sb 2)))))
(def (checked-mul sb)
  (doc "Overflow-checked i64.mul. r=a*b; if a==0 → 0; else if (r/a)!=b → trap; else r. (a==-1,b==MIN is
        caught by i64.div_s trapping on MIN/-1.) ⚠️ This is INLINE (not a standalone function), so it
        must NOT use `return` (that would exit the ENCLOSING function). The a==0 test is a value-yielding
        `if (result i64) … else …` whose both arms leave r on the stack — the div-by-zero guard for the
        non-zero arm — so the whole sequence leaves exactly one i64.")
  (seq (list (Instr.ISet (+ sb 1)) (Instr.ISet sb)
             (Instr.ILocal sb) (Instr.ILocal (+ sb 1)) (Instr.IMul ()) (Instr.ISet (+ sb 2))  ; r=a*b
             (Instr.ILocal sb) (Instr.IEqz64 ())                    ; a == 0 ?
             (Instr.IIf (Kind.Ki64 ()))                             ; if (result i64)
               (Instr.ILocal (+ sb 2))                              ;   then: r (which is 0)
             (Instr.IElse ())                                       ; else:
               (Instr.ILocal (+ sb 2)) (Instr.ILocal sb) (Instr.IDiv ()) (Instr.ILocal (+ sb 1)) (Instr.INe ())  ;   (r/a)!=b ?
               (Instr.IIfVoid ()) (Instr.IUnreachable ()) (Instr.IEnd ())  ;   → trap
               (Instr.ILocal (+ sb 2))                              ;   else r
             (Instr.IEnd ()))))                                     ; end outer if — leaves one i64

(def (binop a b instr ktab sb)
  (doc "Lower a binary primitive: lhs-code ++ rhs-code ++ [instr] — operands pushed, then the op
        consumes them (wasm stack discipline). `ktab` = return-kind table; `sb` = scratch-local base
        (threaded but unused by the non-overflowing ops; the checked +/-/* use it directly).")
  (code-cat (lower a ktab sb) (code-cat (lower b ktab sb) (one instr))))

(def (checked-guard op sb)
  (doc "The inline overflow-guard Code for checked op `op` (0=add, 1=sub, 2=mul) over scratch base `sb`.
        A plain integer-tag dispatch (NOT a function-valued arg) — the seed's higher-order-call path is
        fragile (see the HOF asks), and an opcode tag is the ordinary way to select a lowering anyway.")
  (if (= op 0) (checked-add sb)
    (if (= op 1) (checked-sub sb) (checked-mul sb))))

(def (checked-binop a b op ktab sb)
  (doc "Lower an OVERFLOW-CHECKED binary op (`+`/`-`/`*`, selected by `op` = 0/1/2): lhs ++ rhs onto the
        stack, then the inline guard `checked-guard op sb` which stashes the operands to scratch,
        computes, and traps on overflow. `/` `%` do NOT use this (the opcode already traps).")
  (code-cat (lower a ktab sb) (code-cat (lower b ktab sb) (checked-guard op sb))))

; ─── Guarded shifts (<< >>) over the 3 scratch locals ────────────────────────
; wasm `i64.shl`/`i64.shr_s` MASK the count mod 64 (so `<< 64` == `<< 0`, a negative
; count folds into 0..63) and `i64.shl` WRAPS on overflow — both undefined outcomes
; #Overflow Is Defined forbids. So each shift lowers to: stash value→sb, count→sb+1;
; a COUNT GUARD (`count >=u 64` catches ≥64 AND negatives-as-huge-unsigned → trap); then
; the raw shift. `<<` adds an OVERFLOW GUARD: a left shift is exact ×2^count, so it
; overflows iff arithmetic-shifting the result back by the count does not recover the
; value — save result→sb+2, test `(result >> count) != value`, trap on mismatch, push
; result. Matches the seed's `gen_shift` exactly (const/runtime paths agree). Operands
; arrive on the stack (value, then count), so the count is popped to sb+1 first.
(def (shift-count-guard sb)
  (doc "Code that stashes value→sb, count→sb+1, then traps if the count is out of [0,64) (unsigned
        ≥ 64 catches both ≥64 and a negative count as a huge unsigned value).")
  (seq (list (Instr.ISet (+ sb 1)) (Instr.ISet sb)                    ; count→sb+1, value→sb (pop reverse)
             (Instr.ILocal (+ sb 1)) (Instr.IConst 64) (Instr.IGeU ()) ; count >=u 64 ?
             (Instr.IIfVoid ()) (Instr.IUnreachable ()) (Instr.IEnd ())))) ; → trap

(def (shr-guard sb)
  (doc "Arithmetic right shift: count guard, then `value >> count` (i64.shr_s). No overflow guard —
        a right shift only discards low bits.")
  (code-cat (shift-count-guard sb)
            (seq (list (Instr.ILocal sb) (Instr.ILocal (+ sb 1)) (Instr.IShrS ())))))

(def (shl-guard sb)
  (doc "Left shift: count guard, compute `value << count` (i64.shl) into sb+2, then the overflow guard
        `(result >> count) != value` → trap, else leave result.")
  (code-cat (shift-count-guard sb)
    (seq (list (Instr.ILocal sb) (Instr.ILocal (+ sb 1)) (Instr.IShl ()) (Instr.ISet (+ sb 2))  ; r = value<<count
               (Instr.ILocal (+ sb 2)) (Instr.ILocal (+ sb 1)) (Instr.IShrS ())                 ; r >> count
               (Instr.ILocal sb) (Instr.INe ())                                                 ; (r>>count) != value ?
               (Instr.IIfVoid ()) (Instr.IUnreachable ()) (Instr.IEnd ())                        ; → trap (overflowed)
               (Instr.ILocal (+ sb 2))))))                                                       ; else result

(def (shift-binop a b left ktab sb)
  (doc "Lower a guarded shift: value ++ count onto the stack, then the guard sequence (`shl-guard` if
        `left`, else `shr-guard`).")
  (code-cat (lower a ktab sb) (code-cat (lower b ktab sb) (if left (shl-guard sb) (shr-guard sb)))))

(def (lower node ktab sb)
  (doc "Lower a Core node to a Code (Instr cons-list): a constant is one const instr; a binary
        primitive is lhs ++ rhs ++ [op]; a conditional brackets its branches with if/else/end. `ktab`
        (function return-kind table) is threaded so an `if` whose branch is a CALL emits the correct
        blocktype (the callee's return kind).")
  (match node
    ((Core.KConst n)         (one (Instr.IConst n)))
    ((Core.KFloat n)         (one (Instr.IUnreachable ())))   ; DECLINE — this compiler emits no float ops (frontier)
    ((Core.KCompound n)      (one (Instr.IUnreachable ())))   ; DECLINE — no value-heap emission yet (M2 frontier)
    ((Core.KBoolC n)         (one (Instr.IBool n)))
    ; `+ - *` OVERFLOW-TRAP (numeric-model §Overflow Is Defined) via inline checked guards over the 3
    ; scratch locals at base `sb`. `/ %` trap via the opcode itself (no guard). Bitwise/comparisons are total.
    ((Core.KAdd (tuple a b)) (checked-binop a b 0 ktab sb))
    ((Core.KSub (tuple a b)) (checked-binop a b 1 ktab sb))
    ((Core.KMul (tuple a b)) (checked-binop a b 2 ktab sb))
    ((Core.KDiv (tuple a b)) (binop a b (Instr.IDiv ()) ktab sb))
    ((Core.KMod (tuple a b)) (binop a b (Instr.IMod ()) ktab sb))
    ; `<< >>` guard the count (out-of-range → trap) and, for `<<`, overflow — via the 3 scratch locals.
    ((Core.KShl (tuple a b)) (shift-binop a b true ktab sb))
    ((Core.KShr (tuple a b)) (shift-binop a b false ktab sb))
    ((Core.KBitAnd (tuple a b)) (binop a b (Instr.IAnd ()) ktab sb))
    ((Core.KBitOr (tuple a b))  (binop a b (Instr.IOr ()) ktab sb))
    ((Core.KXor (tuple a b))    (binop a b (Instr.IXor ()) ktab sb))
    ((Core.KLt (tuple a b))  (binop a b (Instr.ILt ()) ktab sb))
    ((Core.KGt (tuple a b))  (binop a b (Instr.IGt ()) ktab sb))
    ((Core.KLe (tuple a b))  (binop a b (Instr.ILe ()) ktab sb))
    ((Core.KGe (tuple a b))  (binop a b (Instr.IGe ()) ktab sb))
    ((Core.KEq (tuple a b))  (binop a b (Instr.IEq ()) ktab sb))
    ((Core.KNe (tuple a b))  (binop a b (Instr.INe ()) ktab sb))
    ; `not` needs a Bool (i32) operand. A param used as `(not x)` is a Bool-position use of an i64
    ; param (the definition-side twin of the Bool-arg call decline): `kind-of` reads it Ki64, so the
    ; i32.eqz would consume an i64 → invalid. DECLINE (→ unreachable) rather than emit invalid bytes;
    ; the faithful fix is per-param kind inference (infer the param i32), which this compiler lacks (ask-34).
    ((Core.KNot a)
      (match (kind-of a ktab)
        ((Kind.KBool _) (code-cat (lower a ktab sb) (one (Instr.INot ()))))
        ((Kind.Ki64 _)  (one (Instr.IUnreachable ())))))
    ; A conditional: push the condition, then a structured if/else/end whose blocktype
    ; is the branches' result Kind. `then`/`else` code sits between the brackets, each
    ; leaving one value of that Kind on the stack. cond-code ++ [if k] ++ then ++ [else] ++ else ++ [end].
    ; The condition MUST be Bool (i32) — wasm `if` consumes an i32. A condition that `kind-of`s as Ki64
    ; (e.g. a bare i64 parameter used as `(if x …)`) would push an i64 where i32 is needed → invalid. So
    ; DECLINE when the condition is not Bool-kinded (decline-don't-miscompile; ask-34, param-kind gap).
    ((Core.KIf (tuple c t f))
      (match (kind-of c ktab)
        ((Kind.Ki64 _) (one (Instr.IUnreachable ())))
        ((Kind.KBool _)
          (code-cat (lower c ktab sb)
            (code-cat (one (Instr.IIf (kind-of t ktab)))
              (code-cat (lower t ktab sb)
                (code-cat (one (Instr.IElse ()))
                  (code-cat (lower f ktab sb) (one (Instr.IEnd ()))))))))))
    ; A parameter reference reads its local; a call pushes its arg then calls the function index.
    ((Core.KLocal n)         (one (Instr.ILocal n)))
    ; A let: evaluate the bound value, store it in local `ix` (an i64 local — locals-decl declares them
    ; all i64), then evaluate the body (which reads local `ix` via KLocal). value-code ++ [local.set ix]
    ; ++ body-code. ⚠️ A `let`-bound value that is BOOL-kinded (i32, e.g. `(let ((b (< 1 2))) …)`) cannot
    ; be stored into the i64 local — `local.set` of an i32 into an i64 local is an invalid component. This
    ; is the `let` face of the same i64-model-vs-Bool collision as the KIf/KNot/call-arg declines: without
    ; per-binding kind (a Bool local declared i32), the store mismatches. DECLINE (→ unreachable) rather
    ; than emit invalid bytes; the faithful fix is a per-let-binding kind (ask-34/ask-35 family).
    ((Core.KLet (tuple ix v body))
      (match (kind-of v ktab)
        ((Kind.Ki64 _)  (code-cat (lower v ktab sb) (code-cat (one (Instr.ISet ix)) (lower body ktab sb))))
        ((Kind.KBool _) (one (Instr.IUnreachable ())))))
    ; A call. PARAMS ARE i64 in this compiler's model, but a parameter can be USED as a Bool (`(if x …)`,
    ; `(not x)`) or RETURNED (a kind-polymorphic pass-through like `(def (id x) x)`). Faithfully compiling
    ; such a call needs per-parameter kind inference + per-call specialization (monomorphization over the
    ; i64/i32 boundary) that this compiler does not yet do (ask-34). So passing a BOOL argument is UNSOUND
    ; here: widening it to i64 either loses the Bool on a pass-through (`(id true)` → the integer 1, a
    ; WRONG-VALUE miscompile) or mismatches a Bool-position use (`(pick true)` → an invalid component). To
    ; honour decline-don't-miscompile, DECLINE any call with a Bool argument (→ `unreachable`) rather than
    ; emit a wrong value / invalid bytes. An i64 argument is fine (the common case: recursion, arithmetic).
    ; When per-param kind inference lands, this decline becomes a correct specialized call.
    ((Core.KCall (tuple fi args))
      (if (args-have-bool args 0 ktab)
          (one (Instr.IUnreachable ()))
          (code-cat (lower-args args 0 ktab sb) (one (Instr.ICall fi)))))
    ; A `do` block: emit each form's code in order; DROP the result of every non-final form (its value is
    ; discarded, but it is still EVALUATED — so a trapping non-final form still traps); the LAST form's
    ; value is left on the stack as the block's result. `lower-do` walks the form list. An empty `do`
    ; can't occur (the reader routes it to `malformed`), but `lower-do` returns `unreachable` for safety.
    ((Core.KDo forms) (lower-do forms 0 ktab sb))
    ((Core.KError _)         (one (Instr.IUnreachable ())))))

(def (lower-do forms i ktab sb)
  (doc "Lower a `do` block's form list from index `i`: each NON-final form → its code ++ [drop] (evaluated
        for its trap/effect, result discarded); the LAST form → its code (its value is the block's result).
        `i` is the last form iff the NEXT index has no element. An empty list (i past the end at the start)
        → `unreachable` (a `do` with no forms — the reader already rejects it as `malformed`).")
  (match (List.at forms i)
    ((None _) (one (Instr.IUnreachable ())))
    ((Some form)
      (match (List.at forms (+ i 1))
        ((None _)  (lower form ktab sb))                                    ; last form → its value stays
        ((Some _)  (code-cat (lower form ktab sb)                           ; non-final → evaluate then
                     (code-cat (one (Instr.IDrop ())) (lower-do forms (+ i 1) ktab sb))))))))

(def (args-have-bool args i ktab)
  (doc "Does any argument in the call's arg list have Bool kind? A Bool argument to a user function is
        unsound in the i64-parameter model (see the KCall arm) — the call must decline.")
  (match (List.at args i)
    ((Some a) (match (kind-of a ktab)
                ((Kind.KBool _) true)
                ((Kind.Ki64 _)  (args-have-bool args (+ i 1) ktab))))
    (None false)))

(def (lower-args args i ktab sb)
  (doc "Lower a call's argument list to code: each arg's code concatenated in order (arg 0 first), so
        the args land on the stack left-to-right — the order the callee's i64 params expect (wasm calling
        convention). The trailing `call` (emitted by the KCall arm) consumes them. Only reached when NO
        argument is Bool-kinded (the KCall arm declines such a call), so every arg is i64 here. `sb`
        threads the scratch-local base for a checked-arithmetic sub-expression in an argument.")
  (match (List.at args i)
    ((Some a) (code-cat (lower a ktab sb) (lower-args args (+ i 1) ktab sb)))
    (None (list))))

; ─── kind-of: Core → result Kind (type-directed emission) ───────────────────
; The framing must present `run`'s result at the right wasm valtype: an i64
; program is s64 at the boundary, a Bool program is i32/bool. So the pipeline
; must KNOW a program's result kind — a small inference over the resolved Core.
; The Kind is a SUM TYPE, not an integer tag: `kind-of` and the valtype maps
; below pattern-match it EXHAUSTIVELY, so adding a kind (a float result, say) is
; a compile error until every consumer handles it — the same reject-don't-
; miscompile discipline the Instr sum gives the backend. (This is the seam where
; full kind inference will live; the operand kinds of a primitive are currently
; fixed by the operator, so no unification is needed yet.) `Kind` itself is
; declared up by the Instr sum, since `IIf` carries a block-result Kind.

(def (ktab-at ktab fi)
  (doc "The return Kind of function `fi` from the return-kind table `ktab` (a `list Kind`, one entry
        per module function, in def order). A missing entry (the single-expression path's empty table)
        defaults to Ki64.")
  (match (List.at ktab fi) ((Some k) k) (None (Kind.Ki64 ()))))

(def (kind-of node ktab)
  (doc "The result Kind of a Core node: Ki64 (arithmetic / int constant / i64 local) or KBool
        (comparison / bool constant / logical). A CALL's kind is the CALLEE's return kind, looked up
        in the function return-kind table `ktab` — so a Bool-returning helper frames its callers as
        Bool (i32), not i64. Read by the framing to pick the core + component result valtypes.
        ⚠️ `ktab` is SINGLE-PASS (build-ktab): a function whose body is DIRECTLY Bool is captured, and
        its callers frame right. A DEPTH-2 chain (a helper whose body is only a call to a Bool helper)
        is NOT propagated — that needs a fixpoint, which still OOMs (seed gap 3k, fixpoint form). The
        common case (main / a helper directly returns a comparison) is correct; document, don't fake.")
  (match node
    ((Core.KConst n)  (Kind.Ki64 ()))
    ((Core.KFloat n)  (Kind.Ki64 ()))   ; defensive: a KFloat DECLINES in `lower` (traps), so this framing kind is
                                         ; never consumed; the two-valued codegen Kind has no float — Ki64 is a safe stub.
    ((Core.KCompound n)  (Kind.Ki64 ()))   ; defensive: KCompound DECLINES in `lower` — framing kind never consumed.
    ((Core.KBoolC n)  (Kind.KBool ()))
    ((Core.KAdd p)    (Kind.Ki64 ()))
    ((Core.KSub p)    (Kind.Ki64 ()))
    ((Core.KMul p)    (Kind.Ki64 ()))
    ((Core.KDiv p)    (Kind.Ki64 ()))
    ((Core.KMod p)    (Kind.Ki64 ()))
    ((Core.KBitAnd p) (Kind.Ki64 ()))
    ((Core.KBitOr p)  (Kind.Ki64 ()))
    ((Core.KXor p)    (Kind.Ki64 ()))
    ((Core.KShl p)    (Kind.Ki64 ()))
    ((Core.KShr p)    (Kind.Ki64 ()))
    ((Core.KLt p)     (Kind.KBool ()))
    ((Core.KGt p)     (Kind.KBool ()))
    ((Core.KLe p)     (Kind.KBool ()))
    ((Core.KGe p)     (Kind.KBool ()))
    ((Core.KEq p)     (Kind.KBool ()))
    ((Core.KNe p)     (Kind.KBool ()))
    ((Core.KNot p)    (Kind.KBool ()))
    ; A conditional's Kind is its branches' Kind (both share it); read the then-branch.
    ((Core.KIf (tuple c t f)) (kind-of t ktab))
    ; A parameter is i64 in the i64-parameter function model.
    ((Core.KLocal n)  (Kind.Ki64 ()))
    ((Core.KLet (tuple ix v body)) (kind-of body ktab))
    ; A call's result kind is its callee's return kind (from the table).
    ((Core.KCall (tuple fi args)) (ktab-at ktab fi))
    ; A `do` block's kind is its LAST form's kind (the value it yields).
    ((Core.KDo forms) (kind-of (do-last forms 0) ktab))
    ((Core.KError _)  (Kind.Ki64 ()))))

(def (do-last forms i)
  (doc "The last Core form of a `do` block's form list (the one whose value the block yields). Walks to
        the element with no successor. An empty list → KError (a `do` with no forms — reader rejects it).")
  (match (List.at forms i)
    ((None _) (Core.KError 0))                 ; defensive: reader already rejects an empty `do` — silent decline
    ((Some form)
      (match (List.at forms (+ i 1))
        ((None _) form)
        ((Some _) (do-last forms (+ i 1)))))))

(def (core-valtype kind)
  (doc "The core wasm valtype byte for a result Kind: i64 = 0x7E, Bool = i32 = 0x7F.")
  (match kind ((Kind.Ki64 _) 0x7E) ((Kind.KBool _) 0x7F)))

(def (comp-valtype kind)
  (doc "The component-model valtype byte the run export presents: i64 → s64 = 0x78, Bool = 0x7F.")
  (match kind ((Kind.Ki64 _) 0x78) ((Kind.KBool _) 0x7F)))

; ─── ck-of: the CONSERVATIVE three-valued check kind (Core → CKind) ──────────
; The type-check pass must NOT reuse `kind-of` (the codegen framing kind), because
; `kind-of` DEFAULTS a parameter / call / KError to Ki64 so the framing can pick a
; valtype. Reading that default as a FACT over-rejects a Bool parameter used as an
; `if` condition or a recursive Bool predicate (native infers Bool → COMPILES; the
; check must not emit). `ck-of` returns CKi64 / CKBool ONLY where the kind is
; PROVABLE from the node itself (a literal, an arithmetic/comparison result), and
; CKUnk for anything the coarse surface can't prove (a parameter, a call, a compound
; KError, an `if` whose branches don't agree concretely). The check then emits ONLY
; on a provable mismatch — an unknown never fires — so under-rejection (a decline)
; is the failure mode, never a false rejection. (ask-53 conservative-lattice fix.)
(def (ck-of node ktab)
  (doc "The provable check-kind of a Core node: CKi64 / CKBool where derivable from the node itself,
        CKUnk where the coarse surface can't prove it (parameter / call / compound / disagreeing `if`).")
  (match node
    ((Core.KConst n)  (CKind.CKi64 ()))
    ((Core.KFloat n)  (CKind.CKFloat ()))   ; a float literal is PROVABLY a float — the whole point (int-vs-float mismatch)
    ((Core.KCompound n)  (CKind.CKCompound ()))   ; a compound literal is PROVABLY not a scalar (scalar-vs-compound mismatch)
    ((Core.KBoolC n)  (CKind.CKBool ()))
    ((Core.KAdd p)    (CKind.CKi64 ()))
    ((Core.KSub p)    (CKind.CKi64 ()))
    ((Core.KMul p)    (CKind.CKi64 ()))
    ((Core.KDiv p)    (CKind.CKi64 ()))
    ((Core.KMod p)    (CKind.CKi64 ()))
    ((Core.KBitAnd p) (CKind.CKi64 ()))
    ((Core.KBitOr p)  (CKind.CKi64 ()))
    ((Core.KXor p)    (CKind.CKi64 ()))
    ((Core.KShl p)    (CKind.CKi64 ()))
    ((Core.KShr p)    (CKind.CKi64 ()))
    ((Core.KLt p)     (CKind.CKBool ()))
    ((Core.KGt p)     (CKind.CKBool ()))
    ((Core.KLe p)     (CKind.CKBool ()))
    ((Core.KGe p)     (CKind.CKBool ()))
    ((Core.KEq p)     (CKind.CKBool ()))
    ((Core.KNe p)     (CKind.CKBool ()))
    ((Core.KNot p)    (CKind.CKBool ()))
    ; A conditional's provable kind is its branches' shared kind — CKUnk if they don't agree concretely
    ; (the DISAGREEMENT itself is checked at the KIf node; here we only report the result kind upward).
    ((Core.KIf (tuple c t f)) (ck-join (ck-of t ktab) (ck-of f ktab)))
    ; A parameter's kind is NOT provable (the i64-param model defaults it; it may be Bool) → CKUnk.
    ((Core.KLocal n)  (CKind.CKUnk ()))
    ((Core.KLet (tuple ix v body)) (ck-of body ktab))
    ; A call's return kind is not provable at the check level (ktab defaults unknowns to Ki64) → CKUnk.
    ((Core.KCall (tuple fi args)) (CKind.CKUnk ()))
    ((Core.KDo forms) (ck-of (do-last forms 0) ktab))
    ; A KError is a compound / unsupported / error node — not a provable scalar → CKUnk.
    ((Core.KError _)  (CKind.CKUnk ()))))

(def (ck-concrete x)
  (doc "Is a check-kind a CONCRETE kind (CKi64/CKBool/CKFloat/CKCompound), i.e. one the check can reason about?
        CKUnk is not concrete — a mismatch/agreement against it is never provable.")
  (match x ((CKind.CKi64 _) true) ((CKind.CKBool _) true) ((CKind.CKFloat _) true) ((CKind.CKCompound _) true) ((CKind.CKUnk _) false)))

(def (ck-eq x y)
  (doc "Are two check-kinds the same concrete kind? CKi64=CKi64, CKBool=CKBool, CKFloat=CKFloat, and — CRUCIALLY —
        CKCompound=CKCompound is TREATED AS EQUAL so `ck-provably-mismatch` does NOT fire for two compounds (a
        compound-vs-compound is SHAPE-DEPENDENT — same shape compiles, differ rejects — which this coarse kind
        cannot decide, so it must not claim a provable mismatch). A CKCompound vs a scalar IS unequal → a provable
        mismatch (a compound is never a scalar). Any CKUnk is not equal to anything.")
  (match x
    ((CKind.CKi64 _)  (match y ((CKind.CKi64 _) true)  ((CKind.CKBool _) false) ((CKind.CKFloat _) false) ((CKind.CKCompound _) false) ((CKind.CKUnk _) false)))
    ((CKind.CKBool _) (match y ((CKind.CKi64 _) false) ((CKind.CKBool _) true)  ((CKind.CKFloat _) false) ((CKind.CKCompound _) false) ((CKind.CKUnk _) false)))
    ((CKind.CKFloat _) (match y ((CKind.CKi64 _) false) ((CKind.CKBool _) false) ((CKind.CKFloat _) true) ((CKind.CKCompound _) false) ((CKind.CKUnk _) false)))
    ((CKind.CKCompound _) (match y ((CKind.CKi64 _) false) ((CKind.CKBool _) false) ((CKind.CKFloat _) false) ((CKind.CKCompound _) true) ((CKind.CKUnk _) false)))
    ((CKind.CKUnk _)  false)))

(def (ck-join x y)
  (doc "The provable kind of an `if` from its two branch kinds: their shared concrete kind if they agree,
        else CKUnk (maximally conservative — an unknown or a disagreement upward is not provable).")
  (if (ck-eq x y) x (CKind.CKUnk ())))

(def (ck-provably-not-i64 node ktab)
  (doc "Is `node` PROVABLY not an i64? — a concrete non-i64 kind (CKBool, CKFloat, or CKCompound). A parameter/
        call CKUnk is NOT provably-not-i64, so it does not trigger an arithmetic-operand rejection. A COMPOUND
        arith operand (`(+ (list 1) 2)`) IS provably not-i64 → rejects (native rejects it).")
  (match (ck-of node ktab) ((CKind.CKBool _) true) ((CKind.CKFloat _) true) ((CKind.CKCompound _) true) ((CKind.CKi64 _) false) ((CKind.CKUnk _) false)))

(def (ck-provably-not-bool node ktab)
  (doc "Is `node` PROVABLY not a Bool? — a concrete non-Bool kind (CKi64, CKFloat, or CKCompound). A CKUnk is
        NOT provably-not-bool, so a Bool parameter used as an `if` condition / `not` operand does not fire. A
        COMPOUND `if`-condition (`(if (tuple 1 2) …)`) IS provably not-Bool → rejects (native rejects it).")
  (match (ck-of node ktab) ((CKind.CKi64 _) true) ((CKind.CKFloat _) true) ((CKind.CKCompound _) true) ((CKind.CKBool _) false) ((CKind.CKUnk _) false)))

(def (ck-provably-mismatch a b ktab)
  (doc "Do two operands PROVABLY mismatch? — both CONCRETE and NOT equal (e.g. CKi64 vs CKBool, CKi64 vs
        CKFloat, CKBool vs CKFloat). If either is CKUnk the mismatch is not provable → false. This is the
        conservative core: emit only when a mismatch is certain — now covering int-vs-float via CKFloat.")
  (let ((ka (ck-of a ktab)) (kb (ck-of b ktab)))
    (and (and (ck-concrete ka) (ck-concrete kb)) (not (ck-eq ka kb)))))

(def (ck-is-float node ktab)
  (doc "Is `node` PROVABLY a float? (Only a concrete CKFloat.)")
  (match (ck-of node ktab) ((CKind.CKFloat _) true) ((CKind.CKi64 _) false) ((CKind.CKBool _) false) ((CKind.CKCompound _) false) ((CKind.CKUnk _) false)))

(def (numeric-mismatch-code a b ktab)
  (doc "The CDZ code for a PROVABLE mismatch at a NUMERIC position (arithmetic operand / ordering `< > <= >=`):
        CDZ0301 (numeric types do not silently promote / ordering between different types) when a FLOAT is
        involved — native's float-specific code — else CDZ0201 (a Bool-vs-i64 mismatch, `(+ 1 true)` /
        `(< 1 true)`). Equality (`= !=`), `if`-branches, and `if`-cond/`not` always use 201 (native does).")
  (if (or (ck-is-float a ktab) (ck-is-float b ktab)) 301 201))

; ─── well-typed?: the type-rejection pass (Core → Bool) ──────────────────────
; A self-hosted compiler MUST reject an ill-typed program, not emit a component for
; it (reject-don't-miscompile at the whole-program level; constitution: static typing
; mandatory). This pass walks the resolved+folded Core and returns false for a program
; whose kinds don't check, so the driver replaces its body with KError (→ trap) rather
; than lowering a mismatch. Scope is the COARSE i64/Bool kind lattice `kind-of` tracks
; (the two kinds this compiler emits): it catches an `if` whose branches differ in kind
; (`(if c 1 false)`), an arithmetic/bitwise/shift operand that is not i64 (`(+ 1 true)`),
; a comparison across kinds (`(< 1 true)`), a non-Bool `if` condition or `not` operand
; (which is also how `(and 1 true)` is caught — `and` desugared to `(if 1 true false)`).
; Float/String type errors are NOT representable here (the compiler emits neither type
; yet); this is the achievable subset of ask-30's type-inference half. The provable-kind
; predicates (`ck-provably-not-i64`/`ck-provably-not-bool`/`ck-provably-mismatch`, over the
; three-valued `CKind`) decide operand agreement — an UNKNOWN operand (parameter/call) is
; never rejected, only a PROVABLE mismatch. (Native returns a coded CDZ diagnostic; this
; compiler emits CDZ0201 via the Diag effect — the point is to STOP mis-accepting, matching
; native's REJECTION, WITHOUT false-rejecting a Bool parameter native infers and compiles.)
(def (both-i64 a b ktab)
  (doc "Do both operands of `+ - * / % & | ^ << >>` type-check? Both sub-expressions must be well-typed,
        and NEITHER may be PROVABLY not-i64 (a concrete Bool operand → reject). An operand whose kind is
        not provable — a parameter, a call — is ACCEPTED conservatively (native infers it; a false reject
        would be a disagree, an under-reject is a safe decline). ask-53 conservative-lattice fix.")
  (and (and (well-typed? a ktab) (well-typed? b ktab))
       (not (or (ck-provably-not-i64 a ktab) (ck-provably-not-i64 b ktab)))))

(def (cmp-ok a b ktab)
  (doc "A comparison type-checks iff both operands are well-typed and do NOT PROVABLY mismatch (a concrete
        i64-vs-Bool compare like `(< 1 true)` is rejected). If either operand's kind is not provable, the
        compare is accepted conservatively — native infers it. ask-53 conservative-lattice fix.")
  (and (and (well-typed? a ktab) (well-typed? b ktab))
       (not (ck-provably-mismatch a b ktab))))

(def (well-typed? node ktab)
  (doc "Does `node` type-check under the coarse i64/Bool lattice? Recursive over Core. Arithmetic/
        bitwise/shift require both operands i64; comparisons require matching operand kinds; `not`
        requires Bool; `if` requires a Bool condition and matching branch kinds; `let` checks value
        and body; a call checks its args (params are i64, so a Bool arg is caught by lower's decline —
        here we just recurse). Leaves (const/bool/local/error) are well-typed.")
  (match node
    ((Core.KConst n)  true)
    ((Core.KFloat n)  true)   ; a bare float is well-typed-as-a-value (native compiles it); codegen declines it. A
                              ; MISMATCH (int beside float) is caught at the KAdd/KIf/cmp site via ck-provably-mismatch.
    ((Core.KCompound n)  true)   ; a bare compound is well-typed-as-a-value (native compiles it); codegen declines it.
                              ; A scalar-vs-compound MISMATCH is caught at the KIf/arith site via ck-provably-mismatch.
    ((Core.KBoolC n)  true)
    ((Core.KLocal n)  true)
    ((Core.KAdd (tuple a b)) (both-i64 a b ktab))
    ((Core.KSub (tuple a b)) (both-i64 a b ktab))
    ((Core.KMul (tuple a b)) (both-i64 a b ktab))
    ((Core.KDiv (tuple a b)) (both-i64 a b ktab))
    ((Core.KMod (tuple a b)) (both-i64 a b ktab))
    ((Core.KBitAnd (tuple a b)) (both-i64 a b ktab))
    ((Core.KBitOr (tuple a b))  (both-i64 a b ktab))
    ((Core.KXor (tuple a b))    (both-i64 a b ktab))
    ((Core.KShl (tuple a b)) (both-i64 a b ktab))
    ((Core.KShr (tuple a b)) (both-i64 a b ktab))
    ((Core.KLt (tuple a b))  (cmp-ok a b ktab))
    ((Core.KGt (tuple a b))  (cmp-ok a b ktab))
    ((Core.KLe (tuple a b))  (cmp-ok a b ktab))
    ((Core.KGe (tuple a b))  (cmp-ok a b ktab))
    ((Core.KEq (tuple a b))  (cmp-ok a b ktab))
    ((Core.KNe (tuple a b))  (cmp-ok a b ktab))
    ((Core.KNot a)           (and (well-typed? a ktab) (not (ck-provably-not-bool a ktab))))
    ((Core.KIf (tuple c t f))
      (and (and (well-typed? c ktab) (not (ck-provably-not-bool c ktab)))
           (and (and (well-typed? t ktab) (well-typed? f ktab))
                (not (ck-provably-mismatch t f ktab)))))
    ((Core.KLet (tuple ix v body)) (and (well-typed? v ktab) (well-typed? body ktab)))
    ((Core.KCall (tuple fi args)) (args-well-typed args 0 ktab))
    ; A `do` block type-checks iff every form does. Each non-final form's value is dropped (any droppable
    ; single-value kind is fine — i64 and Bool both drop), and the block's kind is the last form's.
    ((Core.KDo forms) (args-well-typed forms 0 ktab))
    ((Core.KError _)  true)))

(def (args-well-typed args i ktab)
  (doc "Are all of a call's arguments well-typed? (Recurse the arg list.)")
  (match (List.at args i)
    ((Some a) (and (well-typed? a ktab) (args-well-typed args (+ i 1) ktab)))
    (None true)))

(def (typecheck body ktab)
  (doc "Gate a function body through the type-rejection pass: return it unchanged if well-typed, else a
        REJECT `(KError 1)` (→ trap + CDZ0201 diagnostic) — a type error is a genuine rejection native emits,
        not a silent decline.")
  (if (well-typed? body ktab) body (Core.KError 201)))

; ─── rejection detection for the diagnostics channel (ask-40) ────────────────
; `compile` now returns `Result<Bytes, list<diagnostic>>` (ask-40 landed the ABI): an
; ill-typed / malformed program yields the FAILURE arm with a coded diagnostic instead
; of lowering to a trapping `unreachable`. A program is rejected iff any function body
; is not `well-typed?` OR contains a `KError` node — the resolve/reader marker for an
; unrecognized head, an unbound name, a malformed-arity form, or a bad entry (all the
; front-end rejection points). Every such rejection in this compiler's coarse surface is
; native's CDZ0201 (mismatched types / malformed form); float-promotion (CDZ0301) and
; non-exhaustive-match (CDZ0210) are out of scope here. `has-kerror?` walks the Core.
(def (has-kerror? node)
  (doc "Does the Core tree contain a `KError` (the front-end rejection marker)?")
  (match node
    ((Core.KConst n)  false)
    ((Core.KFloat n)  false)   ; a float is a value node, not a KError reject marker
    ((Core.KCompound n)  false)   ; a compound literal is a value node, not a KError reject marker
    ((Core.KBoolC n)  false)
    ((Core.KLocal n)  false)
    ((Core.KAdd (tuple a b)) (or (has-kerror? a) (has-kerror? b)))
    ((Core.KSub (tuple a b)) (or (has-kerror? a) (has-kerror? b)))
    ((Core.KMul (tuple a b)) (or (has-kerror? a) (has-kerror? b)))
    ((Core.KDiv (tuple a b)) (or (has-kerror? a) (has-kerror? b)))
    ((Core.KMod (tuple a b)) (or (has-kerror? a) (has-kerror? b)))
    ((Core.KBitAnd (tuple a b)) (or (has-kerror? a) (has-kerror? b)))
    ((Core.KBitOr (tuple a b))  (or (has-kerror? a) (has-kerror? b)))
    ((Core.KXor (tuple a b))    (or (has-kerror? a) (has-kerror? b)))
    ((Core.KShl (tuple a b)) (or (has-kerror? a) (has-kerror? b)))
    ((Core.KShr (tuple a b)) (or (has-kerror? a) (has-kerror? b)))
    ((Core.KLt (tuple a b))  (or (has-kerror? a) (has-kerror? b)))
    ((Core.KGt (tuple a b))  (or (has-kerror? a) (has-kerror? b)))
    ((Core.KLe (tuple a b))  (or (has-kerror? a) (has-kerror? b)))
    ((Core.KGe (tuple a b))  (or (has-kerror? a) (has-kerror? b)))
    ((Core.KEq (tuple a b))  (or (has-kerror? a) (has-kerror? b)))
    ((Core.KNe (tuple a b))  (or (has-kerror? a) (has-kerror? b)))
    ((Core.KNot a)           (has-kerror? a))
    ((Core.KIf (tuple c t f)) (or (has-kerror? c) (or (has-kerror? t) (has-kerror? f))))
    ((Core.KLet (tuple ix v body)) (or (has-kerror? v) (has-kerror? body)))
    ((Core.KCall (tuple fi args)) (args-have-kerror args 0))
    ((Core.KDo forms) (args-have-kerror forms 0))
    ((Core.KError _)  true)))

(def (args-have-kerror args i)
  (doc "Does any element of an arg/form list contain a KError?")
  (match (List.at args i)
    ((Some a) (or (has-kerror? a) (args-have-kerror args (+ i 1))))
    (None false)))

(def (func-rejects? f ktab)
  (doc "Is function `f`'s body a rejection — ill-typed OR containing a front-end KError marker?")
  (or (not (well-typed? (func-body f) ktab)) (has-kerror? (func-body f))))

(def (any-func-rejects? funcs ktab)
  (doc "Does any function in the program reject (ill-typed / malformed)? Drives `compile`'s Err arm.
        Index-walk over the built-in `list<Func>`.")
  (any-func-rejects?-go funcs 0 (List.len funcs) ktab))
(def (any-func-rejects?-go funcs i n ktab)
  (if (< i n)
      (or (match (List.at funcs i) ((None _) false) ((Some h) (func-rejects? h ktab)))
          (any-func-rejects?-go funcs (+ i 1) n ktab))
      false))

(def (reject-diagnostic)
  (doc "The diagnostic list for a rejected program: a single CDZ0201 (the code native rejects every
        type/malformed error in this compiler's coarse surface with). The message is not graded
        (component-check compares CODE only), but is human-readable.")
  (list (record (code "CDZ0201") (message "the program is ill-typed or malformed"))))

; ─── check: the effect-based rejection pass (performs Diag.emit) ─────────────
; The effect twin of `well-typed?`/`has-kerror?`: instead of returning a Bool,
; `check-node` PERFORMS `(Diag.emit 201)` at every rejection point in the Core —
; a KError marker (malformed form / unknown head / unbound name / bad entry) or a
; local type error (arithmetic/shift operand not i64, comparison across kinds,
; non-Bool `not`/`if`-condition, mismatched `if` branches). It recurses into every
; sub-node so a rejection anywhere in the body is reported. Run under a `Diag`
; handler in `compile`, this accumulates the program's diagnostic codes; the
; handler's `collect` op reads the list back. (CDZ0201 for all — the coarse-surface
; code; a richer code map is future work when float/exhaustiveness enter scope.)
; emit-per-node uses the same predicates as `well-typed?` so the two agree.
(def (emit-diag code) (Diag.emit code))

(def (check-node node ktab)
  (doc "Walk `node`, performing `(Diag.emit code)` at each type error / KError, and recursing into every
        sub-node. The effect-based rejection pass — the Bool `well-typed?`'s emitting twin. The local
        type-error positions (arith/cmp operand, `if`-cond/branch, `not`) emit CDZ0201 (the coarse-surface
        code); a KError carries its OWN code in the payload (201 malformed, 210 non-exhaustive), emitted
        as-is (0 = a silent decline). Coded diagnostics — the channel is no longer monochrome.")
  (match node
    ((Core.KConst n)  unit)
    ((Core.KFloat n)  unit)   ; a bare float emits no diagnostic (native compiles it); a MISMATCH is emitted at the parent
    ((Core.KCompound n)  unit)   ; a bare compound emits no diagnostic; a scalar-vs-compound MISMATCH emitted at parent
    ((Core.KBoolC n)  unit)
    ((Core.KLocal n)  unit)
    ((Core.KAdd (tuple a b)) (check-arith a b ktab))
    ((Core.KSub (tuple a b)) (check-arith a b ktab))
    ((Core.KMul (tuple a b)) (check-arith a b ktab))
    ((Core.KDiv (tuple a b)) (check-arith a b ktab))
    ((Core.KMod (tuple a b)) (check-arith a b ktab))
    ((Core.KBitAnd (tuple a b)) (check-arith a b ktab))
    ((Core.KBitOr (tuple a b))  (check-arith a b ktab))
    ((Core.KXor (tuple a b))    (check-arith a b ktab))
    ((Core.KShl (tuple a b)) (check-arith a b ktab))
    ((Core.KShr (tuple a b)) (check-arith a b ktab))
    ((Core.KLt (tuple a b))  (check-order a b ktab))
    ((Core.KGt (tuple a b))  (check-order a b ktab))
    ((Core.KLe (tuple a b))  (check-order a b ktab))
    ((Core.KGe (tuple a b))  (check-order a b ktab))
    ((Core.KEq (tuple a b))  (check-cmp a b ktab))
    ((Core.KNe (tuple a b))  (check-cmp a b ktab))
    ((Core.KNot a)
      (do (check-node a ktab) (if (ck-provably-not-bool a ktab) (emit-diag 201) unit)))
    ((Core.KIf (tuple c t f))
      (do (check-node c ktab) (check-node t ktab) (check-node f ktab)
          (if (ck-provably-not-bool c ktab) (emit-diag 201) unit)
          (if (ck-provably-mismatch t f ktab) (emit-diag 201) unit)))
    ((Core.KLet (tuple ix v body)) (do (check-node v ktab) (check-node body ktab)))
    ((Core.KCall (tuple fi args)) (check-args args 0 ktab))
    ((Core.KDo forms) (check-args forms 0 ktab))
    ; A KError carries its CDZ code in the payload: emit that code, EXCEPT 0 = a silent DECLINE (an
    ; unsupported construct native COMPILES — float/string/list/user-sum-match; the frontier, not an error).
    ; A malformed form carries 201, a non-exhaustive bool match carries 210 (native's codes). ask-53 + coded.
    ((Core.KError k)  (if (= k 0) unit (emit-diag k)))))

(def (check-arith a b ktab)
  (doc "Check an arithmetic/bitwise/shift node: recurse operands, then emit if EITHER is PROVABLY not-i64
        (a concrete Bool or Float operand). An operand whose kind is not provable (parameter/call) is
        accepted — native infers it; an under-reject is a safe decline. The CODE is float-aware: a FLOAT
        operand → CDZ0301 (numeric non-promotion, native's code), a Bool operand → CDZ0201. ask-53/54.")
  (do (check-node a ktab) (check-node b ktab)
      (if (or (ck-provably-not-i64 a ktab) (ck-provably-not-i64 b ktab))
          (emit-diag (numeric-mismatch-code a b ktab))
          unit)))

(def (check-order a b ktab)
  (doc "Check an ORDERING node (`< > <= >=`): recurse operands, then emit if they PROVABLY mismatch. The
        code is float-aware: an int-vs-FLOAT ordering → CDZ0301 (native's `ordering between different
        types` for numerics), an int-vs-Bool ordering (`(< 1 true)`) → CDZ0201. ask-54.")
  (do (check-node a ktab) (check-node b ktab)
      (if (ck-provably-mismatch a b ktab) (emit-diag (numeric-mismatch-code a b ktab)) unit)))

(def (check-cmp a b ktab)
  (doc "Check an EQUALITY node (`= !=`): recurse operands, then emit ONLY if they PROVABLY mismatch. The code
        is float-aware like arith/ordering: an int-vs-FLOAT equality → CDZ0301 (native applies numeric
        non-promotion to `=` too), an int-vs-Bool equality → CDZ0201. ask-53/54.")
  (do (check-node a ktab) (check-node b ktab)
      (if (ck-provably-mismatch a b ktab) (emit-diag (numeric-mismatch-code a b ktab)) unit)))

(def (check-args args i ktab)
  (doc "Check every element of an arg/form list, in order.")
  (match (List.at args i)
    ((Some a) (do (check-node a ktab) (check-args args (+ i 1) ktab)))
    (None unit)))

(def (check-funcs funcs ktab)
  (doc "Run the effect-based check over every function body (performs Diag.emit per rejection).
        Index-walk over the built-in `list<Func>`.")
  (check-funcs-go funcs 0 (List.len funcs) ktab))
(def (check-funcs-go funcs i n ktab)
  (if (< i n)
      (do (match (List.at funcs i) ((None _) unit) ((Some h) (check-node (func-body h) ktab)))
          (check-funcs-go funcs (+ i 1) n ktab))
      unit))

; ─── diagnostic records: emitted codes → the `compile-output` diagnostics list ──
; The `Diag` handler accumulates a `list` of Int64 CODES (each `check-node` rejection
; performs `(Diag.emit code)`). `compile` turns those codes into the `list<diagnostic>`
; the kinded-artifact ABI wants — `diagnostic = record{code: string, message: string,
; severity: enum{error=0, warning=1}}` (ask-41; fields sorted by key at the value level).
; The channel is CODED (no longer monochrome CDZ0201): each emitted Int64 maps to its CDZ
; string + message via `code-string`/`code-message`. This compiler emits CDZ0201 (type
; mismatch / malformed form) and CDZ0210 (a non-exhaustive bool match); more as the surface
; grows. `mk-diagnostic` builds one record; `codes->diagnostics` maps the list.
(def (code-string code)
  (doc "The CDZ code STRING for an emitted Int64 diagnostic code (201 → \"CDZ0201\", 203 → \"CDZ0203\"
        annotation contradicts value, 210 → \"CDZ0210\", 301 → \"CDZ0301\" numeric non-promotion). An
        unrecognized code falls back to CDZ0201 — a diagnostic is always coded, never blank. ⚠ Every code
        the check emits MUST have a case here, or it silently collapses to CDZ0201 (the bug that hid 301).")
  (if (= code 203) "CDZ0203"
  (if (= code 210) "CDZ0210"
  (if (= code 301) "CDZ0301"
      "CDZ0201"))))
(def (code-message code)
  (doc "A human-readable message for an emitted code (component-check compares CODE only, so the message
        is advisory). 203 = annotation contradicts value; 210 = non-exhaustive match; 301 = numeric
        non-promotion; else = ill-typed/malformed.")
  (if (= code 203) "the annotation contradicts the value"
  (if (= code 210) "the match does not cover every value of the scrutinee"
  (if (= code 301) "numeric types do not silently promote"
      "the program is ill-typed or malformed"))))
(def (mk-diagnostic code)
  (doc "One `diagnostic` record from an emitted CDZ code — its coded string + message, severity error(0).")
  (record (code (code-string code)) (message (code-message code)) (severity 0)))

(def (codes->diagnostics codes i out)
  (doc "Map a `list` of emitted Int64 codes to a `list<diagnostic>` (record per code), preserving order.")
  (match (List.at codes i)
    ((Some c) (codes->diagnostics codes (+ i 1) (List.push out (mk-diagnostic c))))
    ((None _) out)))

; ─── frame: a body's code bytes → a complete component ──────────────────────
; The fixed component envelope; only the result valtype bytes and the body bytes
; vary with the program's result kind. The surround is the byte shape validated
; end-to-end at ignition.

(def (encode-body code-bytes)
  (doc "A code-section entry for a function with NO extra locals: <uleb len> <0 locals> <code> <end>.
        The single-expression driver uses this; the multi-function path uses `encode-body-locals`.")
  (encode-body-locals code-bytes 0))

(def (encode-body-locals code-bytes nlocals)
  (doc "A code-section entry declaring `nlocals` extra i64 locals (beyond params): <uleb len(inner)>
        <inner>, where inner = <local-decl> <code> <end 0x0B>. The local declaration is `00` for no
        extra locals, else `01 <nlocals> 7E` (one group of `nlocals` i64s).")
  (sized (cat (locals-decl nlocals) (cat code-bytes (u8 0x0B)))))

(def (locals-decl nlocals)
  (doc "The wasm local-declaration vector for `nlocals` i64 locals: `00` if none, else one group
        `01 <count> 7E`.")
  (if (< nlocals 1)
      (u8 0x00)
      (cat (u8 0x01) (cat (uleb nlocals) (u8 0x7E)))))

(def (count-lets node)
  (doc "How many `let`-bound locals a Core body introduces — the count of KLet nodes, so the function
        can declare that many extra i64 locals. Recurses through every sub-node so nested/sequential
        lets are all counted.")
  (match node
    ((Core.KConst n)  0)
    ((Core.KFloat n)  0)
    ((Core.KCompound n)  0)
    ((Core.KBoolC n)  0)
    ((Core.KLocal n)  0)
    ((Core.KAdd (tuple a b)) (+ (count-lets a) (count-lets b)))
    ((Core.KSub (tuple a b)) (+ (count-lets a) (count-lets b)))
    ((Core.KMul (tuple a b)) (+ (count-lets a) (count-lets b)))
    ((Core.KDiv (tuple a b)) (+ (count-lets a) (count-lets b)))
    ((Core.KMod (tuple a b)) (+ (count-lets a) (count-lets b)))
    ((Core.KBitAnd (tuple a b)) (+ (count-lets a) (count-lets b)))
    ((Core.KBitOr (tuple a b))  (+ (count-lets a) (count-lets b)))
    ((Core.KXor (tuple a b))    (+ (count-lets a) (count-lets b)))
    ((Core.KShl (tuple a b)) (+ (count-lets a) (count-lets b)))
    ((Core.KShr (tuple a b)) (+ (count-lets a) (count-lets b)))
    ((Core.KLt (tuple a b))  (+ (count-lets a) (count-lets b)))
    ((Core.KGt (tuple a b))  (+ (count-lets a) (count-lets b)))
    ((Core.KLe (tuple a b))  (+ (count-lets a) (count-lets b)))
    ((Core.KGe (tuple a b))  (+ (count-lets a) (count-lets b)))
    ((Core.KEq (tuple a b))  (+ (count-lets a) (count-lets b)))
    ((Core.KNe (tuple a b))  (+ (count-lets a) (count-lets b)))
    ((Core.KNot a)           (count-lets a))
    ((Core.KIf (tuple c t f)) (+ (count-lets c) (+ (count-lets t) (count-lets f))))
    ((Core.KLet (tuple ix v body)) (+ 1 (+ (count-lets v) (count-lets body))))
    ((Core.KCall (tuple fi args)) (count-lets-args args 0))
    ((Core.KDo forms) (count-lets-args forms 0))
    ((Core.KError _)         0)))

(def (count-lets-args args i)
  (doc "Sum the `let`-local counts across a call's argument list (a `list Core`) — each arg is a
        subtree that may itself introduce lets.")
  (match (List.at args i)
    ((Some a) (+ (count-lets a) (count-lets-args args (+ i 1))))
    (None 0)))

(def (count-checked node)
  (doc "How many ops that need the 3 scratch locals the body contains — the overflow-checked `+`/`-`/`*`
        (KAdd/KSub/KMul) AND the guarded shifts `<<`/`>>` (KShl/KShr, which stash value/count/result to
        scratch). Returns 0 if none (declare no scratch), else > 0 (declare 3). Walks the whole Core; the
        3 slots are SHARED across all such ops (each drains its scratch before an enclosing one uses it),
        so the exact count doesn't matter beyond 0/1.")
  (match node
    ((Core.KConst n)  0)
    ((Core.KFloat n)  0)
    ((Core.KCompound n)  0)
    ((Core.KBoolC n)  0)
    ((Core.KLocal n)  0)
    ((Core.KAdd (tuple a b)) (+ 1 (+ (count-checked a) (count-checked b))))
    ((Core.KSub (tuple a b)) (+ 1 (+ (count-checked a) (count-checked b))))
    ((Core.KMul (tuple a b)) (+ 1 (+ (count-checked a) (count-checked b))))
    ((Core.KDiv (tuple a b)) (+ (count-checked a) (count-checked b)))
    ((Core.KMod (tuple a b)) (+ (count-checked a) (count-checked b)))
    ((Core.KBitAnd (tuple a b)) (+ (count-checked a) (count-checked b)))
    ((Core.KBitOr (tuple a b))  (+ (count-checked a) (count-checked b)))
    ((Core.KXor (tuple a b))    (+ (count-checked a) (count-checked b)))
    ((Core.KShl (tuple a b)) (+ 1 (+ (count-checked a) (count-checked b))))
    ((Core.KShr (tuple a b)) (+ 1 (+ (count-checked a) (count-checked b))))
    ((Core.KLt (tuple a b))  (+ (count-checked a) (count-checked b)))
    ((Core.KGt (tuple a b))  (+ (count-checked a) (count-checked b)))
    ((Core.KLe (tuple a b))  (+ (count-checked a) (count-checked b)))
    ((Core.KGe (tuple a b))  (+ (count-checked a) (count-checked b)))
    ((Core.KEq (tuple a b))  (+ (count-checked a) (count-checked b)))
    ((Core.KNe (tuple a b))  (+ (count-checked a) (count-checked b)))
    ((Core.KNot a)           (count-checked a))
    ((Core.KIf (tuple c t f)) (+ (count-checked c) (+ (count-checked t) (count-checked f))))
    ((Core.KLet (tuple ix v body)) (+ (count-checked v) (count-checked body)))
    ((Core.KCall (tuple fi args)) (count-checked-args args 0))
    ((Core.KDo forms) (count-checked-args forms 0))
    ((Core.KError _)         0)))

(def (count-checked-args args i)
  (match (List.at args i)
    ((Some a) (+ (count-checked a) (count-checked-args args (+ i 1))))
    (None 0)))

(def (scratch-count node)
  (doc "The number of SCRATCH i64 locals a body needs: 3 if it contains any overflow-checked op
        (`+`/`-`/`*`), else 0. They sit past the params + let-locals.")
  (if (< (count-checked node) 1) 0 3))

; ─── Multi-function module assembly ─────────────────────────────────────────
; A program is a list of functions. `main` is func 0 (nullary, the `run` entry);
; helper functions may take parameters. Each function has a wasm functype
; `(param i64^nparams) (result <kind>)`; func i uses type i (1:1, matching the
; oracle). The type / function / code sections each become N-entry vectors, built
; by folding a `FList` of functions — the anchored recursive-Bytes fold (each
; section is `(cat (uleb count) items)`, so inference sees Bytes; see Tier 3d).

(type Func (Fn (Tuple Int64 Core)))   ; (param-count, Core body). Arithmetic-overflow-check HELPERS are
                                      ; ordinary Core functions too (see `checked-add-fn` etc.) — no raw
                                      ; instruction stream in the IR; every pass handles them normally.
; The module's function list is a built-in `list<Func>` (ask-62: the custom `FList` cons-sum-type
; retired for the built-in list — its walks CONSUME the list across passes, the ask-63 multi-consume
; pattern, now safe). Position = function index (main = func 0); walks are index-scans over List.at.

(def (func-nparams f) (match f ((Func.Fn (tuple np body)) np)))
(def (func-body f)     (match f ((Func.Fn (tuple np body)) body)))

; ─── resolve-module: DList → FList (the whole front end) ─────────────────────
; Walk the surface definition list to the typed function list: each `Def` becomes a
; `Func` whose body is the def-body Node resolved to Core. This is the front rung at
; module scope — the reader hands `resolve-module` a DList and it produces exactly
; what `compile-program` consumes, so a full multi-def surface program compiles.

(def (resolve-module defs)
  (doc "Resolve a surface module (a `list<Def>`) to a typed `list<Func>`: each def's body Node is
        resolved to Core, its param-count carried through. main is the first def (func 0). Appends in
        order (List.push), so position i = def i = func i. Index-walk over the built-in list.")
  (resolve-module-go defs 0 (List.len defs) (list)))

(def (resolve-module-go defs i n acc)
  (doc "Walk the surface `list<Def>` [i,n), appending each resolved Func to `acc` in order (position = func index).")
  (if (< i n)
      (resolve-module-go defs (+ i 1) n
        (match (List.at defs i)
          ((None _) acc)
          ((Some d) (List.push acc (Func.Fn (tuple (def-nparams d) (resolve (def-body d))))))))
      acc))

; ─── read-module: canonical AST bytes → surface DList (the whole reader) ─────
; Decode a whole `(module <name> (def (f params…) body) …)` from its canonical CBOR.
; The root is `[module-idx, name, def…]`; each `def` is `[def-idx, sig, body]` where
; `sig = [fn-name-idx, param-idxs…]` (so param-count = sig element count − 1). This
; walks the root's def elements (index 2 onward) into a `DList` of `Def`s, reading each
; body with `read-node` — exactly what `resolve-module` consumes. Verified end-to-end:
; the CBOR of `(module m (def (main) 42))` → read-module → resolve-module →
; compile-program → valid 89-byte component (`i64.const 42`). This is `module bytes →
; component`: the compiler reading a whole multi-`def` program's own AST and compiling
; it — the full self-hosting shape. (The def NAME is carried but unused by
; resolve-module, which indexes functions positionally; hence the "" placeholder.)

(def (mod-root b)
  (doc "The offset of the module's root array: skip the file-array version (offset 1) and prelude
        (offset 2) to reach the third element.")
  (cbor-skip b 2))

(def (mod-elem0 b i)
  (doc "The offset of array item i's first element (past its head).")
  (+ i (cbor-head-len b i)))

(def (mod-elem b i k)
  (doc "The offset of element k of the array at offset i.")
  (skip-elems b (mod-elem0 b i) k))

(def (mod-ndefs b root)
  (doc "How many `def` forms the module root holds: its array length minus the module-head and name
        (elements 0 and 1); defs are the remaining elements from index 2 onward.")
  (- (cbor-arg b root) 2))

(def (def-nparams-of b defoff)
  (doc "The parameter count of the def whose array head is at `defoff`: the signature (element 1) is
        `[fn-name, params…]`, so its element count minus 1 is the number of parameters.")
  (- (cbor-arg b (mod-elem b defoff 1)) 1))

(def (def-body-off b defoff)
  (doc "The offset of a def's body — element 2 of `[def-idx, sig, body]`.")
  (mod-elem b defoff 2))

(def (def-sig-off b defoff)
  (doc "The offset of a def's signature array — element 1 of `[def-idx, sig, body]`. The sig is
        `[fn-name, param1, param2, …]` (each a tagged name-ref).")
  (mod-elem b defoff 1))

(def (sig-param-env b sigoff k np)
  (doc "Build the parameter environment for a signature: a built-in `list<Int64>` of the parameter
        names' prelude indices, in order (position = local slot). Sig element 0 is the fn name;
        parameters are elements 1..np. `k` counts parameters read (0-based); the k-th parameter is
        sig element (k+1). Pushes param k first so position k = param k's slot.")
  (sig-param-env-go b sigoff k np (list)))

(def (sig-param-env-go b sigoff k np acc)
  (doc "Append parameters [k,np) of the signature to `acc`, low→high, so position i = param i's slot.")
  (if (< k np)
      (sig-param-env-go b sigoff (+ k 1) np
        (List.push acc (name-ref-index b (mod-elem b sigoff (+ k 1)))))
      acc))

(def (def-param-env b defoff)
  (doc "The parameter environment (a `list<Int64>` of prelude indices) of the def at `defoff`, derived
        from its signature. Threaded into read-node so a body name-ref resolves to the right local slot.")
  (sig-param-env b (def-sig-off b defoff) 0 (def-nparams-of b defoff)))

(def (def-fn-name-index b defoff)
  (doc "The prelude index of a def's FUNCTION NAME. A signature `(f p…)` is an APPLICATION headed by
        the function name, so element 0 is the HEAD-INDEX — a bare prelude-index uint (NOT a tagged
        name-ref like a param or a body name use). Read it directly as the sig array's head-index. This
        is the key a call head is matched against; the function's position in `mod-fenv` is its index.")
  (read-head-index b (def-sig-off b defoff)))

; ─── Entry ordering: `main` becomes func 0 ───────────────────────────────────
; The component boundary lifts func 0 as the nullary `run`, and the native seed
; selects the def NAMED `main` as that entry (not the first def). So the reader
; computes a DEF VISITING ORDER — main's def-index first, then the rest in their
; original order — and BOTH `mod-fenv` (name→index) and `read-defs` (the DList)
; walk that order. This keeps func 0 = main (nullary) while call indices resolve to
; the reordered positions, so a helper-first module `(def (add …)…)(def (main)…)`
; compiles correctly instead of emitting an invalid component (a param'd func 0
; lifted as nullary `run`, and callers of the entry-guarded stub breaking on arity).

; ENTRY = the def NAMED `main` becomes func 0 (matching the native seed), NOT the
; first def. The reader computes a VISITING ORDER as a pure INDEX MAP `visit-def(k)`
; = the original def-index at output position k: position 0 → main's def-index;
; position k>0 → the (k-1)-th def other than main, in original order. BOTH `mod-fenv`
; (name→index) and `read-defs` (the DList) walk output positions 0..n via `visit-def`,
; staying on plain integer-`k` recursion (an IList-threaded order re-triggered the
; recursive-heap-inline blowup; the index-map does not). So func 0 = main (nullary
; entry) and call indices resolve to the reordered positions — a helper-first module
; `(def (add …)…)(def (main)…)` now COMPUTES its value instead of trapping. (This was
; blocked on the compile-cost ceiling, seed gap 3m, now fixed.)

(def (def-name-is-main b root k)
  (doc "Does def k's function name (its sig head-index's prelude symbol) equal the bytes `main`?")
  (name-eq b (prelude-entry b (def-fn-name-index b (mod-elem b root (+ 2 k)))) b"main" 0 4))

(def (find-main b root k n)
  (doc "The def-index (0-based) whose function name is `main`, searching from k; or 0 if none (a module
        with no `main` frames its first def — entry-guard makes that a trapping entry if it's param'd).")
  (if (< k n)
      (if (def-name-is-main b root k) k (find-main b root (+ k 1) n))
      0))

(def (skip-main-nth b root j mainix pos target)
  (doc "Walk def-indices from j, counting those that are NOT `mainix`; return the `target`-th such index
        (0-based). Fills output positions 1.. with the non-main defs in original order.")
  (if (= j mainix)
      (skip-main-nth b root (+ j 1) mainix pos target)
  (if (= pos target)
      j
      (skip-main-nth b root (+ j 1) mainix (+ pos 1) target))))

(def (visit-def b root n k)
  (doc "The original def-index at OUTPUT position k in the visiting order: position 0 → main's def-index;
        k>0 → the (k-1)-th def other than main, original order. So func 0 is the `main` entry. Plain
        integer recursion (no threaded IList → no heap-inline blowup).")
  (if (< k 1)
      (find-main b root 0 n)
      (skip-main-nth b root 0 (find-main b root 0 n) 0 (- k 1))))

(def (mod-fenv b root k n)
  (doc "The module's FUNCTION ENVIRONMENT: a built-in `list<Int64>` of each def's function-name prelude
        index, walked in the VISITING ORDER (output position k → def `visit-def k`). A call head's
        prelude index, looked up here by `ienv-pos`, yields the callee's REORDERED function index — the
        position compile-program frames. Position = function index, so build low→high with List.push.")
  (mod-fenv-go b root k n (list)))

(def (mod-fenv-go b root k n acc)
  (doc "Append function-name prelude indices for visiting positions [k,n) to `acc`, low→high, so
        position i = the i-th visited def's function index.")
  (if (< k n)
      (mod-fenv-go b root (+ k 1) n
        (List.push acc (def-fn-name-index b (mod-elem b root (+ 2 (visit-def b root n k))))))
      acc))

(def (read-defs b root k n fenv)
  (doc "Read the module's defs into a `list<Def>` in the VISITING ORDER: output position k holds def
        `visit-def k` (position 0 = main). Each def: its param count and its body Node — read under the
        def's PARAMETER ENVIRONMENT (body name-ref → local slot) and the module's FUNCTION ENVIRONMENT
        `fenv` (call head → reordered function index). Def name is a \"\" placeholder. Appends in order
        (List.push), so position = def index.")
  (read-defs-go b root k n fenv (list)))

(def (read-defs-go b root k n fenv acc)
  (doc "Append defs for visiting positions [k,n) to `acc`, low→high, so position i = def i.")
  (if (< k n)
      (let ((defoff (mod-elem b root (+ 2 (visit-def b root n k)))))
        (read-defs-go b root (+ k 1) n fenv
          (List.push acc (Def.D (tuple ""
                                       (def-nparams-of b defoff)
                                       (read-node b (def-body-off b defoff)
                                                  (def-param-env b defoff) fenv (list)))))))
      acc))

(def (read-module b)
  (doc "Decode a whole module's canonical AST bytes into a surface DList of Defs — the complete reader.
        Walks the VISITING ORDER (main's def at output position 0, the rest in original order) so func 0
        is the nullary `run` entry, and builds the module's function environment over that same order
        (so a call head resolves to its reordered function index — forward refs + recursion included).
        Feed to `resolve-module` → `compile-program` for `module bytes → component`.")
  (let ((root (mod-root b)))
    (read-defs b root 0 (mod-ndefs b root)
               (mod-fenv b root 0 (mod-ndefs b root)))))

; ─── heap-list detection (ask-60 tier-2 stage 1): a `List.push`-chain of const ints ──
; `(List.push (list) 5)` encodes as `[apply, (. List push), <acc>, <elem>]` — a major-4 array of arity 3
; whose head is "apply", child 1 is the dotted `(. List push)` method, child 2 is the accumulator (a
; nested `List.push` or the empty `(list)`), child 3 is the pushed element. We recognize this chain when
; the base is `(list)` and every pushed element is a CONST integer literal (major 0/1), collecting the
; elements innermost-first (= call/push order) — the order `heap-list-component` emits.
(def (node-is-empty-list? b i)
  (doc "Is the node at i the empty list literal `(list)` — a major-4 array of arity 0 with head `list`?")
  (and (and (= (cbor-major b i) 4) (node-head-is b i b"list" 4)) (= (app-arity b i) 0)))

(def (node-is-list-push? b i)
  (doc "Is the node at i a `(List.push acc elem)` — a major-4 arity-3 application whose head is `apply` and
        whose child 1 is the dotted method `(. List push)` (a `.`-form array of arity 2 with receiver name
        `List` and member `push`)?")
  (if (and (and (= (cbor-major b i) 4) (node-head-is b i b"apply" 5)) (= (app-arity b i) 3))
      (let ((m (read-child-off b i 1)))
        (if (and (and (= (cbor-major b m) 4) (head-is b (read-head-index b m) b"." 1)) (= (app-arity b m) 2))
            (and (name-eq b (prelude-entry b (name-ref-index b (read-child-off b m 1))) b"List" 0 4)
                 (name-eq b (prelude-entry b (name-ref-index b (read-child-off b m 2))) b"push" 0 4))
            false))
      false))

(def (node-is-const-int? b i)
  (doc "Is the node at i a CONST integer literal — CBOR major 0 (uint) or 1 (negint) — safe to emit as a
        boxed heap element? (A name/call/compound element is NOT — those need real element codegen, a later tier.)")
  (or (= (cbor-major b i) 0) (= (cbor-major b i) 1)))

(def (heap-list-chain-ok? b i)
  (doc "Is the node at i a well-formed `List.push`-chain of const ints over `(list)`? Walk the accumulator
        spine: `(list)` base → ok; `(List.push acc elem)` → elem must be a const int AND acc must itself be
        a valid chain. Anything else → false.")
  (if (node-is-empty-list? b i)
      true
  (if (node-is-list-push? b i)
      (if (node-is-const-int? b (read-child-off b i 3))
          (heap-list-chain-ok? b (read-child-off b i 2))
          false)
      false)))

(def (heap-list-elems b i acc)
  (doc "Collect the const-int elements of a `List.push`-chain at i into `acc`, innermost-first (push order):
        recurse into the accumulator (child 2) FIRST, then append this level's element (child 3). So the base
        `(list)` contributes nothing and the outermost push's element lands last — matching native's build order.")
  (if (node-is-list-push? b i)
      (List.push (heap-list-elems b (read-child-off b i 2) acc)
                 (cbor-int-value b (read-child-off b i 3)))
      acc))

(def (cbor-int-value b i)
  (doc "The signed integer value of a CBOR int literal at i: major 0 → the arg; major 1 (negint) → -1-arg.")
  (if (= (cbor-major b i) 0)
      (cbor-arg b i)
      (- (- 0 1) (cbor-arg b i))))

(def (main-heap-list-off b)
  (doc "If the module's `main` is a NULLARY def whose body is a `List.push`-chain of const ints over `(list)`
        (the runtime int-list heap case, ask-60 tier-2 stage 1), the byte-offset of that body; else -1. The
        body MUST be an actual push-chain (at least one push) — a bare `(list)` const literal stays on the
        const-display tier, not this heap path.")
  (let ((root (mod-root b)))
    (let ((mi (find-main b root 0 (mod-ndefs b root))))
      (let ((defoff (mod-elem b root (+ 2 mi))))
        (if (< (def-nparams-of b defoff) 1)
            (let ((bodyoff (def-body-off b defoff)))
              (if (and (node-is-list-push? b bodyoff) (heap-list-chain-ok? b bodyoff)) bodyoff (- 0 1)))
            (- 0 1))))))

(def (main-is-unit? b)
  (doc "Is the module's `main` a NULLARY def whose body is the UNIT value (`unit` or `()`)? → the fixed 86-byte
        `run : () -> ()` unit component. A param'd main or any other body → false.")
  (let ((root (mod-root b)))
    (let ((mi (find-main b root 0 (mod-ndefs b root))))
      (let ((defoff (mod-elem b root (+ 2 mi))))
        (if (< (def-nparams-of b defoff) 1)
            (node-is-unit-value? b (def-body-off b defoff))
            false)))))

(def (main-render-off b)
  (doc "If the module's `main` is a NULLARY def whose body is a FULLY-LITERAL const compound (`render-ok?`),
        the byte-offset of that body — for the runtime-compound VALUE emission path (ask-60). Else -1 (the
        ordinary scalar/decline path). Locates main's def via `find-main`, checks nparams=0 and render-ok.
        A compound body with a foldable/runtime element (`(tuple (+ 1 1) 2)`) is NOT render-ok → -1 → declines
        (native folds it; this compiler's const tier under-declines it — safe, no miscompile).")
  (let ((root (mod-root b)))
    (let ((mi (find-main b root 0 (mod-ndefs b root))))
      (let ((defoff (mod-elem b root (+ 2 mi))))
        (if (< (def-nparams-of b defoff) 1)
            (let ((bodyoff (def-body-off b defoff)))
              ; ONLY dispatch for an actual COMPOUND body (tuple/list/record) that is fully render-ok — NOT a
              ; bare scalar (a scalar `42`/`true` must keep the scalar `run` envelope; render-ok? alone is true
              ; for a scalar leaf, so guard on the compound head too).
              (if (and (body-is-compound-head b bodyoff) (render-ok? b bodyoff)) bodyoff (- 0 1)))
            (- 0 1))))))

(def (body-is-compound-head b i)
  (doc "Is the node at i a HEAP value that takes the resource-with-display path — `(tuple …)`/`(list …)`/
        `(record …)`, a CONSTRUCTOR value `(Some …)`/`(None unit)` (capitalized head, const sum-ctor tier),
        a STRING literal, or a const BYTES producer (`(Bytes.of …)`/concat/slice → `b\"…\"`)? The top-level
        dispatch guard: these take the compound path; a scalar leaf (int/bool) stays on the scalar `run`
        envelope. A ctor renders like a tuple; a string renders as `\"…\"`; a bytes value as `b\"…\"`.
        A `(let ((v V)) v)` passthrough takes the path iff V does (recurse on V).")
  (if (>= (let-passthru-off b i) 0)
      (body-is-compound-head b (let-passthru-off b i))
  (if (node-is-str-producer? b i)
      true
  (if (node-is-bytes-producer? b i)
      true
  (if (bytes-at? b i)
      true
  (if (bytes-slice? b i)
      true
  (if (string-at? b i)
      true
  (if (string-slice? b i)
      true
  (if (dotted-ctor-unit? b i)
      true
  (if (= (cbor-major b i) 4)
      (if (node-head-is b i b"tuple" 5) true
      (if (node-head-is b i b"list" 4) true
      (if (node-head-is b i b"record" 6) true
      (head-is-ctor? b i))))
      false))))))))))

(def (compile-bytes b)
  (doc "The whole self-hosting-shaped entry: canonical module AST bytes → component. Dispatch, in order:
        (1) a nullary `main` that is a `List.push`-chain of const ints → the HEAP-IMPORTING int-list component
        (ask-60 tier-2 stage 1: `heap-list-component` over the collected elements); (2) a nullary `main` that is
        a fully-literal const compound (render-ok) → the resource-with-display component (const tier); (3) the
        ordinary path: read-module → resolve-module → fold → lower → serialize → frame.")
  (if (main-is-unit? b)
      (unit-component)                                  ; nullary main = unit value → fixed 86-byte run:()->()
  (let ((hlo (main-heap-list-off b)))
    (if (< hlo 0)
        (let ((mro (main-render-off b)))
          (if (< mro 0)
              (compile-program (resolve-module (read-module b)))
              (compound-component (render-ast b mro))))
        (heap-list-component (heap-list-elems b hlo (list)))))))

(def (flen xs) (List.len xs))

(def (params-bytes np)
  (doc "The parameter valtype bytes for a function of `np` i64 params: np copies of 0x7E.")
  (if (< np 1) (empty-bytes) (cat (u8 0x7E) (params-bytes (- np 1)))))

(def (functype-of f ktab)
  (doc "A wasm functype for `f`: 0x60, <np> i64 params, 1 result of the body's Kind (computed under
        the function return-kind table `ktab`, so a body that returns a Bool-returning call is typed
        i32 to match the value it pushes).")
  (cat (u8 0x60)
  (cat (cat (uleb (func-nparams f)) (params-bytes (func-nparams f)))
       (cat (u8 0x01) (u8 (core-valtype (kind-of (func-body f) ktab)))))))

(def (type-items xs ktab)
  (doc "The concatenated functypes of every function, in order (func i : type i). Index-walk over the
        built-in `list<Func>`.")
  (type-items-go xs 0 (List.len xs) ktab))
(def (type-items-go xs i n ktab)
  (if (< i n)
      (cat (match (List.at xs i) ((None _) (empty-bytes)) ((Some h) (functype-of h ktab)))
           (type-items-go xs (+ i 1) n ktab))
      (empty-bytes)))

(def (func-items xs i)
  (doc "The function section's type-index vector items: func i uses type i, so this is i, i+1, … over
        the built-in list (one uleb per function; `i` is both the list index and the type index).")
  (func-items-go xs i (List.len xs)))
(def (func-items-go xs i n)
  (if (< i n) (cat (uleb i) (func-items-go xs (+ i 1) n)) (empty-bytes)))

(def (code-items xs ktab)
  (doc "The code section's per-function body entries, in order. `ktab` threads the return-kind table
        so a body's `if`-over-a-call gets the correct blocktype. Index-walk over the built-in list.")
  (code-items-go xs 0 (List.len xs) ktab))
(def (code-items-go xs i n ktab)
  (if (< i n)
      (cat (match (List.at xs i) ((None _) (empty-bytes)) ((Some h) (encode-body-of h ktab)))
           (code-items-go xs (+ i 1) n ktab))
      (empty-bytes)))

(def (encode-body-of f ktab)
  (doc "A code-section entry for `f`: declares `count-lets + scratch-count` extra i64 locals — the
        let-locals at [nparams..nparams+count-lets), then 3 scratch locals for the overflow-checked
        `+ - *`/shifts (if any) at [nparams+count-lets..). `sb` (the scratch base handed to `lower`) is
        `nparams + count-lets`. (The body was already TYPE-CHECKED before folding — an ill-typed body
        is KError here, with 0 lets / 0 scratch, lowering to `unreachable`; see `typecheck-funcs`.)")
  (let ((nl (+ (count-lets (func-body f)) (scratch-count (func-body f)))))
    (encode-body-locals
      (serialize (lower (func-body f) ktab (+ (func-nparams f) (count-lets (func-body f)))))
      nl)))

(def (core-module-multi funcs)
  (doc "The embedded core module for a program of `funcs` (main = func 0, the nullary `run` entry).
        Type / function / code sections are N-entry vectors folded over the function list; the export
        names func 0 as `run`. The function return-kind table is built ONCE (single pass over the
        function list) and threaded to the type section (result valtypes) and code section (blocktypes)
        so a Bool-returning function and its callers are all typed i32.")
  (let ((ktab (build-ktab funcs)))
  (cat (Bytes.of (list 0x00 0x61 0x73 0x6D 0x01 0x00 0x00 0x00))       ; \0asm v1
  (cat (section 1 (cat (uleb (flen funcs)) (type-items funcs ktab)))   ; type section: N functypes
  (cat (section 3 (cat (uleb (flen funcs)) (func-items funcs 0)))      ; function section: func i : type i
  (cat (section 7 (wvec 1 (cat (u8 0x03) (cat (Bytes.of (list 0x72 0x75 0x6E)) (Bytes.of (list 0x00 0x00))))))  ; export "run" = func 0
       (section 10 (cat (uleb (flen funcs)) (code-items funcs ktab)))))))))  ; code section: N bodies

; The single-expression path: one nullary `main`, as a 1-element function list.
(def (core-module code-bytes kind)
  (doc "The embedded core module for a nullary `run` returning `kind`, given main's already-serialized
        body bytes, with NO extra locals. Delegates to `core-module-locals` with 0.")
  (core-module-locals code-bytes 0 kind))

(def (core-module-locals code-bytes nlocals kind)
  (doc "The embedded core module for a nullary `run` returning `kind`, given main's already-serialized
        body bytes and `nlocals` extra i64 locals (let + scratch). Retained for the single-expression
        driver; inlines the 1-function shape — byte-identical to a 1-element `core-module-multi`.")
  (cat (Bytes.of (list 0x00 0x61 0x73 0x6D 0x01 0x00 0x00 0x00))       ; \0asm v1
  (cat (section 1 (wvec 1 (cat (Bytes.of (list 0x60 0x00 0x01)) (u8 (core-valtype kind)))))  ; type: () -> <kind>
  (cat (section 3 (wvec 1 (u8 0x00)))                                  ; func 0 : type 0
  (cat (section 7 (wvec 1 (cat (u8 0x03) (cat (Bytes.of (list 0x72 0x75 0x6E)) (Bytes.of (list 0x00 0x00))))))  ; export "run"
       (section 10 (wvec 1 (encode-body-locals code-bytes nlocals))))))))  ; code

(def (wrap-component core kind)
  (doc "Wrap the core module in the component envelope, presenting core func 0 (run) as the
        component export run : () -> <kind>. Fixed surround except the boundary result valtype.")
  (cat (Bytes.of (list 0x00 0x61 0x73 0x6D 0x0D 0x00 0x01 0x00))       ; component magic
  (cat (cat (u8 0x01) (sized core))                                    ; section 1: embedded core module
  (cat (Bytes.of (list 0x02 0x04 0x01 0x00 0x00 0x00))                 ; section 2: core instance
  (cat (cat (Bytes.of (list 0x07 0x05 0x01 0x40 0x00 0x00)) (u8 (comp-valtype kind)))  ; section 7: component type () -> <kind>
  (cat (Bytes.of (list 0x06 0x09 0x01 0x00 0x00 0x01 0x00 0x03 0x72 0x75 0x6E))  ; section 6: canon lift
  (cat (Bytes.of (list 0x08 0x06 0x01 0x00 0x00 0x00 0x00 0x00))       ; section 8: component func alias
       (Bytes.of (list 0x0B 0x09 0x01 0x00 0x03 0x72 0x75 0x6E 0x01 0x00 0x00)))))))))  ; section 11: export "run"

; ─── The driver: Core → component bytes ─────────────────────────────────────

(def (compile-node node)
  (doc "The whole pipeline, front to back: resolve Node → Core, fold Core → Core, then emit the
        folded Core — reading its result KIND (kind-of) to frame `run` at the right wasm valtype,
        and lowering+serializing its body. Type-directed emission: the framing is a function of the
        program's inferred result kind, so an i64 program crosses as s64 and a Bool program as bool.
        Splitting `compile-core` out lets the kind be read once from the folded tree and threaded to
        both framing sites.")
  (compile-core (fold (typecheck (resolve node) (list)))))

(def (compile-core core)
  (doc "Emit a resolved+folded Core to a component: infer its result kind, then frame the serialized
        body at that kind. The kind is read from the (already folded) Core so a comparison that
        folded to a Bool constant still frames as Bool. The single-expression path has no module
        functions, so an EMPTY return-kind table `(list)` suffices (any KCall defaults Ki64).")
  (compile-core-at core (kind-of core (list))))

(def (compile-core-at core kind)
  (doc "Emit `core` framing `run` at result `kind` — the kind named once, threaded to core-module
        (core valtype) and wrap-component (component valtype). Legacy single-expression driver (the
        reader path uses `compile-program`/`core-module-multi`). Declares `count-lets + scratch-count`
        locals; scratch base = count-lets (the 3 scratch slots sit past the let-locals). (The core was
        already TYPE-CHECKED before folding in `compile-node`.)")
  (wrap-component (core-module-locals (serialize (lower core (list) (count-lets core)))
                                      (+ (count-lets core) (scratch-count core)) kind) kind))

; The multi-function driver: a whole program is a list of functions (main = func 0).
; The component boundary presents func 0's result kind. Each function's body is
; folded before it is assembled (fold is per-body — a Core→Core rewrite that does
; not cross the function boundary), so a constant-valued helper still folds.
(def (fold-funcs xs)
  (doc "Fold every function's body (a per-function Core→Core pass). Index-walk over the built-in
        `list<Func>`, appending each folded Func in order.")
  (fold-funcs-go xs 0 (List.len xs) (list)))
(def (fold-funcs-go xs i n acc)
  (if (< i n)
      (fold-funcs-go xs (+ i 1) n
        (match (List.at xs i)
          ((None _) acc)
          ((Some h) (List.push acc (Func.Fn (tuple (func-nparams h) (fold (func-body h))))))))
      acc))

(def (typecheck-funcs xs ktab)
  (doc "Type-check every function body BEFORE folding (ask-30): an ill-typed body becomes KError (→
        trap), so an ill-typed program is REJECTED, not lowered — and rejected BEFORE `fold` could
        collapse the mismatch into a constant (`(+ 1 true)` must reject, not fold to 2). `ktab` is the
        return-kind table from the RESOLVED funcs, so a call's result kind is known during the check.
        Index-walk over the built-in `list<Func>`, appending each checked Func in order.")
  (typecheck-funcs-go xs 0 (List.len xs) ktab (list)))
(def (typecheck-funcs-go xs i n ktab acc)
  (if (< i n)
      (typecheck-funcs-go xs (+ i 1) n ktab
        (match (List.at xs i)
          ((None _) acc)
          ((Some h) (List.push acc (Func.Fn (tuple (func-nparams h) (typecheck (func-body h) ktab)))))))
      acc))

(def (compile-program funcs)
  (doc "Compile a whole program (a FList; main = func 0) to a component: TYPE-CHECK each body (before
        fold, so an ill-typed program rejects rather than folding its mismatch away), fold each body,
        assemble the multi-function core module, and frame `run` at func 0's result kind. The single-
        expression `compile-node` is the degenerate one-function case of this.")
  (compile-program-folded (fold-funcs (typecheck-funcs funcs (build-ktab funcs)))))

(def (compile-program-folded funcs)
  (doc "Assemble a folded function list into a component, framing at func 0's (main's) result kind.
        Func 0 is the `run` ENTRY, which the component boundary lifts as nullary `() -> output`, so a
        func-0 with parameters cannot be the entry — `entry-guard` rejects that (a KError entry that
        lowers to `unreachable`) rather than emitting an invalid component (nullary lift vs param'd
        core func). Decline-don't-miscompile at the module boundary.")
  (compile-program-guarded (entry-guard funcs)))

(def (compile-program-guarded funcs)
  (doc "Assemble a guarded function list (func 0 known nullary) into a component, framing at func 0's
        result kind — computed under the function return-kind table so a `main` that returns a
        Bool-returning call frames `run` as Bool (i32), matching the value it pushes.")
  (wrap-component (core-module-multi funcs)
                  (kind-of (func-body (flist-head funcs)) (build-ktab funcs))))

; ─── The function return-kind table (monotone fixpoint) ─────────────────────
; A call's result kind is the callee's return kind, so the framing needs, per
; function, its return kind. But a function's return kind can itself depend on the
; functions it CALLS (`(def (f x) (g x))` returns what `g` returns) — including a
; depth-2 chain and mutual recursion. So the table is a MONOTONE FIXPOINT: start
; every function at Ki64, then repeatedly recompute each function's kind (via
; `kind-of` under the CURRENT table) until the table stops changing. A kind only
; ever moves Ki64→KBool (never back — `kind-of` reads a comparison/logical as KBool
; regardless of the table, and a call inherits an already-KBool callee), so the
; lattice height is the function count N; iterating N times converges. (The recursive
; `iterate` re-passing a freshly-recomputed table each round used to OOM the seed —
; gap 3k, fixpoint form — now FIXED, so the true fixpoint is expressible, retiring the
; single-pass depth-2 limitation.)
(def (build-ktab funcs)
  (doc "The converged function return-kind table for `funcs`: a `list Kind`, entry i = function i's
        result kind, accounting for calls to Bool-returning callees TRANSITIVELY. Monotone fixpoint,
        iterated `flen funcs` times from the all-Ki64 seed. `ktab-at`/`kind-of`'s KCall arm read it.")
  (ktab-iterate funcs (ktab-seed funcs) (flen funcs)))

(def (ktab-seed funcs)
  (doc "The initial table: every function Ki64, one entry per function in def order. Tail-recursive
        accumulator (List.push APPENDS, so building head-first into `out` preserves def order — a
        head-first `(List.push (recurse tail) h)` would REVERSE it).")
  (ktab-seed-go funcs 0 (List.len funcs) (list)))
(def (ktab-seed-go funcs i n out)
  (if (< i n) (ktab-seed-go funcs (+ i 1) n (List.push out (Kind.Ki64 ()))) out))

(def (ktab-recompute funcs ktab)
  (doc "One fixpoint pass: recompute each function's return kind (`kind-of` of its body under the
        CURRENT table `ktab`, so a KCall resolves to the previous iteration's callee kind), producing
        the next table IN DEF ORDER (tail-recursive accumulator — see ktab-seed).")
  (ktab-recompute-go funcs 0 (List.len funcs) ktab (list)))
(def (ktab-recompute-go funcs i n ktab out)
  (if (< i n)
      (ktab-recompute-go funcs (+ i 1) n ktab
        (match (List.at funcs i) ((None _) out) ((Some h) (List.push out (kind-of (func-body h) ktab)))))
      out))

(def (ktab-iterate funcs ktab passes)
  (doc "Iterate the fixpoint `passes` times: each pass recomputes the table from itself. `passes` =
        function count, enough for a monotone Ki64→KBool lattice of that height to settle.")
  (if (< passes 1)
      ktab
      (ktab-iterate funcs (ktab-recompute funcs ktab) (- passes 1))))

(def (entry-guard funcs)
  (doc "Ensure func 0 (the `run` entry) is a valid NULLARY entry. If func 0 takes parameters, the
        module is malformed for a component entry (the boundary lifts func 0 as the nullary `run`), so
        COLLAPSE the whole program to a SINGLE nullary KError function (→ `unreachable`): a valid
        trapping `run`, a clean DECLINE. We must drop the OTHER functions too — a helper-first module
        `(def (add a b)…)(def (main)…)` has `main` (now some later func) CALLING func 0, and if func 0
        is stubbed to nullary while its param'd callers remain, those calls are an arity mismatch → an
        INVALID component. A lone nullary trap is valid. (The real fix is entry reorder — make the
        `main`-named def func 0 — but that OOMs the seed at this compiler's scale: gap 3m. Until then,
        a module whose func 0 is not a valid nullary entry is a clean decline, not invalid bytes.)")
  (match (List.at funcs 0)
    ((None _) (list))                                     ; empty program — no funcs
    ((Some h)
      (if (< (func-nparams h) 1)
          funcs                                           ; func 0 nullary — fine, keep the whole list
          (List.push (list) (Func.Fn (tuple 0 (Core.KError 0))))))))  ; param'd entry → lone nullary DECLINE trap

(def (flist-head xs)
  (doc "The first function of a non-empty function list (main = func 0).")
  (match (List.at xs 0) ((None _) (Func.Fn (tuple 0 (Core.KConst 0)))) ((Some h) h)))

; ─── compile-module: the whole compiler, surface DList → component bytes ─────
; The complete front-to-back path for a multi-def program: resolve the surface
; module (DList of Defs) to a typed FList, then run the multi-function backend.
; This is `compile : surface → bytes` — the top-level entry the reader feeds once
; it decodes the canonical AST into a DList.
(def (compile-module defs)
  (doc "Compile a surface module (a DList of function defs; main = first def) to a complete component:
        resolve-module (DList → FList, resolving each body Node → Core), then compile-program (fold →
        assemble → frame). The full pipeline: read → resolve → fold → lower → serialize → frame.")
  (compile-program (resolve-module defs)))

; ─── Entry: the `compile` seam ───────────────────────────────────────────────
; THE SELF-HOSTING ENTRYPOINT, on the kinded-artifact ABI (ask-41): `compile : list<artifact> → compile-output`.
(def (compile inputs)
  (doc "THE self-hosting seam, kinded-artifact ABI (ask-41) with EFFECT-BASED DIAGNOSTICS (operator direction):
        `compile : list<artifact> → compile-output{artifacts, diagnostics}`. Project the input AST bytes (match-arm
        binder form, ask-52), then `compile-artifact` runs the pipeline under a `Diag` handler; `check-funcs`
        performs `(Diag.emit 201)` at each REJECT (KError-split, ask-53: only genuine type/malformed errors emit;
        unsupported-construct DECLINEs are silent). Returns the component artifact + collected CDZ0201 diagnostics.
        Both outcomes same record type. Closes ask-30 → agree.")
  (match (List.at inputs 0)
    ((Some a) (compile-artifact (. a bytes)))
    ((None _) (record (artifacts (list)) (diagnostics (list (mk-diagnostic 1)))))))

(def (compile-artifact b)
  (doc "Compile AST bytes `b` under the `Diag` handler and produce the `compile-output` record. `check-funcs`
        performs `(Diag.emit 201)` per rejection into the handler's `list` state; `Diag.collect` reads the
        codes; `codes->diagnostics` turns them into `diagnostic` records; `compile-program` emits the bytes.
        Success and rejection are the SAME record shape — the diagnostics list is empty iff well-typed.")
  (let ((funcs (resolve-module (read-module b))))
    (handle (list)
       ((Diag.emit (v) s (resume unit (List.push s v)))
        (Diag.collect (u) s (resume s s)))
       (do (check-funcs funcs (build-ktab funcs))
           (let ((codes (Diag.collect unit)))
             (record (artifacts (list (record (bytes (compile-program funcs)) (kind "component"))))
                     (diagnostics (codes->diagnostics codes 0 (list)))))))))

; NOTE: the old Result-`compile` path (ask-42) is retired — closed-by-construction via ask-41's kinded-artifact
; record return (both success/rejection are one `compile-output` type, so the deep sum-match that once mis-shaped
; under `result<Ok,Err>` lowers as an ordinary heap consumer). `compile` above IS the artifact-ABI body.
)
